HT_DATA.load("detail", {"Ik1AK56FtVc":{"wy":"A perennial HN recommendation whenever lobbying, SOPA, taxes or political corruption come up, with several commenters saying it changed their views.","q":{"x":"That video has changed my point of view on the entire subject and has allowed me to start striking at the root.","i":3766701,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4739046,"author":"spoiledtechie","month":"2012-11","story":"512 Paths to the White House","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Worth absorbing deeply","quote":"I really think you should take the time to watch this video, understand it, let it sink in and totally mold your brain around it.","text":"Actually, They are right. I really think you should take the time to watch this video, understand it, let it sink in and totally mold your brain around it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3711799,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-03","story":"Lawrence Lessig's New Book: One Way Forward","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how government relates to corporations instead of people, and what could fix it","quote":"I saw the 1 hour speech at Google from last year, that was based on it, and it was great.","text":"I haven't read his previous book, \"Republic, Lost\", but I saw the 1 hour speech at Google from last year, that was based on it, and it was great. He explains how the Government ended up having a relationship with corporations instead of \"the people\", and what is needed to solve this (almost unsolvable) problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3766701,"author":"spoiledtechie","month":"2012-03","story":"The new Comcast Xbox Xfinity app is the first nail in net neutrality’s coffin","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's view on the subject and motivated action","quote":"That video has changed my point of view on the entire subject and has allowed me to start striking at the root.","text":"If your really interested in doing something about it, I would suggest taking a look at [LINK] And without a second lost, watch this: [VIDEO] That video has changed my point of view on the entire subject and has allowed me to start striking at the root."},{"id":3490927,"author":"Dn_Ab","month":"2012-01","story":"The next SOPA","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-argued talk with supporting data on political donations.","quote":"It's well worth watching and counters your statement.","text":"> This idea that the root cause of all bad legislation is political donations: it's wrong, and not supported by the data. The vast majority of political donations are made by people who are not lobbyists. You make a very bold statement without backing it with any line of reasoning or pointing to any supporting material. I am no expert in this matter so I can not know if you are wrong. But in an older thread someone posted this excellent talk by Lawrence Lessig: [VIDEO] . It's well worth watching and counters your statement. His arguments are very well presented and reasoned. He also shows some supporting data. Given that, I cannot attribute much weight to your statement."},{"id":14889239,"author":"sn9","month":"2017-07","story":"Al Gore: 'The rich have subverted all reason'","stance":"rec","why":"Condensed version of Lessig's Republic, Lost argument","quote":"His 2011 Google Tech Talk provides a condensed version","text":"Lawrence Lessig's Republic, Lost directly addresses this. His 2011 Google Tech Talk provides a condensed version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10720146,"author":"steego","month":"2015-12","story":"Sen. Ted Cruz wants minimum H-1B wage of $110,000","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig explains the issue better than commenter can","quote":"Larry Lessig will expand on this better than me.","text":"Sorry to punt and give you homework, but Larry Lessig will expand on this better than me. Here's a link if you want to listen to something while you work. His book, Republic Lost, is a good read on this subject if you're interested. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7109505,"author":"masmullin","month":"2014-01","story":"Snowden Calls Russian-Spy Story “Absurd”","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested starting point for what citizens can do about political corruption","quote":"For a starter, watch Lawerence Lessig @google talk","text":"\"What are they supposed to do?\" For a starter, watch Lawerence Lessig @google talk [VIDEO] Get off your butts and vote independent. For those who claim that you're throwing your vote away reply with \"No, you're throwing YOUR vote away.\""},{"id":5391432,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2013-03","story":"Can Hospital Chains Improve the Medical Industry?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows legislation driven by re-election funding rather than public good","quote":"Lessig covers that topic really well in Republic Lost","text":"Whose dollars? The dollars used in legislation have little to do with public good and a lot more to do with re-election funding. Lessig covers that topic really well in Republic Lost: [VIDEO] Any change in legislation will result in intense lobbying (ie politician funding) because so many vested interests benefit from the current structure. As with many things in American politics, the issue isn't so much about what the objectively best thing to do is, but rather how that could happen with the current political process. There is some light at the end of the tunnel in pushing things to individual states. There is no need to come up with perfection - let 50 states do their own thing and see what works best."},{"id":5149493,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2013-02","story":"Mark Cuban Is Endowing A Chair To ‘Eliminate Stupid Patents’","stance":"rec","why":"Shows legislation used as fund raising, with corruption at 25m20s","quote":"See this excellent talk by Lessig","text":"It would be far better if congress is representative of the people of the country and be composed of doctors, engineers, teachers, taxi drivers, farmers, candlestick makers etc. The legislators do not actually write the laws. They have staff as well as there being a process to help improve the text before legislating (eg publishing drafts). For some legislation (eg covering the financial industry) often the law that is passed just requires a government agency (eg SEC) to come up with the rules. One of Michael Moore's films had a clip of a congressman explaining that they don't even read the legislation they vote on. It is a common claim that much of the legislation is written by lobbyists. And if you actually read any of it, it would obviously not really be what you would expect (short, legible). Here is an example of trying to solve the problem: [LINK] The representatives use legislation as a fund raising mechanism (contributions are well documented). For example there were several taxes added temporarily to see if they worked well. They did, but congress doesn't make them permanent. Instead they wait to be paid each time to renew them temporarily again. (This started in the Reagan administration.) See this excellent talk by Lessig: [VIDEO] The whole tax provisions as fund raising is covered at 7m30s. And the corruption is obvious at 25m20s."},{"id":4812847,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2012-11","story":"Can the US tax code be simplified with computer modeling and simulation","stance":"rec","why":"Covers tax provisions as fund raising, at 7m30s","quote":"See this excellent talk by Lessig","text":"A big reason for why things are so screwed up is corrupt politicians who use their power as a fund raising mechanism. For example there were several taxes added temporarily to see if they worked well. They did, but congress doesn't make them permanent. Instead they wait to be paid each time to renew them temporarily again. (This started in the Reagan administration.) See this excellent talk by Lessig: [VIDEO] The whole tax provisions as fund raising is covered at 7m30s. I congress were actually honourable and I was president I'd mandate that the tax code shrinks by 10% each year. If the politicians can't find 10% then at the end of year every 10th provision is automatically deleted."},{"id":4465823,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2012-09","story":"Proposal for tax revolution: 3% income tax, 4% wealth tax","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how US political system is distorted by money","quote":"Watch this talk about the US","text":"We already have progressive tax rates which try to capture this. The problem that is being struggled with is that HNWI don't have frequent taxable events, generally because they hold assets and make savings. However there is a perception they aren't providing value (which is mostly wrong). Generally any scheme you come up with to get at them will also affect everyone else, and to a far greater degree since they have less money and diversity to start with. And the HNWI have far greater incentive to move their money and themselves, as well as pay people to work out how to retain their money. You can move to almost any country in the world by plonking down a lump sum as an \"investment\" or as savings. (eg see how Dotcom ended up living in New Zealand.) The usual solution to this is to try tweaking the tax code, seeing what the effects are and repeating. However scumbag politicians and the people who vote for them distort that process, so it doesn't have the intended effect. An actually representative democracy would be a good first step rather than tax rate changes. Watch this talk about the US - the UK pretty much follows behind a few years later [VIDEO]"},{"id":3854213,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2012-04","story":"Shouldn't Robots Be Doing My Taxes By Now?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers tax provisions used as fund raising by Congress","quote":"See this excellent talk by Lessig","text":"If I was ever elected president, I would direct congress to reduce the size of the tax code by 10% each year. If they fail to do so then 10% are automatically eliminated - eg all those whose last digit of the article number is 3. The real reason for why things are so screwed up is corrupt politicians who use their power as a fund raising mechanism. For example there were several taxes added temporarily to see if they worked well. They did, but congress doesn't make them permanent. Instead they wait to be paid each time to renew them temporarily again. (This started in the Reagan administration.) See this excellent talk by Lessig: [VIDEO] The whole tax provisions as fund raising is covered at 7m30s."},{"id":3484169,"author":"necolas","month":"2012-01","story":"Maddox - I hope SOPA passes","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig talk on how money corrupts Congress.","quote":"You might find Lawrence Lessig's talk at Google interesting","text":"You might find Lawrence Lessig's talk at Google interesting: How Money Corrupts Congress—and a Plan to Stop It [VIDEO] However, the article suggests that if some terrible legislation passed it would ignite people into real action. That clearly failed to happen when the Patriot Act was passed. I'm sure there are many more examples. If the discourse surrounding legislation convinces enough people not to care (or fails to make it into wider public awareness), then there doesn't seem to be much standing in its way."},{"id":3505264,"author":"scott_s","month":"2012-01","story":"Show HN: I'm tired of corrupt US politicians, so I created this","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig's framing of legal corruption of the system.","quote":"it's worth watching.","text":"I'm going to crib from my own post from two days ago: Donations don't literally buy votes, but what they do literally buy is face time. That is, people in Congress will hold fundraisers, and lobbyists pay to attend those fundraisers. The understanding is that the lobbyists who attend those fundraisers will have time to talk to the politician about the issues their clients care about. This episode of Planet Money clearly demonstrates this: [LINK] Edit: I remove the SOPA specific stuff, and didn't realize that in doing so, I removed the punchline. The implication of the above is that politicians' agenda becomes set by those who have the money to pay lobbyists. When you spend a lot of time talking to a bunch of people with an agenda, you will start to think about that agenda a lot. It's a natural consequence of the circumstances and the incentives. I've harped on this point several times in the last few days. My reason is simple: we must understand the real problem before we can fix it. And the real problem is not \"Vote for x and I'll give you money.\" That is illegal. What I described above is legal, and while it is not illegal corruption, it is a corruption of how we want the system to work. And I stole that last sentiment from Lawrence Lessig: [VIDEO] Someone else here linked to that a few days ago, and it's worth watching."},{"id":3410642,"author":"webfuel","month":"2011-12","story":"An email I received from Jimmy Wales regarding SOPA","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig on how money corrupts Congress and a plan to stop it.","quote":"I think you'll enjoy Lawrence Lessig's talk about the exact issue you brought up.","text":"I think you'll enjoy Lawrence Lessig's talk about the exact issue you brought up. It's call Republic, Lost: How Money Corrupts Congress—and a Plan to Stop It [Link] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7765689,"author":"aaronwall","month":"2014-05","story":"Google is Breaking the Internet","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for engineers' views on centralized power","quote":"","text":"When monopolies distort markets, the impact of those distortions have outsized impacts on the rest of the ecosystem. Ask Google engineers how they feel about centralization of power in telecom or finance or politics & they will tell you it is the worst thing ever [VIDEO] Yet that same sort of centralization is fine when it is Google. Those same engineers will admit that any algorithm has some level of false positives & false negatives. They may try to minimize these, but they can't make them zero. The other thing which gets very little coverage, but is crucially important is that in spite of already having a monopoly market position Google keeps buying search marketshare with: their secret Android partner contracts, Firefox default search placement, iOS default search placement, Flash embedded into Chrome (so security updates happen in the background without sending you to a download site where trash can be bundled in with the update) & Flash security updates that hit all other web browsers coming bundled with Chrome bundleware which sets Chrome as your default browser, etc. If Google decides they don't like you (for any reason), then you need searchers to use a different search engine in a browser which isn't paid off by Google for default placement. And you need to hope that users are savvy enough to repeatedly say \"no thank you\" to the automatically bundled Chrome install with their frequent Flash updates. Google mentions how anything is only a click away or similar, but m"},{"id":3366589,"author":"adambyrtek","month":"2011-12","story":"SOPA vote is now December 21","stance":"reference","why":"Lessig supports the Rootstrikers campaign.","quote":"supported by Larry Lessig[2]","text":"This is exactly the point of the Rootstrikers campaign[1], supported by Larry Lessig[2]. I encourage all of you who live in the US to take action. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["wq5D43qAsVg",1],["LfascZSTU4o",1],["7Q25-S7jzgs",1],["nBiJB8YuDBQ",1],["VVp8UGjECt4",1],["uTRUQ-RKfUs",1],["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1],["HfHgUu_8KgA",1],["RfdZTZQvuCo",1],["nBNTAmLRmUg",1],["y5UT04p5f7U",1],["T-TwcmT6Rcw",1],["MYucYon2-lk",1],["akVL7QY0S8A",1],["gMqZR3pqMjg",1]]},"WVDQEoe6ZWY":{"wy":"The standard link when VPN marketing comes up, as a clear debunking of the scare tactics behind VPN ads.","q":{"x":"It's well worth the seven and a half minutes to watch it, IMO.","i":22125694,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":37584764,"author":"jjice","month":"2023-09","story":"We have successfully completed our migration to RAM-only VPN infrastructure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great video on VPN scare tactics versus HTTPS","quote":"Tom Scott is the only person I've ever seen have a great video about this [0]","text":"> Some pour thousands of dollars into forcing influencers to say they care about security, Tangential to this, it always irks me how they talk about how they all act as if the majority of the websites their users are going to aren't HTTPS and they act like their main benefits are filling in the gaps that HTTPS actually fills in. HTTPS isn't a cure all by any means but most of the scare tactics that the big VPN companies that advertise via YouTube act like anyone will rip you credit card because you happened to be on Amazon while you were at the coffee shop. Tom Scott is the only person I've ever seen have a great video about this [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26897378,"author":"katbyte","month":"2021-04","story":"SponsorBlock – Skip over sponsorship segments on YouTube","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tom Scott turned down VPN sponsorship and debunked the ads","quote":"Mad respect for him, and if he ever suggest a paid product in the future i'd trust him.","text":"I feel like if someone is hawking wares on their channel they should at least use it/have faith in it/do their research. Tom Scott has a great video about VPN sponsorship and how he turned down a sponsorship from a VPN company because of the copy they wanted him to say and debunks the ads: [VIDEO] Mad respect for him, and if he ever suggest a paid product in the future i'd trust him. Conversely i really dislike when they clearly don't use the product, or are shilling a product that i KNOW is bad - looking at you raycons. I can't help but lose respect and definitely distrust every sponsorship they have. tl;dr integrity matters, raycons are really not that great, and i'm tired of hearing the same copy over and over: [LINK]"},{"id":41243651,"author":"voidUpdate","month":"2024-08","story":"SponsorBlock – skip sponsor segments on YouTube","stance":"rec","why":"Puts the VPN security claims well","quote":"I think Tom Scott put it well here","text":"The thing about nordVPN (and VPN services in general) is they always talk about how funneling all your traffic through them makes it more secure and it means that governments cant spy on you and whatever. But sending all your traffic through a single point of failure seems like a bad idea from a government protection view, and how is it any more secure than https? The only thing that I've seen it be good for is making it look like you're from somewhere else to watch different stuff on streaming services. I think Tom Scott put it well here [VIDEO]"},{"id":40582773,"author":"fellerts","month":"2024-06","story":"Google to shut down Google One VPN on June 20","stance":"rec","why":"Explains misleading VPN ad claims well","quote":"Tom Scott said it better than I could","text":"Can you fault them when that's what NordVPN and others claim in their YouTube ads? Tom Scott said it better than I could: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32841905,"author":"rchowe","month":"2022-09","story":"Why is every podcast sponsored by a VPN company?","stance":"rec","why":"Uses and non-uses of VPNs","quote":"Tom Scott made a good video on the uses and non-uses of VPNs [1].","text":"... and a lot of those VPN companies are probably VC-funded. Tom Scott made a good video on the uses and non-uses of VPNs [1]. VPN advertising has changed since that video to downplay \"security\", but what I think is odd is that they've replaced that with advertisement that basically is \"violate the terms of service of Netflix\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27789795,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2021-07","story":"VPN – A Very Precarious Narrative","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes sentiment about misleading VPN ads on YouTube","quote":"I think that this tom scott video summarizes the same sentiment nicely","text":"I think that this tom scott video summarizes the same sentiment nicely, especially around the VPN ads on youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26651600,"author":"Deathmax","month":"2021-03","story":"Latest Mozilla VPN features","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks misleading VPN marketing claims","quote":"Tom Scott put out a good video[1] that tries to debunk various marketing claims.","text":"Unfortunately as GP has mentioned, advertising around these typical VPN companies (Nord, Proton, ExpressVPN, Surfshark and many more) tends to be very misleading. Tom Scott put out a good video[1] that tries to debunk various marketing claims. Sure there are use cases like getting around georestrictions, and like you mentioned you can use it to get around tracking. Except that for privacy and evading tracking you need more than just a VPN, you need to be doing things like adblocking, tracker blocking, clearing all of your cookies, not signing in to anything because then the service gets to link your new VPN IP with you again. VPN ads that sell \"privacy\" is snake oil unless it is paired with a guide on the additional things you should be doing. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22125694,"author":"jlgaddis","month":"2020-01","story":"U.S. surveillance laws have proven ineffective at countering terrorism","stance":"rec","why":"Honest VPN commercial the sponsor disliked","quote":"It's well worth the seven and a half minutes to watch it, IMO.","text":"Tom Scott recently made an video [0] you might be interested in. It's well worth the seven and a half minutes to watch it, IMO. > I tried to write a more honest VPN commercial. The sponsor wasn't happy about it. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21665454,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2019-11","story":"How Is NordVPN Unblocking Disney+?","stance":"rec","why":"Honest VPN commercial exposing sponsor's misleading claims","quote":"You may enjoy Tom Scott's video","text":"> * Seriously creepy stuff. I hate how VPNs are being shilled by e-celebs these days as a privacy improvement.* You may enjoy Tom Scott's video: > I tried to write a more honest VPN commercial. The sponsor wasn't happy about it. • Get ■■■ days of ■■■ VPN free at ■■■■.com/honest * [VIDEO]"},{"id":45123775,"author":"JdeBP","month":"2025-09","story":"Who Owns, Operates, and Develops Your VPN Matters","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts VPN advertising with real features","quote":"Tom Scott's honest VPN advertisement","text":"At this point I'm reminded of Tom Scott's honest VPN advertisement , contrasting how VPNs are advertised (on YouTube, at least) with the main features that they really provide. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":41945290,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2024-10","story":"Post World War II Food","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why VPN ad copy was misleading","quote":"where he explains why a lot of the ad copy at that time was often misleading, and why he didn't take money from them","text":"> Given the amount of advertising for vpns you might think they are a scam. Tom Scott did a video in 2019 entitled \"This Video Is Sponsored By [redacted] VPN\" where he explains why a lot of the ad copy at that time was often misleading, and why he didn't take money from them: * [VIDEO] In 2022 he made video with an ad read from a VPN provider with more honest claims about their use cases: * [LINK]"},{"id":36387514,"author":"simondotau","month":"2023-06","story":"Swing VPN app is a DDoS botnet","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Famously described by Tom Scott","text":"> one major problem is VPNs are advertised or promoted as if they're synonymous with antivirus software. Famously described by Tom Scott — [How it started [VIDEO] [How it's going] [LINK]"},{"id":35152853,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"NordVPN library and client code open-sourced","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why most VPN ad reasons are useless","quote":"where he explains most of the reasons listed to use VPN by ads is useless","text":"This reminds me of the 'original' 2019 video by Tom Scott called \"This Video Is Sponsored By [redacted] VPN\" where he explains most of the reasons listed to use VPN by ads is useless: * [VIDEO] And then a few years later with \"My robot double sells out (so I don't have to)\" he did a follow-up listing useful reasons (geo-based content, better prices on vacation sites, etc) which was sponsored by NordVPN: * [LINK]"},{"id":35152900,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"NordVPN library and client code open-sourced","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why most VPN ad reasons are useless","quote":"where he explains most of the reasons listed to use VPN by ads is useless.","text":"> [VIDEO] This is Tom Scott's 'original' 2019 video called \"This Video Is Sponsored By [redacted] VPN\" where he explains most of the reasons listed to use VPN by ads is useless. However a few years later with \"My robot double sells out (so I don't have to)\" he did a follow-up listing useful reasons (geo-based content, better prices on vacation sites, etc) in which he was sponsored by NordVPN: * [LINK]"},{"id":34413522,"author":"noirscape","month":"2023-01","story":"The FBI Identified a Tor User","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No offense but TOR is just too sluggish to be really useful unless you're well a gay pirate assassin (or one of those three components)[0]. It has basically all the drawbacks a VPN has (most sites will distrust you on recaptcha, speed bumps, sometimes a connection gets timed out or dropped) whilst having a shitton of latency on top of that because y'know, it's free. There's always been more people willing to use TOR than the server space needed to prevent overburdening the network (in no small part because running the server space needed usually incurs significant legal risks since congrats, you've now become a target for law enforcement to bust your door down and ask why an IP you own a machine on is being used to upload CP to the clearnet - few people want to deal with this scenario for blatantly obvious reasons). This isn't really something you can fix, it's just kind of an inherent issue with the protocol. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30639718,"author":"david_allison","month":"2022-03","story":"Investigating Influencer VPN Ads on YouTube [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I wrote a more honest advert for VPN services and I found a company that was willing to sponsor the video.","text":"> I wrote a more honest advert for VPN services and I found a company that was willing to sponsor the video. Unfortunately they kept asking for changes, and we disagreed on those, so at the last minute, I have had to blank their name out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25173954,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2020-11","story":"Show HN: WireGuard vs. OpenVPN, a 2.5x Difference","stance":"reference","why":"Describes issues with VPN marketing","quote":"This video[1] that describes some of the issues with VPN marketing in general","text":"Half off-topic but I find NordVPN's marketing extremely distasteful and in the same vein as snake oil (same as many VPN providers). This video[1] that describes some of the issues with VPN marketing in general is so widespread that it has created old-school SEO sites[2] to game it where they hide the actual search term with white font on white background. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":23894831,"author":"pen2l","month":"2020-07","story":"Seven 'no log' VPN providers accused of leaking 2TB of user data","stance":"reference","why":"VPN as honeypot argument","quote":"","text":"> If you wanted to see what the most paranoid, security-conscious people are connecting to, and you wanted to install software on their systems that is designed to read all their network traffic and then redirect it through a single choke point, then setting up a VPN service with a huge advertising budget would be a great way to do it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48982217,"author":"dijit","month":"2026-07","story":"I'm Done with Mullvad","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"it's always funny how the hardline advocates of the free internet (especially outside of the US) are subject to this kind of unpersoning. It's enough to consider that there's something malicious going on. But I guess we should use one of the VPN providers that aren't pushing for systems transparency[0] and are dubiously everywhere across all of youtube advertising despite that being provably the place where the scummiest of companies go.[1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] & [LINK] Not wanting unfettered and uncontrolled migration (when it has proven to massively increase crime - which was the context) is not racism, that's stupid. If 15% of the population of Japan had moved from the UK in the last 10 years and committed a disproportionate amount of crime, it would cause racism, but talking about it as a problem that needs to be solved is not itself racism."},{"id":35152725,"author":"msla","month":"2023-03","story":"NordVPN library and client code open-sourced","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That's a video about NordVPN's dishonest advertising and how deeply it's infected YouTube.","text":"[VIDEO] That's a video about NordVPN's dishonest advertising and how deeply it's infected YouTube. [LINK] > The UK's Advertising Standards Agency(Opens in a new window) has banned a NordVPN commercial for misleading viewers about the privacy risks of using a public Wi-Fi network without also having a VPN. In essence: HTTPS already does what NordVPN claims you need a VPN to do. More, in fact, because HTTPS validates that the domain you're communicating with is the domain that shows up in your browser's address bar, which a VPN can't do on its own."}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"6y8XgGhXkTQ":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a nuanced analysis of online harassment, defensiveness, and gaming culture conflict.","q":{"x":"This video series does a pretty amazing job in describing those two groups and their motivations","i":10456090,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":16136599,"author":"jdlshore","month":"2018-01","story":"The problem of game developers receiving abusive messages","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed investigation of GamerGate's origins.","quote":"There is a fantastic YouTube series that goes into detail","text":"GamerGate was orchestrated by misogynistic racists on 4chan's /pol/ board. They used \"ethics in journalism\" as a deliberate excuse to rile people up, and tricked a large crowd of gamers into buying that excuse and thus providing them with cover and additional power. Ultimately it was a disgusting and successful attack against women and people of color. By repeating the excuse (that it was about a game review, when in fact it was an orchestrated attack, and the supposed review does not exist ), you're perpetuating a mistake (providing cover to a small group of misogynistic racists) that caused a lot of harm. I doubt you did this intentionally, so I suggest you do some more research. There is a fantastic YouTube series that goes into detail, including investigating the public record of the people behind GamerGate, here: [VIDEO] If you can't watch the whole thing (although it's worth it), this part goes into the origins of GamerGate: [LINK] ."},{"id":10456090,"author":"chiaro","month":"2015-10","story":"SXSW Cancels Gamer Panels After Threats","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Describes motivations of harassers and those caught up with them.","quote":"This video series does a pretty amazing job in describing those two groups and their motivations","text":"This video series does a pretty amazing job in describing those two groups and their motivations (the harassers, and those caught up with them): [VIDEO]"},{"id":9953442,"author":"gradstudent","month":"2015-07","story":"The Oppressed Rulers of the Games Industry","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Analysis of strawman critiques of Sarkeesian.","quote":"It's really well done and worth checking out","text":"Invariably such refutations fixate on the specific examples chosen by Sarkeesian and go on to make flawed arguments like e.g. (paraphrasing): > Violence against women in XYZ is not problem because XYZ also features violence against men! Which is completely besides the point and ignores the societal context into which these games are released. Other common \"critiques\" leveled against Sarkeesian, that I've seen anyway, are straight up strawmen. These include gems like she's in favour of censorship or that she argues games have the ability to directly manipulate people into violence or sexism. Innuendo Studios recently posted a great analysis of this phenomenon in their \"Why Are You So Angry?\" video series. It's really well done and worth checking out: [VIDEO] The OP reads just like an Angry Jack."},{"id":32889050,"author":"wwweston","month":"2022-09","story":"DHS to Spend Almost $700k Investigating ‘Radicalization in Gaming’","stance":"rec","why":"Reflection on cultural conflict and radicalization in gaming.","quote":"The YouTube channel Innuendo Studios has done some interesting reflection on gaming in general","text":"Nobody would pause for a moment on studies investigating the cultural impact of messaging in movies or TV; in fact we talk about it regularly. Games and the culture of discussion around them have started to have a huge impact. This is worth studying. The YouTube channel Innuendo Studios has done some interesting reflection on gaming in general (I really like \"Adventure Games About Jesus\" [LINK] ) and some specific stuff on cultural conflict and radicalization in gaming: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":25811379,"author":"iuguy","month":"2021-01","story":"People that think you’re an asshole","stance":"rec","why":"Helped commenter understand own online behaviour.","quote":"I found Innuendo Studios' Why Are You So Angry[1] and SSC's varieties of Argumentative Experience[2] really helpful","text":"It doesn't matter what you do, if it experiences some success, no matter how fleeting someone somewhere will get really upset about it. I believe that part of this is connected to the inversion of small-scale private conversations into large-scale text-driven public ones without us really being fully aware of the difference. Without the context of non-verbal communication or the closeness towards people we've never met we react differently online to offline. I think it's important not to lose sight of the idea that people can blow up online, call you every name under the sun and still be perfectly good people. I found Innuendo Studios' Why Are You So Angry[1] and SSC's varieties of Argumentative Experience[2] really helpful in coming to terms with my own online behaviour. There's also a pg essay[3] that's fairly relevant. I particularly enjoyed Rationality.org's double-cruxing approach[4]. Right now I'm focusing on avoiding continuing discussions at the point they stop adding overall. Nobody's perfect but it's definitely keeping my internal Angry Jack at bay. [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK] [4] - [LINK]"},{"id":24026964,"author":"rewq4321","month":"2020-08","story":"Tech Journalism Is Less Diverse Than Tech","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced video essays on the controversy.","quote":"here's a series of video essays which gives the whole thing some nuance","text":"> If the audience is more outraged/enthused at a group being moral crusaders then they'll push that angle. Well, yes. News companies are mostly reflections of their audiences, and if they weren't, they wouldn't exist. This is mostly a problem with the people, unless you want to try \"top-down\" media companies like China et al. That said, drawing an equivalence between the recent BLM protests and \"Gamergate\" is... a bit much. Gamergate is/was a toxic hell-hole of a movement in my opinion. Just a thin veil over a mass harassment campaign. But here's a series of video essays which gives the whole thing some nuance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16464741,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2018-02","story":"Why can’t women get pregnant without the menstrual cycle? (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why people get defensive about being judged.","quote":"please watch Why Are You So Angry? by Innuendo Studios[0]","text":"Before continuing the conversation, can I ask you to take a moment to step back and ask yourself with honesty whether you think that these statements imply that I am saying that you are not a good person because you also behave this way. Because if so, there is a - perfectly reasonable - misunderstanding of my intent here. Read what I wrote again, more closely: all it is describing is a societal norm that I would like to get rid of. If you think you don't act that way, great! This is not an accusation, it would be a perfectly understandable response (I get defensive all the time when my GF points things out to me). At no point am I talking about you, nor saying that you are \"not a real man\". At no point am I saying that you are a bad person if you do or do not act this way. I'm sorry that you feel defensive, but there really is no need to. I'm not accusing you, nor would I blame you or any man for having toxic masculine behaviours. It's not your fault to be raised in a society with those norms, after all. If you have the time, please watch Why Are You So Angry? by Innuendo Studios[0]. It also goes into how societal norms about good and evil train us to think things are about us and personal attacks when they aren't (the video highlights gamergate, but the process is not gender specific). But the short version is: people tend to get very defensive about topics like sustainability, feminism, racism, animal rights, etcetera, because it makes them question whether they are good p"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["LQRAfJyEsko",1],["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["4moyKUHApq4",1]]},"t_rzYnXEQlE":{"wy":"HN loves the deep, hands-on look at N64 hardware quirks such as bus contention and the surprisingly large wins from low-hanging optimizations.","q":{"x":"amazing video about refactoring Mario 64 to reach 30 FPS and render 6x faster on N64","i":38986826,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39547200,"author":"alpaca128","month":"2024-02","story":"How video games use lookup tables","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reworked Mario 64 code for stable 60FPS","quote":"I love that channel, he reworked the entire Mario 64 code","text":"Not always, though it might depend on what platform you mean with retro. Kaze Emanuar on YT does a lot of development for the N64 and it feels like half the time he talks about how the memory bus affects all kinds of optimizations. In Mario 64 he replaced the original lookup table for the sine function because an approximation was faster and accurate enough (or rather two approximations for two different purposes). I love that channel, he reworked the entire Mario 64 code [0] to make it run at stable 60FPS...because he wanted his mods to run faster. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38986826,"author":"CSMastermind","month":"2024-01","story":"Decompilation of Paper Mario for N64","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Refactoring Mario 64 for 30 FPS and split-screen co-op","quote":"amazing video about refactoring Mario 64 to reach 30 FPS and render 6x faster on N64","text":"There's an amazing video about refactoring Mario 64 to reach 30 FPS and render 6x faster on N64: [VIDEO] The changes were much more extensive than just removing the debug flags. They were also able to get local coop and a 4 player split screen working which were features originally intended for the game but cut for performance reasons."},{"id":40047672,"author":"Apofis","month":"2024-04","story":"I ported thousands of apps to Windows 95 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Developer optimized SM64 code to 60fps+","quote":"optimized the ever living hell out of to get 60fps+","text":"If you found the video on Super Mario 64 being published with debug symbols interesting, check this out: After finding out about the debug symbols, a developer took a deeper dive through the codebase and optimized the ever living hell out of to get 60fps+. FIXING the ENTIRE SM64 Source Code (INSANE N64 performance) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38947343,"author":"Wolfbeta","month":"2024-01","story":"Super Mario 64 on the Web","stance":"rec","why":"Modder optimizing SM64 source to double frame rate","quote":"enjoying watching this young modder on YouTube optimize all of the original SM64 source code","text":"I've recently been enjoying watching this young modder on YouTube optimize all of the original SM64 source code, doubling frame rate, and modding his own Return To Yoshi's Island [VIDEO]"},{"id":37969315,"author":"rdlw","month":"2023-10","story":"The Worlds First FPGA N64","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of optimizing Super Mario 64 for complex romhacks","quote":"an amazing series about optimizing Super Mario 64","text":"If anyone is interested in new projects for N64 hardware, Kaze Emanuar has an amazing series about optimizing Super Mario 64 to run incredibly complex romhacks on original hardware. Interestingly, SM64 is usually throttled by memory speed. This means that using 'inline' is detrimental to performance, since making the program larger can cause another read from memory. Here's an overview video, he has many more specific videos about specific topics: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37267408,"author":"psychphysic","month":"2023-08","story":"Leaked Wipeout source code leads to near-total rewrite and remaster","stance":"rec","why":"Fun watch on limitations justifying optimizations","quote":"His videos on the Sine function in sm64 is a fun watch","text":"Excellent..more of this please. Super Mario 64 has also had stupendous rewrites from decompiled code I believe. [VIDEO] His videos on the Sine function in sm64 is a fun watch as the limitations make nearly all optimizations justifiable."},{"id":35206349,"author":"psychphysic","month":"2023-03","story":"The model for the coins in Super Mario Odyssey is 65% simpler than in SM Galaxy","stance":"rec","why":"N64 quirks and decompiled Mario 64 optimization work","quote":"If you like this stuff Kaze Emanuar does loads of neat work","text":"If you like this stuff Kaze Emanuar does loads of neat work with the super mario 64 decompiled code. [VIDEO] Loads of interesting quirks of the N64 to do with slow bus."},{"id":34365497,"author":"MarioMan","month":"2023-01","story":"Nintendo 64 Java","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive into optimizing Super Mario 64 source","quote":"Kaze has done a deep dive into optimizing the Super Mario 64 source code for his ROM hacks.","text":"On a related note, Kaze has done a deep dive into optimizing the Super Mario 64 source code for his ROM hacks. Here’s one of his more popular videos on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44487635,"author":"haiku2077","month":"2025-07","story":"I Ported SAP to a 1976 CPU. It Wasn't That Slow","stance":"reference","why":"Lookup tables slower on N64 due to shared bus","quote":"it shared a single bus between the RAM","text":"> Lookup tables are always faster than calculation - is that true? I know it's not always true on the Nintendo 64, because it shared a single bus between the RAM and \"GPU\": [VIDEO] , [LINK]"},{"id":44025163,"author":"phire","month":"2025-05","story":"Palette lighting tricks on the Nintendo 64","stance":"reference","why":"Details N64 memory bus contention flaws","quote":"Kaze Emanuar has a bunch of videos","text":"Both use a unified memory architecture, where the GPU and CPU share the same pool of memory. On the N64, the CPU always ends up bottlenecked by memory latency. The RAM latency is quite high to start with, your CPU is sitting idle for ~40 cycles if it ever misses the cache, assuming RCP is idle. If RCP is not idle, contention with can sometimes push that well over 150 cycles. Kaze Emanuar has a bunch of videos (like this one [VIDEO] ) going into detail about this flaw. The gamecube fixed this flaw in multiple ways. They picked a CPU with a much better cache subsystem. The PowerPC 750 had Multi-way caches instead of a direct mapped, and a quite large L2 cache. Their customisations added special instructions to stream graphics commands without polluting the caches, resulting in way less cache misses. And when it did cache miss, the latency to main memory is under 20 cycles (despite the Gamecube's CPU running at 5x the clock speed). The engineers picked main memory that was super low latency. To fix the issue of bus contention, they created a complex bus arbitration scheme and gave CPU reads the highest priority. The gamecube also has much less traffic on the bus to start with, because many components were moved out of the unified memory. --------------------------- The N64 famously had only 4KB of TMEM (texture memory). Textures had to fit in just 4KB, and to enable mipmapping, they had to fit in half that. This lead to most games on the N64 using very small textures stretched o"},{"id":43117829,"author":"01HNNWZ0MV43FF","month":"2025-02","story":"RT64: N64 graphics renderer in emulators and native ports","stance":"reference","why":"Mario 64 code optimization claiming roughly 6x speedup","quote":"claims roughly 6x speedup","text":"For those who haven't heard, Kaze is the guy who optimized Mario 64's code, fixed some physics bugs, etc., claims roughly 6x speedup, so that he can make a mod / ROM-hack that runs smoother despite having better graphics: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42291039,"author":"Manuel_D","month":"2024-12","story":"Programming the C64 with Visual Studio Code","stance":"reference","why":"Mario 64 optimized to run better on original hardware","quote":"optimized super Mario 64 to such a degree that it runs with much better framerate on the original hardware","text":"Retro game coders achieve some pretty astounding results sometimes. One of my favorite examples is one who optimized super Mario 64 to such a degree that it runs with much better framerate on the original hardware. Also, multiplayer was added: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39747189,"author":"echelon","month":"2024-03","story":"VR Powered by N64 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Creator of incredible N64 demos and technical videos","quote":"James is like Kaze","text":"This is what James Lambert does as a follow up when Valve shuts down [1] his \"Portal in N64\" project [2]. James is like Kaze [3] and builds incredible N64 demos [4] and technical videos about them. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":38741369,"author":"dharmab","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: What's your \"it's not stupid if it works\" story?","stance":"reference","why":"Loop unrolling hurting performance due to bus contention","quote":"a modder recently found that unrolling loops","text":"Similarly, a modder recently found that unrolling loops _hurt_ performance on the N64 because of RAM bus contention: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36500897,"author":"kibwen","month":"2023-06","story":"Finding the best sine function for Nintendo 64","stance":"reference","why":"Low-hanging optimizations with demonstrated improvements","quote":"picking the low-hanging fruit results in much greater improvements","text":"Kaze has already done so, and picking the low-hanging fruit results in much greater improvements than the optimizations shown here (which frees up less than one millisecond per frame). Here's the previous video with explanations and video demonstration of their improvements: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36502162,"author":"phire","month":"2023-06","story":"Finding the best sine function for Nintendo 64","stance":"reference","why":"Tracks CPU and RCP time across optimizations","quote":"he tracks both CPU and RCP time over various optimisations","text":"In one of Kaze's older videos [1] , he tracks both CPU and RCP time over various optimisations. The numbers make it pretty apparent that you can get significant RDP performance gains by reducing the amount of CPU bandwidth and/or improving it's access locality. The N64 is a unified memory system, and memory stalls triggered by the CPU will slow RDP down. The N64's memory controller appears to be very simple. As I understand it, if one sub-component attempts to access a DRAM row that is closed (DRAM rows are 2KB long), then the entire memory controller stalls for ~100ns as the RDRAM chip closes the previous row (writing out any dirty changes) and opens the new row. The controller doesn't appear to do any reordering to optimise access patterns. If multiple components are accessing the same 1MB bank simultaneously, you can hit pathological bad cases were the entire system slows down, as the memory controller is continually stalling for 100ns at a time while the memory chips close and reopen rows. Which is why some games can enable a high resolution mode when the memory expansion pack is present. They often don't need the extra 4MB of ram, but simply being able to strategically spread their data across eight different banks instead of four banks can massively improve performance. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31741494,"author":"Thaxll","month":"2022-06","story":"Quake on the Game Boy Advance [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Impressive optimization of Mario 64 in C","quote":"I remember that impressive opti on Mario64","text":"Are modern compiler good enough / have good optimization on old hardware so that you don't need to write ASM? I remember that impressive opti on Mario64 and it's using C / GCC: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31104925,"author":"prophesi","month":"2022-04","story":"Overhauling Mario 64's code to reach 30 FPS and render 6x faster on N64 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains cut splitscreen multiplayer due to performance","quote":"he talks about this at 14:50","text":"I think the main thing is that Nintendo had splitscreen multiplayer planned, but performance prevented it. Edit: Got farther into the video and he talks about this at 14:50 ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":33681161,"author":"psychphysic","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Publish old projects even though the source code embarrasses you by now?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Just look at the face palms at Nintendo HQ","text":"Just open source it. No one will judge you by 15 year old code if you have recently produced good code. That's what people will look at. Just look at the face palms at Nintendo HQ despite the amazing game they made [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nmgFG7PUHfo",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1],["rgaTLrZGlk0",1],["sR8M4zARBXY",1],["PtKhbbcc1Rc",1],["xOCurBYI_gY",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1],["uqY3FMuMuRo",1],["uNjxe8ShM-8",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1]]},"tiJVJ5QRRUE":{"wy":"Recommended in HN threads on gender gaps in tech as a well-researched look at how gender differences and professional choices may arise.","q":{"x":"the whole series is brilliant","i":10372392,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10372392,"author":"T2_t2","month":"2015-10","story":"What Really Keeps Women Out of Tech","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on Scandinavian attitudes to gender relations","quote":"the whole series is brilliant","text":"> We yearn to be with others like ourselves and to fit in. The idea is that this is what \"keeps people out\". Surely, the flip side is true, and people are defined / made happy by the same notions. People in the west are so far up Maslow's hierarchy of needs, that we've stopped caring about the basics of life. Specifically, of [LINK] we no longer choose a job that gives us food, shelter or safety (the first two), and instead we choose jobs based on how well it provides us with love/belonging, esteem and self-actualisation. That is why young people choose jobs less and less with money as the sole or even primary determinant. If this is the case, that people are choosing professions that help them feel self-actualised or belonging, no matter what tech does, or what Public Relations, Child-care workers or predominantly female dominated occupations do, is less important that the people it attracts / already has attracted. And these same sorts of people will dominate, as this belonging and fitting in becomes increasingly entrenched, and snowballs on itself like runaway recurssion. That seems, to me, the logical conclusion of slight biological gender differences combined with a rise up the pyramid of needs, which lessens the pressure of the former two to create cultures that trump biology. [VIDEO] is a documentary about Scandinavia's attitude to gender relations, and the whole series is brilliant, and shows that gender separation is, if not inevitable, certainly understandable."},{"id":9090704,"author":"sputr","month":"2015-02","story":"An Open Letter to Jessica Livingston About YC's Female Founders Conference","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Norwegian documentary on gender equality paradox","quote":"I think everyone discussing any gender gap should really watch this Norwegian documentary","text":"I think everyone discussing any gender gap should really watch this Norwegian documentary: Brainwashed: The Gender Equality Paradox [VIDEO] The \"social expectations\" hypothesis fails to explain why countries with higher living standards have more polarized sectors. The idea that in a perfect world with no \"social pressure\" we would reach a parity between genders ignores just about all research done on the biological gender differences."},{"id":29333925,"author":"mzan","month":"2021-11","story":"Stereotypes about girls dissuade many from careers in computer science","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on gendered interests","quote":"you will find interesting answers","text":"If you view \"Brainwash: The Gender Equality Paradox\" on YouTube, you will find interesting answers. [VIDEO] In a scientific experiment, from the age of nine months, boys choose masculine toys, while girls choose femmine toys. Boys are more attract from inanimate systems, while girls from social interactions. Obviously culture has an effect, every person is unique, etc., etc. So there cannot be limitation on what a person want and can do. It is only unrealistic pretending that 50% of boys and 50% of girls will have the same interests in work and life, because they are different."},{"id":24652888,"author":"woohoojoo","month":"2020-10","story":"Half of young women will leave their tech job by age 35, study finds","stance":"rec","why":"Comedian interviews academics across the political spectrum on gender","quote":"a Scandinavian comedian explores this topic by interviewing academics from various sides of the political spectrum","text":"100% agree. There are major psychological And physiological differences Between men and women. (Reminder: difference is the opposite of equal. Also, genetically distinct individuals are not equal. “Equal” is only useful in quantitative analysis). Learn more here: “Gender Equality Paradox” - a Scandinavian comedian explores this topic by interviewing academics from various sides of the political spectrum. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21528927,"author":"the-dude","month":"2019-11","story":"A.I. Systems Echo Biases They’re Fed, Putting Scientists on Guard","stance":"rec","why":"Norwegian documentary covering gender, including visit to a doctor","quote":"Brainwash, a Norwegian documentary does a good job","text":"Brainwash, a Norwegian documentary does a good job : [VIDEO] Among other things, he visits a doctor who has to decide which gender to choose for babies who obviously need some work ( sorry for the wording )."},{"id":12860438,"author":"T2_t2","month":"2016-11","story":"You can’t fix diversity in tech without fixing the technical interview","stance":"rec","why":"Norwegian documentary on gender choices in professions","quote":"is a Norwegian documentary on this, in 8 parts, and it is well worth a watch.","text":"> I am less inclined to believe there is systemic bias against women, and minorities, and instead a culture difference between groups who are well represented in engineering, and groups which are not. And that cultural difference is INDIVIDUAL CHOICE. When people are free to choose whatever they like, because they aren't scared of starving, which is true in Western countries, they overwhelmingly choose gender stereotypical professions. This is most true in the most gender neutral countries, Scandanavia basically, where the gap is the widest in traditional roles such as nursing. As you go down the GDP per capita list, the gender gap shrinks. Indian, Chinese, and Russians are from cultures where ANY job is hard to get, and good jobs are very few and far between. Because jobs are scarce and a safe, middle class life requires sacrifice, people in these developed countries are more likely to choose jobs based on pay and availability than what they will enjoy. [VIDEO] is a Norwegian documentary on this, in 8 parts, and it is well worth a watch."},{"id":7617204,"author":"oblique63","month":"2014-04","story":"Who Gets Into Accelerators? Persistent Men With SaaS Apps, Says Study","stance":"rec","why":"Norwegian documentary questioning social-cause view of gender disparity","quote":"Recently saw a really interesting well-researched Norwegian documentary that explores this topic","text":"> Most people feel that isn't the case though. That the disparity is a social problem that we can change and fix. Recently saw a really interesting well-researched Norwegian documentary that explores this topic and kinda throws that whole view into question: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29593094,"author":"stopglobalism","month":"2021-12","story":"Job Applicant Resumes Are Effectively Impossible to De-Gender","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Men != Women Women != Men Sorry if that offends you. Youtube: \"Brainwash: The Gender Equality Paradox\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":21115500,"author":"pdub123","month":"2019-09","story":"Employers Used Facebook to Keep Women and Older Workers from Seeing Job Ads","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as one of several sources on sex differences in interests","quote":"","text":"It's not, it's actually natural. Presenting it as bad is simply, at worst: propaganda espoused by sheep and their shepherds, and at best: ignornace. There has been a ton of research done on \"Thing vs People\" career interests, which show very clearly that men prefer working with Things, whereas Women prefer working with People. (When I say prefer, I mean the average in a normal distribution) Simply google: occupational interests people vs things Ex1: \"Men and Things, Women and People: A Meta-Analysis of Sex Differences in Interests\" [LINK] Ex2: \"Straight Talk About Sex Differences in Occupational Choices and Work-Family Tradeoffs\" \"Sex differences in occupational interests have been known for decades, and a recent aggregate analysis of the interests of more than 500,000 people shows that some of these differences are quite large.1 The most relevant finding here is that about 15% of women have the same level of interest in engineering as the average man; 50% of men, by definition, would have stronger interests in engineering than the average man.\" [LINK] Ex3: \"Brainwash: The Gender Equality Paradox\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12005194,"author":"sah2ed","month":"2016-06","story":"We built voice modulation to mask gender in technical interviews","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary exploring gender differences","quote":"There's a documentary titled Brainwash: The Gender Equality Paradox that explored those differences","text":"There's a documentary titled Brainwash: The Gender Equality Paradox that explored those differences [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9374516,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2015-04","story":"Women preferred 2:1 over men for STEM faculty positions","stance":"reference","why":"Norwegian documentary discussing more equal societies having more unequal outcomes","quote":"Talked about in \"Brainwash\", a Norwegian documentary[2].","text":"There are actually studies on this. It turns out that the more egalitarian and more wealthy a society, the more unequal the outcomes[1]. Talked about in \"Brainwash\", a Norwegian documentary[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K_nnUbX7uuQ",1],["0sHCQWjTrJ8",1],["la-sGpTpkxE",1],["MGfhQTbcqmA",1]]},"RuOvn4UqznU":{"wy":"Several HN commenters call it one of the best explanations of fasting and insulin and recommend it in diet and weight loss threads.","q":{"x":"This is the best video I have found to explain the incredible changes and benefits of multi-da","i":40506544,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40506544,"author":"robomartin","month":"2024-05","story":"Ozempic keeps wowing: trial data show benefits for kidney disease","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains benefits of multi-day fasting","quote":"This is the best video I have found to explain the incredible changes and benefits of multi-da","text":"No, you are wrong. It's a binary decision. Millions of people do it. The key is to make the decision and stick to it. The \"magic pill\" for sticking to the decision is starting with a 3 to 5 day water-only fast, which is easy. Ask anyone who has done it. This rewires your brain, removes cravings and more. From there, just eat fruits, vegetables, fermented foods and your favorite range of proteins. If type 2 diabetic, avoid high glycemic index foods and favor fiber (which slows down the rate of change of your blood glucose level). Except for extreme cases, a type 2 diabetic can be off medication a week after fasting. A decision that should be made under medical supervision, of course. The key is that simple decision: Today, I stop eating for three days (or five, but three is a great starting point, with seven being amazing). And anyone can do this. Of course, again, consult with a medical professional for safety and recommendations. Fasting IS NOT ABOUT LOSING WEIGHT. This is what I though for many years before I learned. Fasting sets off a wide range of biochemical processes in your body that are incredibly beneficial on more than one front. It is these changes, as well as the psychological perspective gained (Oh, wow, I don't have to eat every three hours!) that lead to weight loss, the reversal of inflammation and metabolic disease. The objective, however, again, isn't weight loss. This is the best video I have found to explain the incredible changes and benefits of multi-da"},{"id":30628644,"author":"rjammala","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Any weird tips for weight loss?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Guide for pursuing intermittent fasting","quote":"highly recommend watching this video","text":"Intermittent Fasting, specifically OMAD. Cut all sugars and processed foods. If you want to pursue this, highly recommend watching this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28170796,"author":"criticaltinker","month":"2021-08","story":"America’s losing battle against diabetes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cardiologist's talk on fasting benefits","quote":"Here is one of my favorite videos on the subject [2]","text":"I strongly believe that fasting is one of the best ways to combat obesity, diabetes, cancer, high blood pressure, and more. The scientific literature is certainly accumulating tremendous evidence supporting this hypothesis [1]. Here is one of my favorite videos on the subject [2], a talk by Dr Pradip Jamnadas who is one of the top cardiologists in Florida. There is no industry that profits on you being healthier through fasting. Consequently it doesn't get the attention or consideration that it should given the supporting evidence. However it has an advantage over every other solution suggested here - everyone can start reaping the benefits immediately . No legislation, no taxation, etc. Eating healthy and natural foods is still a critical part of the solution. Perhaps most importantly, we need to properly educate the youth and show them the benefits and consequences of their choices when it comes to diet and exercise. [1] Fasting: Molecular Mechanisms and Clinical Applications [LINK] [2] Fasting For Survival Lecture by Dr Pradip Jamnadas [VIDEO]"},{"id":27809103,"author":"joseluis","month":"2021-07","story":"A Chemical Hunger: Mysteries","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on insulin and fasting","quote":"Everybody should watch this lecture","text":"Insulin is the big elephant in the room that's barely talked about. Everybody should watch this lecture [VIDEO]"},{"id":44645362,"author":"mediumsmart","month":"2025-07","story":"Erythritol linked to brain cell damage and stroke risk","stance":"rec","why":"Diet advice from Dr Jamnadas","quote":"here listen to jamnadas and judge for yourself","text":"no regrets, never too late to change the diet :) all the things I would have to regret ... here listen to jamnadas and judge for yourself: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30627990,"author":"subpixel","month":"2022-03","story":"A beginner's guide to intermittent fasting (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Benefits of extended fasting and low-carb diet","quote":"Interesting stuff","text":"Most of this thread is about time-boxing your meal(s) and losing weight. An important aspect of fasting is that there are highly beneficial results to not eating - especially for 36 hours or more. Most of what Galen Foundation promotes seems to be a low carb, low sugar, low manufactured (including processed vegetable oils), non-vegetarian diet combined with what ever level of fasting you feel comfortable with. Interesting stuff: [VIDEO] \"Our life revolves around eating too much\". God yes it does."},{"id":37196044,"author":"mediumsmart","month":"2023-08","story":"Insulin resistance after a 3-day fast","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as explanation relating to fasting","quote":"This makes more sense to me but maybe it’s unrelated.","text":"This makes more sense to me but maybe it’s unrelated. [VIDEO] I eat once a day. An 8 hour window for eating is business as usual."}],"r":[["XI5frPV58tY",1],["-FENubmZGj8",1],["mf5otGNbkuc",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["nv7-YM4wno8",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1]]},"iRkZcTg1JWU":{"wy":"Recommended whenever the demoscene comes up as a well-made, accessible history and tour of the art form.","q":{"x":"Both are expertly crafted and fun documentaries","i":32801956,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32801956,"author":"no_time","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Expertly crafted, fun demoscene documentary with Hungarian twist","quote":"Both are expertly crafted and fun documentaries","text":"Moleman 2 - Demoscene: [VIDEO] Moleman 4 - Longplay (A videogame documentary): [LINK] Both are expertly crafted and fun documentaries about their titular subculture with a Hungarian twist."},{"id":10070275,"author":"chengsun","month":"2015-08","story":"Winning 1kb intro released at Assembly 2015","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual documentary guide to the demoscene and its algorithms","quote":"freely available to watch on YouTube; highly recommended","text":"Also, a fantastic visual guide is the documentary film \"Moleman 2\" (2011). It's freely available to watch on YouTube; highly recommended: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37931412,"author":"omoikane","month":"2023-10","story":"What is the Demoscene? An obscure but influential art form","stance":"rec","why":"English version of the Moleman 2 demoscene documentary","quote":"I second this recommendation for Moleman 2 documentary","text":"I second this recommendation for Moleman 2 documentary, but the video I found has a different link: [VIDEO] Looks like this version is in English, while the earlier link is in Hungarian, otherwise they appear to be the same film."},{"id":33200047,"author":"aphroz","month":"2022-10","story":"Using an Amiga in 2021: Making an intro","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on demoscene art including impressive 4k intros","quote":"If you are interested, watch this documentary","text":"Demoscene is such an interesting form of art. If you are interested, watch this documentary: [VIDEO] The 4k (not the resolution) are very impressive."},{"id":14393254,"author":"kodfodrasz","month":"2017-05","story":"How a 64k intro is made","stance":"rec","why":"History of the demoscene and creative process behind demos","quote":"There is a nice documentary on the history of the demoscene","text":"There is a nice documentary on the history of the demoscene, with some introspection to the creative process behind a demo/intro. It was made by a Hungarian team, who has other interesting documentaries on underground culture. It is also available in English, at: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13833882,"author":"nom","month":"2017-03","story":"Watch demos all day [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the state of the demoscene","quote":"check out the documentary","text":"If you are interested in today's* state of the demo scene, check out the documentary \"Moleman 2 - The Art Of The Algorithms (2012)\": [VIDEO] *Granted, it's 5 years old, but still awesome :D!"}],"r":[["GZadCj8O1-0",1],["xQ3oJlt4GrI",1],["WFCvkkDSfIU",1]]},"xP5-iIeKXE8":{"wy":"The canonical visual for nested simulation, Turing completeness and self-reference; HN readers call it mind-blowing and reach for it constantly.","q":{"x":"here's a famous and really clear sub-2 minutes representation of the scales involved","i":34316345,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34316345,"author":"pierreyoda","month":"2023-01","story":"Tetris Is Capable of Universal Computation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous clear sub-2 minute representation of the scales involved","quote":"here's a famous and really clear sub-2 minutes representation of the scales involved","text":"This is an excellent link, well known in the \"community\" around this. To add to it, here's a famous and really clear sub-2 minutes representation of the scales involved if it helps make things clearer, for those who've never seen the scales involved: [VIDEO] (and that's \"just\" the Game of Life in the Game of Life). I think I would enjoy helping push the state of the art in this field, but it's far beyond my abilities for the moment. If it wasn't, I would love to work on a new library specifically built for the kind of things like your idea, with the API aimed at working with metapixels-based \"hardware\", but with the lowest-level constructs (maybe by far) being thinks like RAM or CPU caches as building blocks - while still being able to zoom to the lowest level of cells in the renderer. But it's basically R&D and HPC combined if one wants to offer an interesting alternative to the established libraries. From what I know of upcoming hardware, such a library may have to wait a couple of years and basically target (as in, full buy-in, maybe at the programming language-level, certainly in tooling) the next Apple Silicon-like (or beyond) breakthough in commonly accessible hardware for a nice, \"free\" performance boost. Maybe GPUs are next?"},{"id":24831788,"author":"lqet","month":"2020-10","story":"Building a computer in Conway's game of life","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Game of Life simulated in Game of Life","quote":"I remember seeing that video 3 years ago and being blown away.","text":"I remember seeing that video 3 years ago and being blown away. In case someone doesn't already know it, here is Conway's Game of Life simulated in Conway's Game of Life: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43033330,"author":"zamalek","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: What's the best implementation of Conway's Game of Life?","stance":"rec","why":"Game of Life running in Game of Life using Golly","quote":"Here's the game of life running in the game of life","text":"It might be argued that one of the dynamic programming (caching) ones would be the best by a reasonable standard. These can be used to simulate absolutely monstrous grids, and is where we see simulated CPUs and whatnot. Here's the game of life running in the game of life: [VIDEO] - that one uses Golly."},{"id":33980086,"author":"fishtoaster","month":"2022-12","story":"Recursive Game of Life","stance":"rec","why":"Earlier Life in Life video","quote":"I've seen \"life in life\" as a video from ages ago","text":"This is amazing! I've seen \"life in life\" as a video from ages ago[0], but this is just suck a slick implementation of it! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22882928,"author":"Groxx","month":"2020-04","story":"Build a Working Game of Tetris in Conway's Game of Life (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Video of metapixels from an earlier post","quote":"has a much-needed youtube video of the metapixels","text":"2017 post has a much-needed youtube video of the metapixels as the top comment: [VIDEO] I haven't been able to find one for tetris tho"},{"id":15246914,"author":"avian","month":"2017-09","story":"Building a working game of Tetris in Conway's Game of Life","stance":"rec","why":"OTCA metapixels implementing Game of Life in Game of Life","quote":"Here's a nice video showing OTCA metapixels in action, implementing Game of Life in Game of Life.","text":"Here's a nice video showing OTCA metapixels in action, implementing Game of Life in Game of Life. [VIDEO] It would be amazing to see this Tetris computer rendered into a video like this."},{"id":35516719,"author":"umvi","month":"2023-04","story":"Nobel Prize winner: The universe keeps dying and being reborn (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> This combined with the fact those interactions have been happening for billions of years in parallel at such a massive scale does not make it surprising that persistent and self replicating patterns emerge. On a large enough scale, even improbable events become likely. I don't think anyone has proven the scales necessary for life to happen by chance. What you have done is worked off the base assumption that abiogenesis happened accidentally and therefore the time and scale needed to do so is the age and scale of the observable universe. Under the infinite monkeys theorem that the works of Shakespeare would eventually be produced, but we are not working with infinite time and resources here. We are working \"just\" 9 billion years and \"just\" 100 billion galaxies, which, for abiogenesis seems pretty short especially considering what % of planets have zero chance for life due to their composition and star proximity. How many billions of years of compute time across how many parallel computers do you think it would take Conway's game of life with random RNG seeds to simulate itself ( [VIDEO] )? Regardless of your answer, I still find it more likely Conway's game of life simulating itself points to an intelligent force of some sort having iteratively created it."},{"id":34553439,"author":"gbell12","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the weirdest or most surreal (recent) technology you have seen?","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life running in Game of Life as surreal technology","quote":"Game of Life running in Game of Life","text":"Computers implemented in other computers. Specifically: Minecraft running in Minecraft [LINK] or Game of Life running in Game of Life [VIDEO] Bonus: Digital clock implemented in Game of Life [LINK] Surreal because it makes it apparent that our universe could really be emergent behavior from a simple set of rules."},{"id":32779904,"author":"DougBTX","month":"2022-09","story":"Python type hints are Turing complete","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life emulating itself","quote":"","text":"FWIW: [VIDEO] If you allowed toggling particular points manually as input from a controller... probably not too far away from possible!"},{"id":31805804,"author":"woojoo666","month":"2022-06","story":"Consciousness is not computation","stance":"reference","why":"Turing complete systems simulating inside others","quote":"","text":"I think the author is misunderstanding the XKCD comic about rocks [1] (or maybe I am). Just because it's possible to run a simulation of the universe on rocks, doesn't mean that rocks are conscious or turing complete. You can't forget about the person who is manipulating the rocks. The system as a whole is turing complete. Likewise with the bar of iron example that the author gave, you can't forget about the person interpreting the atoms in the bar of iron. The system as a whole is turing complete (and also naturally conscious, because the person doing the interpretation is conscious). And there is nothing physical necessary to represent such systems. You can simulate turing complete systems inside turing complete systems [2]. So I don't see why consciousness has to be a \"physical phenomenon\" as the author claims. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30220333,"author":"caf","month":"2022-02","story":"Accidentally Turing-Complete","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life implemented within Game of Life","quote":"you can implement Game of Life within Game of Life","text":"One thing it doesn't mention is Conway's Game of Life itself. So of course, you can implement Game of Life within Game of Life: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29338166,"author":"eggy","month":"2021-11","story":"Neuro Evolution of Augmented Topologies","stance":"reference","why":"Recursive Game of Life emulation","quote":"","text":"I missed that, thanks! I'll have to take another look. When I think of neural networks that evolve changes in their topology and the weights between those connections, over and over towards optimizing a solution to function, I get the vision of the recursive Life in Life video[1] where Conway's Game of Life is emulated in Game of Life. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27737815,"author":"scotty79","month":"2021-07","story":"What are the odds we are living in a computer simulation? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Nested simulation","quote":"","text":"Unknowable. And no, you'll have no way to break out of simulation and access api of the simulator, because if it's in place it works similarly to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26823872,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2021-04","story":"Time flies in Google Earth’s biggest update in years","stance":"reference","why":"Shows complexity at scale looks like lower level","quote":"This Game of Life video shows what I mean perfectly","text":"Right, obviously as scale goes up, much more complexity is needed to maintain the same level of behavior. A bit like how unicellular organisms can replicate simply, but humans need to go through a whole process to make an offspring. This Game of Life video shows what I mean perfectly: [VIDEO] Yes, there's a ton of complexity in there, but when you zoom out enough, it looks and acts very similar to the lower level."},{"id":26476630,"author":"knome","month":"2021-03","story":"Show HN: Create your own cellular automata","stance":"reference","why":"Complexity from simple rules","quote":"","text":"They are a novelty that is often surprising in the how their simple set of rules can lead to quite amazing emergent complexity. That the one-time chaotic game of life was eventually shown to be able to stably infinitely expand and be turing complete is quite interesting. I don't know that they'll ever be useful for efficient computation, but they will likely always remain fun. [VIDEO] I have not read it myself, but I know that Stephen Wolfram ( of Mathematica/Wolfram Alpha) laid out some arguments for the usefulness of studying cellular automata in his early 2000s book \"A New Kind of Science\"."},{"id":25087662,"author":"BenoitP","month":"2020-11","story":"Factorio 1.1 is getting close","stance":"reference","why":"Example of enormous speedups from memoization","quote":"","text":"My secret wish is that they start working on Factorio 2 with a new item state-memoizing game engine. Let me explain. Conway's game of life organisms' different states can be memoized. Starting the simulation slowly, an engine can memoize the following computations. This is what Hashlife [1] does. Quoting from Wikipedia: \"the rapid increase in speed is often described as \"exploding\".\" So much so that this kind of thing is possible: [VIDEO] Simulating the next ticks just entail looking up the future. Except for things the users touch. Since Factorio is a game about reaching the next scale of automation, I believe this is a worthy follow-up to Factorio 1. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":24689657,"author":"munificent","month":"2020-10","story":"Cells are fast and crowded places (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life implemented in Game of Life shows many inner steps per outer step","quote":"here's Conway's Game of Life implemented using... Conway's Game of Life","text":"I find this initially unintuitive, but it makes sense when you think about it. Look at any other system that produces aggregate emergent behavior out the behavior of smaller parts. For example, here's Conway's Game of Life implemented using... Conway's Game of Life: [VIDEO] It takes many many steps of the inner game of life to produce a single iteration of the outer simulation. In general, with emergent behavior, it takes a lot of inner steps to produce a single meaningful step in the outer system. So it makes sense that our cells are much faster than we intuititively think of as \"fast\". Because our intuition about speed is itself the product an emergent system. We are that larger Game of Life there, so it looks incredibly fast to watch the inner one according to our own time scale."},{"id":18757159,"author":"trevyn","month":"2018-12","story":"Lenia – Biology of Artificial Life","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life example","quote":"","text":"Molecular biology: [LINK] Game of Life: [VIDEO] Sure, one is 2D and discrete, the other is 3D and continuous... oh wait, Lenia is 2D and continuous. That’s interesting."},{"id":14471012,"author":"posterboy","month":"2017-06","story":"Oh My Gosh, It’s Covered in Rule 30s","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life emulated inside Game of Life","quote":"","text":"I'd guess that GoL and Cellular Automaton Theory preceded and inspired rule 30 etc. So, maybe the architects implied metarecursion, that GoL metaphorically generated rule 30. GoL is Turing complete. I guess that means there is a starting condition that converges to a rule30 cellular automaton. edit: video of GoL emulated inside a turing machine in GoL [VIDEO] (link replaced)"},{"id":6462015,"author":"lelf","month":"2013-09","story":"Game of Life News: Prime numbers (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Game of Life simulating itself","quote":"","text":"Not only that, but it can find lowest common denominator, solve a system of linear equations, and even run the complete RSA algorithm (albeit with a pseudorandom RNG that's deterministic). Or run a game of live's simulation [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["l70iRcSxqzo",1],["e0elNU0iOMY",1],["TshYfYIxR9E",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1],["yYAw79386WI",1],["X1T3IQ4N-3g",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1]]},"7erJ1DV_Tlo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best video introduction to the actor model, straight from its inventor, and revisited it after Hewitt's death.","q":{"x":"It's truly a masterclass.","i":34273970,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44732798,"author":"matesz","month":"2025-07","story":"Pony: An actor-model, capabilities-secure, high-performance programming language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining 45-minute explainer on actor model foundations from Hewitt","quote":"I highly recommend this entertaining 45-minute explainer by Carl Hewitt","text":"For anyone interested in a deeper dive into the foundations of the Actor Model, I highly recommend this entertaining 45-minute explainer by Carl Hewitt, one of the model’s original pioneers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34273970,"author":"napsterbr","month":"2023-01","story":"Carl Hewitt has died [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Masterclass conversation on the actor model with Hewitt","quote":"It's truly a masterclass.","text":"May he RIP. I first heard about Hewitt after watching this[0] conversation about the actor model. It's truly a masterclass. For those who want to learn more about the actor model, or simply have a fond memory of ProfHewitt, I highly recommend it. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34275150,"author":"TowerTall","month":"2023-01","story":"Carl Hewitt has died [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite tech talk on the actor model","quote":"This is one of my favorite tech talks.","text":"This is one of my favorite tech talks. Hewitt, Meijer and Szyperski: The Actor Model (everything you wanted to know but are afraid to ask) [VIDEO]"},{"id":26535495,"author":"napsterbr","month":"2021-03","story":"A few notes on message passing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing discussion that teaches the actor model","quote":"Your discussion[0] with Meijer and Szyperski was mind-blowing and taught me a lot about the actor model.","text":"I'm sorry to go completely off-topic here, but I just wanted to say thank you! Your discussion[0] with Meijer and Szyperski was mind-blowing and taught me a lot about the actor model. This was, hands down, one of the best classes I've ever had. Thanks for sharing this knowledge. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31200751,"author":"helge9210","month":"2022-04","story":"Actor system for the JVM developed by Electronic Arts","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies actor model versus communicating sequential processes","quote":"This really helped me","text":"> so different in an actor vs a queue with workers In short: different computational model -- Actor model vs. Communicating sequential processes. This really helped me: [VIDEO] Edit: added link to video"},{"id":15183991,"author":"prodtorok","month":"2017-09","story":"Get to Know the Actor Model with JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Interview and lesson on the actor model from its inventor","quote":"For those who like to learn from videos, here is an interview/lesson from Carl Hewitt","text":"For those who like to learn from videos, here is an interview/lesson from Carl Hewitt (he introduced the model): [VIDEO]"},{"id":31824461,"author":"TowerTall","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Are there senior devs who liked switching from static to dynamic typing?","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the actor model","quote":"","text":"Absolutely I would. Everyone has their opinion about Microsofts products, but the platform and frameworks that underpin them are rock solid, long-time battle-tested in production, and easy to work with as a developer. A .net stack comes with the comforting knowledge that this technology will never be made obsolete. It will undergo active development and will have support for many years to come. One day, long into the future, it will be super seeded by something else. When that happens, you know there will be a migration path because Microsoft has based their own business on it, and they need it for themselves, and all of their customers need it. .net does not go away tomorrow because something shinier showed up. That's nice to know. You will find that you will have a very stable and mature stack that is well integrated with Azure making deployment and testing easy. Was I to build a microservice application, I would not choose Kubernetes. I would choose the Microsoft Service Fabric Actor model over anything else that is out there. Service Fabric is Microsoft Secret Sause. It is the technology that underpins the Azure Platform itself. Service Fabric has a beautiful actor model that makes the development of cloud-based applications as simple as writing a single threaded console application. Service Fabric and Kubernetes: community comparison [1] The Actor Model [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22734762,"author":"ahasani","month":"2020-03","story":"Writing an OS in Rust: Async/Await","stance":"reference","why":"Hewitt argues goto is harmless","quote":"quoting Prof Carl Hewitt of Actor Model,","text":"What do you think about hierarchical state machine [1] ? >Has it already been through state X? Like everything else, logging >Will it come back to this state in the future? Mentioned in the comment before, there is a transition graph that you can do static analysis on, note that you might run into a halting problem >Is there a route from state Y to state Z? There is a transition graph >State machines are effectively goto writ large... quoting Prof Carl Hewitt of Actor Model, \"goto is harmless\"[2], function/procedure call is a goto, much like sending an named event while in particular state with/out parameters [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] note: edited for formatting"},{"id":10227150,"author":"noblethrasher","month":"2015-09","story":"Why WhatsApp Only Needs 50 Engineers for Its 900M Users","stance":"reference","why":"Carl Hewitt discussing the actor model he originated","quote":"let's not forget to give credit to Carl Hewitt who came up with actors in the first place","text":"Indeed, Joe Armstrong once said that he believes Erlang to be the only OO language according to Alan Kay's idea [1]. Also, let's not forget to give credit to Carl Hewitt who came up with actors in the first place [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JxAXlJEmNMg",1]]},"Q2aEzeMDHMA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an easy, clear intro to the DCT that finally made it click for several commenters.","q":{"x":"I finally understood DCT after watching","i":49128056,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49128056,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-07","story":"How JPEG works: Interactively explore JPEG's lossy compression methods","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made DCT finally click.","quote":"I finally understood DCT after watching","text":"There's a series of videos showing the algorithm: [LINK] I finally understood DCT after watching [VIDEO] And someone created a \"JPEG to PNG\" tool that \"finds the smoothest possible picture that encodes to the given JPEG file\": [LINK] . I tried it with some very terribly compressed images, and the outputs were a lot better."},{"id":40151155,"author":"itishappy","month":"2024-04","story":"VideoGigaGAN: Towards detail-rich video super-resolution","stance":"rec","why":"Worth looking into how this works.","quote":"it's definitely worth looking into the specifics of how this works, as it's quite impressive!","text":"It receives information about the spacial frequency content of the image. If you're unfamiliar, it's definitely worth looking into the specifics of how this works, as it's quite impressive! Here's a few relevant Wikipedia articles, and a Computerphile video: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38764236,"author":"boolemancer","month":"2023-12","story":"Technical Overview of AV1 Spec","stance":"rec","why":"Describes the concept in context of JPEG.","quote":"There is a good video[1] from the Computerphile channel describing the concept in the context of JPEG.","text":"There is a good video[1] from the Computerphile channel describing the concept in the context of JPEG. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34062642,"author":"skywal_l","month":"2022-12","story":"Video Codec in 100 lines of Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Nice, easy intro to DCT.","quote":"It's a nice ans easy intro to the subject.","text":"The Computerphile channel has a nice series of videos on JPEG and one focusing on DCT [0]. It's a nice ans easy intro to the subject. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24282212,"author":"walagran","month":"2020-08","story":"Discrete Cosine Transform in Video Compression – Explain Like I'm Five","stance":"rec","why":"Follow-up watch from JPEG series.","quote":"I would add Computerphile's Youtube Video as a watch after this","text":"Pretty cool! I would add Computerphile's Youtube Video as a watch after this: [VIDEO] . This is a part of their 4-video series on JPEG: [LINK]"},{"id":10181758,"author":"greggman","month":"2015-09","story":"The Pixel Factory","stance":"rec","why":"Explains JPEG in a way that worked for commenter.","quote":"For example this video explaining jpeg worked for me","text":"Honestly this presentation didn't work for me. I was amazed at the spectacle and a few things like perfect math world vs pixels and multi-sampler helped explain a few things but over all it was all over the place and didn't seem to get to many points or insights. It seems more like someone was asked to do a presentation, didn't have a solid idea of what to talk about, and so threw together a bunch of stuff until it filed their allotted time. I'm not trying to be discouraging. A great presentation can go a long way to making things understandable. For example this video explaining jpeg worked for me [VIDEO] My point is only that fancy graphics and animation are not enough. The presentation actually has to get it's points across. Maybe this one worked for everyone but me but it didn't really work for me. I don't really know what to suggest to make it better. Maybe it all made more sense live with more talking. There were a few \"bugs\" For example slide 22, bi-linear filtering, pixels can't be 2 colors so that slide made no sense. On the positive side I would love to know what software to use to make presentations easy. Yea, I get the author made his own but in general, making animation is hard work. Worse, once I've made them, even if they didn't end up being good for explaining I put so much work into making them that I can't justify starting over for something that works better. So, anything that would make that process not be so much work would be amazingly helpful."}],"r":[["PorfLSr3DDI",1],["Q1lL-hXO27Q",1],["LSxbc1IN9Gg",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["t1MqJPHxy6g",1],["iFTWM7HV2UI",1],["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1]]},"R2Aa4PivG0g":{"wy":"Recommended as a fantastic introduction to declarative and Datalog-based distributed systems and failure modeling.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching Peter Alvaro’s Strangeloop talk about creating Dedalus","i":19509999,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19509999,"author":"alexmingoia","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: Datalog as the only language for web programming, logic and database","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk about creating Dedalus matches the asker's train of thought.","quote":"I highly recommend watching Peter Alvaro’s Strangeloop talk about creating Dedalus","text":"Not anything I’d want to use. The closest is prolog. But at a higher level this is what Eve attempted to do. Eve was built using the semantics of Dedalus which is built from datalog. I highly recommend watching Peter Alvaro’s Strangeloop talk about creating Dedalus - it’s exactly the same train of thought you’re onto. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12822141,"author":"roller","month":"2016-10","story":"Eve: Programming designed for humans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic intro to Dedalus with less reading.","quote":"Peter Alvaro's 2015 Strange Loop talk was fantastic.","text":"For an introduction to Dedalus with less reading, Peter Alvaro's 2015 Strange Loop talk was fantastic. [VIDEO] --- Edit: Actually Chris Granger gave an Eve talk at the same conference. It's interesting to see how Eve has evolved since. [LINK]"},{"id":19425802,"author":"ComNik","month":"2019-03","story":"Introduction to Datalog","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended Alvaro talk for those interested in Datalog semantics.","quote":"almost any talk by Peter Alvaro might be as well.","text":"If model-theoretic semantics and the various ways to slice, dice, and extend Datalog are interesting to you, then almost any talk by Peter Alvaro might be as well. In particular: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18775636,"author":"iamwil","month":"2018-12","story":"Please do not attempt to simplify this code","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how restricting query language eases distributed reasoning.","quote":"I encourage you to check out Peter Alvaro's talk on Distributed Systems.","text":"Your language example, and the burger example seems flipped. I assume you prefer artisan burgers, which means you like no language restrictions. Badly chosen restrictions are not good, we can all agree. But what do good restrictions look like? Back in the day, programmers prided themselves on being able to do their own memory management, and bristled at the idea of a compiler or the runtime doing it for them. Now, that's the exception, as most of the time, we don't think about memory management in most languages we program in. As time marches on, we'll find more of these restrictions that we all eventually agree are good practices, and the next generation of programmers will take it as a given in programming. As a look beyond functional programming restrictions, I encourage you to check out Peter Alvaro's talk on Distributed Systems. Here, he talks about how queries over distributed systems is really hard to reason about, because time is now relative--there's no central clock to measure time. However, if we restrict ourselves to a language whose queries cannot express negation, a lot of the hard stuff about distributed systems go away. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16840969,"author":"svieira","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Thought-provoking look at failure modeling in distributed systems.","quote":"are thought-provoking looks at failure modeling in distributed systems.","text":"Also: I see what you mean: [VIDEO] and Applying Failure Testing Research @Netflix: [LINK] both by Peter Alvaro (second also includes Kolton Andrus) are thought-provoking looks at failure modeling in distributed systems."},{"id":10821543,"author":"jitl","month":"2016-01","story":"BOOM: Berkeley Orders of Magnitude – Declarative Languages And Systems","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to thinking behind declarative distributed systems like Bloom.","quote":"is a good introduction to the thinking behind some of these systems","text":"This Strangeloop talk by Peter Alvaro is a good introduction to the thinking behind some of these systems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21558616,"author":"hencq","month":"2019-11","story":"Tinyland","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source on Bloom.","quote":"","text":"Very interesting. I'm curious what you'll come up with. The description also reminds me of Eve [1][2], which is itself based on Bloom [3][4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17572657,"author":"justinmk","month":"2018-07","story":"Why We’re Building Flux, a New Data Scripting and Query Language","stance":"reference","why":"Makes case for datalog as starting point for a query language.","quote":"Dedalus/Bloom[1] makes a good case for why datalog is a good starting point for a data query language.","text":"The post doesn't mention datalog, but Dedalus/Bloom[1] makes a good case for why datalog is a good starting point for a data query language. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17049147,"author":"hammerandtongs","month":"2018-05","story":"Bloom Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Peter Alvaro's talk on Dedalus and Bloom lineage.","quote":"Here is Peter Alvaro's Strange Loop talk","text":"Of note Bloom is an ancestor of Eve. Bloom is a descendant of Datalog via Dedalus. \"Dedalus: Datalog in Time and Space\" [LINK] Here is Peter Alvaro's Strange Loop talk - [VIDEO] It was a shame that most people, seemingly the Eve devs as well, seemed to focus on making Eve a language for easy programming for the masses. Thats a huge wall of a problem with a limited funding slope. I think if there had been more upfront work and focus on the distributed programming/database possibilities, that Eve would have founded an actual community to push it along. Eve as a language for doing business logic against say Kubernetes? As a language to wire up ETL processes? I think it could have found a niche that would have made it grow in power before trying to take on the mass market. Just a thought."},{"id":16630838,"author":"iamwil","month":"2018-03","story":"Eve: Programming designed for humans","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"One of the more interesting parts (to me) about Eve wasn't the interactive programming or the literate programming, but that it included a database inside of the programming language. Most programming languages think of a database as a thing separate to it (I think Smalltalk is an exception). Hence, they needed language constructs to make it easier to talk to databases. As a result, Eve was inspired by the Bloom language being researched at Berkeley, which was in turn inspired by an old language called Datalog. In it, they take constraints of a declarative language (like SQL) and use it to make distributed computing a lot easier. \"Our main data-structure was now a tree of tables. Rather than one big top-level function, we switched to a pipeline of functions. Each function pulled data out of the global store using a datalog query, ran some computation and wrote data back. Having less nesting reduced the impact of lexical scope and cursor passing. Using datalog allowed normalising the data store, avoiding all the issues that came from hierarchical models. At this point we realised we weren't building a functional language anymore. Most of the programs were just datalog queries on normalised tables with a little scalar computation in the middle. We were familiar with Bloom and realised that it fit our needs much better than the functional pidgin we had built so far - no lexical scoping, no data-structures, no explicit ordering. In late March we began work on a Bloom interpreter."},{"id":13423894,"author":"vii","month":"2017-01","story":"RethinkDB Postmortem","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Slava (coffeemug) is very brave to introspect publicly and whenever I interacted with him, behaved with greatest integrity and kindness. Unfortunately, his hypothesis here that devtools are a tough market does not ring true. Making a business is always tough, making one that does something new is even tougher. There are many tools and database companies doing extraordinarily well, from Atlassian to Cloudera to Docker to Elastic. The difficulties that RethinkDB faced, as it pivoted from closed source MySQL DB backend, to open source independent database with a very cool update mechanism, were in my analysis much more mundane. Simply, RethinkDB was not easy. Here is a flavor of their thinking, when they rejected a REST HTTP interface: \"The RethinkDB query language is so simple, expressive, and pleasant, that trying to make it available over HTTP would be quite difficult and a disservice to users.\" Developers have a big advantage when making devtools in that they are their own target market. They are, but only at the start of the project before they become embroiled in it. Ironically this feeling of making it for oneself leads to a mistaken urge to thrust complexity onto the target. As an infra engineer myself, this is something I always try to keep in mind -- I worked on a modelling language at a large company and I credit its success to its absolutely straightforward and accessible syntax, lack of dependencies and straightforward integration into workflows. The temptation is a"},{"id":11800434,"author":"norswap","month":"2016-05","story":"Ask HN: What do you want to learn in 2016?","stance":"reference","why":"Pointer for deduction systems.","quote":"Deduction systems: see","text":"I'd be more interested by pointers for Javascript front-end principles, because I feel like I'm lacking some background to understand much of what I'm encountering, whereas there's quite a bit about machine learning (which I haven't checked, still I have good hope it covers the fundamentals). Deduction systems: see [VIDEO] for instance."}],"r":[["oyLBGkS5ICk",1],["dPK6t7echuA",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["5jdEG_ACXLw",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["x7drE24geUw",1],["ZSRHeXYDLko",1]]},"G0R09YzyuCI":{"wy":"Recommended as the best exposition of Tainter's theory, and used to explain slowing innovation, centralization and collapse risk.","q":{"x":"Spend some time watching Tainter's speech on complexity and collapse","i":23077254,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23077254,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2020-05","story":"Demand Nothing Less Than a Visionary Future","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tainter on complexity and collapse, with closing call for adult conversations","quote":"Spend some time watching Tainter's speech on complexity and collapse","text":"The best way to think about this is the classic Buckminster Fuller quote \"You never change things by fighting the existing reality. To change something, build a new model that makes the existing model obsolete.\" Spend some time watching Tainter's speech on complexity and collapse: [VIDEO] . Particularly the adult conversations we need to have at the end of the talk. Also try Kate Raworth's explanation of the 'three horizons' framework: [LINK]"},{"id":36841198,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-07","story":"How, Mathematically, We’re Fucked: Exponential growth on a finite planet","stance":"rec","why":"One of several recommended videos on collapse and overshoot","quote":"Why Societies Collapse And What it Means for Us - Joseph A. Tainter","text":"> Burn it all down and start over We won't be the ones doing that. > We're going to have to figure this sustainability thing out though It may be already late. I wish it wasn't so. I think we could have chance at repairing it ... if only we would do what's necessary. But we're not doing what's necessary, we never started even discussing it, and I'm starting to doubt that we'll ever do. We're talking only about climate change, but that's only one of the symptoms of the overshoot. Even people @ hacker news mostly don't have a clue. > we can't just ignore the problems We've been ignoring them for 50-70 years. The limits to growth / club of Rome was in 1972? So far ... we did nothing but ignored the problems. We may cross the 1.5C threshold in 5 years. Not in 2050 as previously expected, but in 5 years. And we're still pursuing economic growth. Have you noticed any evidence to the contrary lately? > it's going to be a big challenge, and things may get ugly I recommend few videos for you. I must warn you ... it may hamper your ability to enjoy parties. Collapse: The Only Realistic Scenario - Arthur Keller [LINK] How to Enjoy the End of the World - Dr. B. Sidney Smith [LINK] Arithmetic, Population and Energy - Dr. Albert A. Bartlett [LINK] Why Societies Collapse And What it Means for Us - Joseph A. Tainter [VIDEO] Revealing the Naked Emperor – Paris, 2° & Carbon Budgets - Kevin Anderson [LINK]"},{"id":15741635,"author":"monday_","month":"2017-11","story":"Theories of civilisational ‘collapse’ grow less convincing when scrutinised","stance":"rec","why":"Framework for studying social collapse","quote":"There is a whole framework for similar studies in social collapse - see this talk.","text":"The author's position seems to be that the very notion of civilizational collapse is flawed because people often come up with reductionist, poorly supported ways of explaining it. The unspoken implication here is that whoever brings up a threat of collapse in a political argument hasn't got a clue what he's talking about. Stephen Bannon referencing Gibbons work comes to mind, and, come think of it, that's probably what prompted the author to write this piece. Denying a historical phenomenon for political gain is as bad as selectively citing a two century old book to justify xenophobia. It is revealing that the author mentions Tainter's \"Collapse of Complex Societies\", and then dismisses it's meticulous definition of collapse as \"jargon\". Before coming up with this definition Tainter spent a whole chapter describing many, many examples of social collapse including the ones mentioned in the article. The definition arises from these examples and absolutely covers the collapse of Rome, Bronze Age and others. As for how we define and study the social collapse without arbitrarily designating some cultures as superior to others - a good example would be collapse of ecosystems. In this case you can have a sensible definition of complexity and energy intake and the collapse would be a sharp decline in both. There is a whole framework for similar studies in social collapse - see this talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13212591,"author":"jontas","month":"2016-12","story":"Are ideas getting harder to find? [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains slowing innovation using a century of patent data","quote":"This video [1] has a great explanation and exploration of why innovation seems to be slowing down.","text":"This video [1] has a great explanation and exploration of why innovation seems to be slowing down. He uses data coming from the patent system over a period of 100 years. The video touches on much of what is in this study and does a great job of explaining it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9326931,"author":"faizshah","month":"2015-04","story":"Is global collapse imminent? (2014) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Tainter presenting his study of societal collapse","quote":"he gives a great study on the nature of collapse with lots of examples","text":"This is incorrect, societies have collapsed regularly throughout history. A collapse doesn't have to be a total destruction of the society, it's most often a fast transition to a lower level of social complexity. One of my favorite books (and a book referenced by the link above) is \"The Collapse of Complex Societies\" by Joseph A Tainter, he gives a great study on the nature of collapse with lots of examples (note, some of the examples are a little dated and dubious like the Ik). He also gave a few presentations on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32530852,"author":"brakmic","month":"2022-08","story":"The Collapse of Complex Societies (Book Review)","stance":"reference","why":"Lists Tainter talk alongside another","quote":"Collapse of Complex Societies by Dr. Joseph Tainter","text":"Collapse of Complex Societies by Dr. Joseph Tainter: [VIDEO] Joseph Tainter on The Dynamics of the Collapse of Human Civilization: [LINK]"},{"id":27338274,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-05","story":"James Bond Unmasked (1968)","stance":"reference","why":"Passing example in discussion of lecture lengths","quote":"","text":"Meandering down this diversion a bit... First, yes, I generally agree with you. What I've noticed listening to a lot of lectures over the past few years are typically breaks at roughly: 90s, 5m, 10m, that 20--30m spot, 45--60m, 90m, and very occasionally a longer session, though I'd argue that 2h is about the maximum which can be sustained in a single session. The shortest are just teasers, often commercial broadcast. They're not informative but more advisory , and really only advertisements for some additional content, which is the medium's strength.[1] A 4--10m segment is a brief introduction to a topic, beginning with about the span of a noncommercial broadcast, and including the typical length of a quick YouTube video. You can actually cover quite a bit of the basics of a topic, or mesh together a number of topics into once related thread. At 20m you're hitting one of the natural spans of human focus and the point at which attention starts to wander without some form of internal break or diversion. Longer presentations are often a fusion of 2--3 15--20 minute segments. I note that many well-structured podcasts aim for about this mark (\"History of Philosophy\" by Peter Adamson comes to mind.) If you're aiming to fill a 30 minute timeslot, 20--25m allows a bit of additional padding on either side for other filler as well. Lecture presentations tend to run variously about 20, 30, 45, 50, or a full 60 minutes. Typically there's a Q&A session, and in an academic context, an int"},{"id":13919638,"author":"mythrwy","month":"2017-03","story":"David Rockefeller has died","stance":"reference","why":"Tainter on troubles of centralization","quote":"Joseph Tainter I think hits the troubles with centralization right on the head.","text":"Except local elites have less real power. Corruption or \"real motives\" of a global elite aside, I look at it as the difference between monolithic code bases and well designed separation of concerns. One is less prone to catastrophic failure and easier to repair when failure does occur. Joseph Tainter I think hits the troubles with centralization right on the head. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10834057,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2016-01","story":"The Refragmentation","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture claims breakthroughs get costlier over time","quote":"","text":">Not everyone who gets rich now does it by creating wealth, certainly. But a significant number do, and the Baumol Effect means all their peers get dragged along too. [23] And as long as it's possible to get rich by creating wealth, the default tendency will be for economic inequality to increase My question is: significantly more wealth seems to be generated by big corporations by means of financial 'instruments' rather than by value creation; is that correct? Also this lecture [1] says that scientific and technological breatkhroughs are much harder to achieve these days - because all the shallow fruit is already taken and it costs more to digg deep. Now doesn't that make value creation harder for the coming decades? (for example the PC revolution was based on breakthroughs in physics/semiconductors/electronics - these will be harder and harder to achieve) So it is hard to tell which one will dominate for the near future: value extraction by means of financial trickery (my guess is that this makes society more hierarchical, and the top of the hierarchy will try to enforce equal standing for the lower ranks) or the possibility of real value creation (this one would create a chance for a wider audience) ? [1] Collapse of Complex Societies by Dr. Joseph Tainter [VIDEO]"},{"id":10766526,"author":"Litost","month":"2015-12","story":"99% of Costa Rica's electricity came from renewable energy in 2015","stance":"reference","why":"Tainter's thesis on complexity and collapse","quote":"Complexity has killed off pretty much every major society prior to ours.","text":"Much as i don't want to cause any further depression in an already quite depressing thread, but if you follow Joseph Tainters work on The Collapse of Complex Societies. [VIDEO] a) Complexity has killed off pretty much every major society prior to ours. b) People's innate desire to problem solve increases complexity. c) Cheap energy has enabled this process. So there's an argument, an unlimited source of free energy would actually be a major problem because 1) It would enable more complexity, which is already pretty unmanagable. 2) You've still the problem of out of control resource usage."}],"r":[["3Y2weLDiUWw",1],["-cEn_83zRFw",1],["OoJsPvmFixU",1],["UcN7SGGoCNI",1],["gSPNRu4ZPvE",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["o0VrZPBskpg",1],["sI1C9DyIi_8",1],["5WPB2u8EzL8",1],["qPb_0JZ6-Rc",1],["H9B5mYfBPlY",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1]]},"FssULNGSZIA":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a showcase of astonishing drumming and music that gives chills.","q":{"x":"here's Danny Carey, quite possibly the greatest living drummer","i":36838137,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36838137,"author":"leeoniya","month":"2023-07","story":"What Happened the Night an Audience Member Filled in for Keith Moon (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing drumming by Danny Carey","quote":"here's Danny Carey, quite possibly the greatest living drummer","text":"also [LINK] but if you want to see something really mind blowing, here's Danny Carey, quite possibly the greatest living drummer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36052526,"author":"wsc981","month":"2023-05","story":"Microtiming in Metallica's “Master of Puppets”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Danny Carey's impressive drumming on Pneuma","quote":"I am not a drummer, but to me this is super impressive.","text":"I am not sure what you mean with AI level drumming, but what about Tool’s Danny Carey though? I am not a drummer, but to me this is super impressive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49103094,"author":"rented_mule","month":"2026-07","story":"Learning Musical Multitasking","stance":"rec","why":"Live performance showing how complicated drumming can be","quote":"Watch his knees to see what he's doing with pedals.","text":"As a fan of drumming, you're probably one of the millions who have seen this. But for anybody who hasn't, here's a fun video showing how complicated drumming can be with a live performance by someone whose nickname is \"The Octopus\". While the entire video is fun, the link is cued up to a place where there's a lot going on, but it's slow enough that you can kind of see what he's doing. Watch his knees to see what he's doing with pedals. Notice that his hands seem to be going in cycles, but there are subtle variations in the cycles. At 8:54, strobe lights start up - that can't make it simpler to keep this focus. What he's doing looks essentially impossible to me, but I'm well aware that I don't know enough to understand just how insanely difficult it really is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31554918,"author":"wsc981","month":"2022-05","story":"Listening to songs can cause a physiological response known as “frisson”","stance":"rec","why":"Tool song that gives a supernatural, chills-inducing feeling","quote":"Pneuma is one of those great Tool songs","text":"I'm surprised I didn't see any mention of Tool in here, while many Tool songs can give this weird, supernatural, out of this world, feeling, at least for me. Pneuma is one of those great Tool songs: [VIDEO] And it's not just the sounds, the lyrics themselves help invoke this feeling as well."},{"id":29187663,"author":"prawn","month":"2021-11","story":"Making the dislike count private across YouTube","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I laugh at the top comment on this video every time","text":"The comments used to be horrific but on a lot of videos now, they're actually quite entertaining. I laugh at the top comment on this video every time: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1],["RCCigccBzIU",1],["AxFzf6yIfcc",1],["QeLu_yyz3tc",1],["GnO7D5UaDig",1],["pXOK-ZVJMaU",1]]},"5AqbCQuK0gM":{"wy":"HN points to it as a rare side-by-side of the TypeScript and Dart designers, often to show how the two approaches to improving JavaScript differed.","q":{"x":"Debate (almost 10 years ago) TypeScript vs. Dart about the best way to improve JavaScript","i":33049759,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29515666,"author":"okareaman","month":"2021-12","story":"Interview with Anders Hejlsberg on Delphi (1995)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Anders explains to Lars why TypeScript will win.","quote":"Another really great video is Anders and Lars Bak around the time Typescript and Dart came out","text":"Another really great video is Anders and Lars Bak around the time Typescript and Dart came out (about the same time.) In this video Anders explains to Lars why TypeScript will win the race to fix JavaScript's drawbacks. Lars was really happy with their Dart VM (as he should be) and didn't really want to hear how TypeScript would prevail. The thing about Anders is that he's usually right and he was. Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak: TypeScript, JavaScript, and Dart [VIDEO]"},{"id":30701490,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2022-03","story":"Flutter is better than React Native in all the ways that don’t matter","stance":"rec","why":"Informative debate between TypeScript and Dart VM designers.","quote":"this is an informative video of a debate between the designer of TypeScript and the designer of the Dart VM","text":"If you're curious, this is an informative video of a debate between the designer of TypeScript and the designer of the Dart VM about which was the better approach. Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak: TypeScript, JavaScript, and Dart [VIDEO]"},{"id":30428189,"author":"dundarious","month":"2022-02","story":"Flutter is the most popular cross-platform mobile SDK","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting interview showing Dart lead's frustration at TypeScript's rise.","quote":"I always found this interview with both Typescript and Dart leads to be interesting.","text":"This is really some armchair psychoanalysis on my part, totally not worth anything in the way of intellectual attention, but I always found this interview with both Typescript and Dart leads to be interesting. [VIDEO] I can't help but infer that Lars is quite often a little bit frustrated with the emergence of Typescript. But on top of it being embarrassing for me to imagine up such personal rivalries based on hardly anything, you could view the interview as Dart-hostile given the interviewer."},{"id":9519411,"author":"brodo","month":"2015-05","story":"Dart's Macro Language","stance":"rec","why":"Discussion comparing TypeScript and Dart.","quote":"There is also a nice discussion about TypeScript and Dart here","text":"When there is no Dart VM in the browser I honestly don't see any advantage over TypeScript. Two VMs inside each other (with two garbage collectors) will always be slower and use more memory than just one. Additionally, you always need to ship the runtime with all your scripts. You also have to use FFI to interact with Javascript. All the advantages over plain JS you mentioned above are also present in TypeSctipt (or not needed). There is also a nice discussion about TypeScript and Dart here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41070159,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2024-07","story":"Node.js adds experimental support for TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of past competition between TypeScript and Dart.","quote":"","text":"> There was no real competition There was: Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak: TypeScript, JavaScript, and Dart [VIDEO] (2013). Summary: [LINK] / [LINK]"},{"id":33049759,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2022-10","story":"Ten Years of TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Decade-old debate on how best to improve JavaScript.","quote":"Debate (almost 10 years ago) TypeScript vs. Dart about the best way to improve JavaScript","text":"Debate (almost 10 years ago) TypeScript vs. Dart about the best way to improve JavaScript Anders Hejlsberg (Microsoft) and Lars Bak (Google): TypeScript, JavaScript, and Dart [VIDEO]"},{"id":10871994,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2016-01","story":"Why I'm joining the Dart Team","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts the two languages' approaches to safety, per their authors.","quote":"The difference in the two approaches is discussed here by the two main authors Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak respectively [1].","text":"> trying to justify why something is still relevant or important in a company roadmap While I know what you mean, I didn't read the article that way at all. The author isn't a marketing type or someone high up in the company, but a programmer making a calculation about Dart and passing on his observation to his friends, who don't see that situation the way he does. Dart has received a lot of bad press because some Dart engineers made the mistake of saying it would replace JavaScript. I didn't get involved in defending JavaScript from Dart and wrote a bunch of Dart, and I noticed I was about twice as productive. I also learned along the way that Google was heavily invested in something called Google Web Toolkit, a Java based system for writing web apps. I figured Google was looking to replace that with Dart so it would always have a place, and it looks like I was right. The code that powers the engine that brings in 17 billion dollars a year is nothing to dismiss, like many do of Dart, if in fact they are converting GWT to Dart, and it sounds like they are. TypeScript is one way to add safety, Dart is another. The difference in the two approaches is discussed here by the two main authors Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak respectively [1]. What I found compelling about Dart was the elimination of \"monkey patching\" that Lars talks about. I was also drawn to try TypeScript and found it a great tool as well. So does Google as they are now using TypeScript in Angular 2.0. Microsoft and"},{"id":9792358,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2015-06","story":"Why We Need WebAssembly: An Interview with Brendan Eich","stance":"reference","why":"Video made the commenter doubt Dart; Anders made the better case.","quote":"I felt Anders made a better case for TypeScript and Lars was feeling a bit threatened","text":"It was very unfortunate that the first time that most heard about Dart was through a leaked memo from Google predicting Dart would replace JavaScript [ [LINK] ]. It created an atmosphere of hostility towards Dart that I'm sure the developers didn't deserve. I now understand your objections and I'm sorry I lumped you in with the general hostility. My own doubts about Dart after using it for a commercial app, began after watching this video [ [VIDEO] ] where Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak discuss Dart and TypeScript. I felt Anders made a better case for TypeScript and Lars was feeling a bit threatened, which confirmed for me that Anders was right. Considering where Dart and TypeScript are today, I made the right choice moving to TypeScript. The other problem that I felt wasn't being addressed was that the Dart2js output and JavaScript interop needed a lot of improvement. Since it became clear that the DartVM was not landing in other browsers, this should have been a top priority and it wasn't. Now it is a priority, but it's not clear to me how many resources Google is giving to it. I'm very glad that the path is much clearer these days and thank you for your efforts to sort these things out."},{"id":8503591,"author":"ceronman","month":"2014-10","story":"AtScript Primer","stance":"reference","why":"Hejlsberg and Bak discuss runtime type checking and are unconvinced.","quote":"In this interview [1] Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak (from TypeScript and Dart) discuss the idea of runtime type checking.","text":"I really like the idea of static type checking, but I'm not convinced about the usefulness of runtime checking. It will introduce a performance penalty and it will go against duck typing, which is one of the coolest features of languages like JS. It seems that this project is trying to build something in between of TypeScript and Dart: they want the optional runtime type checking of Dart vs the good interoperability with JS of TypeScript. In this interview [1] Anders Hejlsberg and Lars Bak (from TypeScript and Dart) discuss the idea of runtime type checking. They don't seem very convinced of the idea either. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AxnotgLql0k",1],["ZTC_RxWN_xo",1],["r9eQth4Q5jg",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["oYk8CKH7OhE",1],["nWTvXbQHwWs",1],["yCd3CzGSte8",1],["WXuK6gekU1Y",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["kFT54hO1X8M",1]]},"elvOZm0d4H0":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a clear introduction to different sizes of infinity that non-mathematicians can follow.","q":{"x":"Numberphile did a pretty good introductory video to this if you want to learn more","i":17300643,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":8718198,"author":"japhyr","month":"2014-12","story":"Almost all numbers contain the digit “3” [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gave students a sense of wonder about higher-level math.","quote":"I really appreciate the way he presents mathematical concepts.","text":"I teach high school math and science. I had a group of students do a mini-project around the question, \"Is there anything bigger than infinity?\" The core of the project was students watching this numberphile video [0], and recreating his reasoning on their own. That project helped them reason about higher-level math in a way they never thought themselves capable. The idea of \"magic\" never came up, but they certainly spoke about their sense of wonder at higher-level math. I really appreciate the way he presents mathematical concepts. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":27849375,"author":"twelfthnight","month":"2021-07","story":"How many real numbers exist? New proof moves closer to an answer","stance":"rec","why":"Clearer explanation of the proof background.","quote":"Here's a video explaining it little better [2].","text":"Here's some background on the proof [1]. Here's a video explaining it little better [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17300643,"author":"cyphar","month":"2018-06","story":"Are real numbers really real?","stance":"rec","why":"Introductory explanation of why reals outnumber integers.","quote":"Numberphile did a pretty good introductory video to this if you want to learn more","text":"Yes, because if there were such a list then you could assign a 1-to-1 mapping (by list index) of each number between 0 and 1 to a whole number which would prove they have the same size. If it is impossible to have a complete list of all real numbers between 0 and 1 then there cannot be such a mapping, and thus you'll \"run out\" of integers before you've counted all of the reals (more correctly you're showing that the size must be larger because if it was smaller or of equal size it could fit in such a list). Numberphile did a pretty good introductory video to this if you want to learn more: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":7363061,"author":"japhyr","month":"2014-03","story":"LHC Physics Center bans Powerpoint, switches to whiteboard-only forums","stance":"rec","why":"Explains bigger infinities in a way students could re-derive.","quote":"My non-math-inclined students were able to follow it by watching several times","text":"I like the numberphile series on youtube. I showed one in class where he explains how some infinities are bigger than others. My non-math-inclined students were able to follow it by watching several times, because they could see the entire process. They re-created the process themselves, rather than just trying to digest an explanation of an already-completed proof/ demonstration. Infinity is bigger than you think [VIDEO]"},{"id":31997554,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2022-07","story":"Absurd Trolley Problems","stance":"reference","why":"Diagonal argument showing some infinities are larger.","quote":"","text":"> There's no such thing as a bigger inifinite? Well, that is where the fun starts. Take every natural number, assign a unique real between 0 and 1 to it. Put all of them under each other. Now take the 1st digit of the first number, 2nd of the 2nd and so on, set it to a different digit than the one you took. Now you have a new, unique, real (Because it differs in at least one digit with every other real taken so far) but no more natural to assign it to. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RCRTgtpC-Go",1],["mOJlg8g8_yw",1],["vyenmLqJQjs",1],["YuIIjLr6vUA",1],["7wC42HgLA4k",1],["yYAw79386WI",1]]},"D6EPuUdIC1E":{"wy":"HN points people to it as a nuanced, medically informed take whenever Wim Hof breathing or cold plunges come up.","q":{"x":"Here is an amazing summary on the research of a sceptical doctor","i":23277720,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":31037128,"author":"tarsinge","month":"2022-04","story":"5-min breathing workout lowers blood pressure as much as exercise, drugs (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Medical professional explains what happens during Wim Hof breathing","quote":"Here is good medical explanation of what happens when practicing the WMH (by a medical professional)","text":"The article is about IMST, which is strictly about muscle strengthening. This is maybe a side effect of the Wim Hoff Method, but the WHM also has other purposes because the main mechanism is hyper ventilation and breath holding. Here is good medical explanation of what happens when practicing the WMH (by a medical professional): [VIDEO] (summary at 33:46)."},{"id":30428272,"author":"mettamage","month":"2022-02","story":"Can you warm yourself with your mind?","stance":"rec","why":"Good medical analysis of the Wim Hof method","quote":"A good medical YT vid on the WHM [1].","text":"Disclaimer: I've met Wim and was one of his guinea pigs in this experiment [2]. The best proof I have for that is that I've claimed this for years on HN ;-) We trained for 4 days in Poland. The hardest challenge I did was climbing up a mountain in my shorts + shoes + socks for 2.5 hours at -7 degrees. I did not have frostbite in the end, none of us did. We did the Harlem Shake (that was a thing back then). I also asked a ton of questions to the researchers back then. From what I've read, g-tummo is very likely not the Wim Hof Method. I've never trained with a monk claiming to know g-tummo. The son of Wim Hof loudly told me \"The Wim Hof Method is not Tummo!\" (almost a direct quote from memory, probably paraphrased). So I'd argue that back in 2014 or whenever it was, they were of the opinion it was something else. I'd be willing to bet they've never met a g-tummo monk either :) A good medical YT vid on the WHM [1]. From my experiential point of view: the WHM doesn't warm your body, you simply don't get cold, nor do you feel cold (you're too high on adrenaline to have any normal sense of temperature). Sometimes I did feel a warmth boost, but that's not the main \"working thing\" behind his method. I use it whenever I need an adrenaline boost, or don't want to feel cold. When is it handy or fun to get an adrenaline boost on purpose (my experience)? 1. When you have a headache (free painkiller when nothing is lying around) 2. When you're at a party (no drugs needed, the WHM is a dru"},{"id":24773241,"author":"skosch","month":"2020-10","story":"Wim Hof’s teachings about breath work and the health benefits of cold plunges","stance":"rec","why":"Reviews literature on cold exposure and breathing effects","quote":"can recommend the Medlife Crisis episode [0]","text":"I haven't listened to them but can recommend the Medlife Crisis episode [0], in which he reviews the scientific literature around the effects of cold exposure, blood oxygenation/acidity from hyper-/hypoventilation, and related topics. He comes to the conclusion that Wim Hof is likely a genetic outlier, but that breathing techniques can have real physiological effects on things like pain perception, and that cold exposure likely has a bunch of real benefits – but also that placebo may play a big role (which is nevertheless a very real effect). [0] [VIDEO] (timestamped to the conclusions)"},{"id":24775110,"author":"mettamage","month":"2020-10","story":"Wim Hof’s teachings about breath work and the health benefits of cold plunges","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced take on the Wim Hof method","quote":"Medlife crisis also did a good nuanced video on it [2].","text":"I'm sorry Rubatuga, I flagged you. To be fair: I'm not having the best of days, so I might be a bit quicker to flag, but I find this behavior on HN unacceptable. You make an overgeneralized claim (\"this is all \") and give no evidence. There is an extreme amount of evidence on the contrary that the WHM has a lot of novel scientific elements to it. See [1] as an example. Medlife crisis also did a good nuanced video on it [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23277720,"author":"mettamage","month":"2020-05","story":"Breathing habits are related to physical and mental health","stance":"rec","why":"Sceptical doctor's in-depth summary of the research","quote":"Here is an amazing summary on the research of a sceptical doctor","text":"There is already a lot of research. I have some comments about it in my whole HN history for if you're truly curious. Go to Google Scholar and look it up. I just re-edited my whole thing (behind a laptop now). For other HN people I did mention pre-edit: \"Wim is crazy. The people studying him aren’t.\" Enough research has been done, as far as I'm concerned it's a case closed. The WHM does things that we couldn't do before (e.g. be more resistant against the cold). Now we can, more research is needed to fully understand it. For people who dismiss it, they simply haven't taken the time to watch some doctor explain it on YouTube in-depth. And I get that, but then don't say it's BS, simply say you're sceptical. I'm the first person to burn occult things to the ground as I hate nonsense. This seems occult, but it isn't. It doesn't help that Wim acts like a guru, but what he does can be rooted in science. He roots it in science by asking scientists to research him. From his perspective that's all he can do. Here is an amazing summary on the research of a sceptical doctor: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29187734,"author":"penjelly","month":"2021-11","story":"How my genius roommate changed my perspective","stance":"reference","why":"Doctor's medical opinion on the Wim Hof method","quote":"","text":"doctors opinion: [VIDEO] personal anecdote: just do the breathing once or twice and youll feel it doing something. i find the effect very useful for eliminating brain fog and impulse control, also relieves my tired eyes, and makes me want to crack my knuckles. to me its like a rapid meditation but more potent. its def not self help focused on alpha males. the breathing imo is the most effective single thing you can do from his 3 things (meditation, breathing exercises, cold exposure). This isnt new btw. the breathing is based on pranayama and people have experimented with blood oxygen/CO2 levels for performance forever. see: apnea training & freedivers."},{"id":27255363,"author":"mettamage","month":"2021-05","story":"Ask HN: What book will significantly improve your life if you apply it?","stance":"reference","why":"Doctor's opinion on the method as a caution","quote":"Doctor’s opinion","text":"Not a book but can be learned via YouTube. The Wim Hof Method. Benefit: never feel unhappy because it’s cold ever again. Much less relevant benefit: you can now get free drugs at parties (it produces a lot of adrenaline when you do it, more than peak bungeejumping [1]). Or as a temporary pain killer, but you need to keep doing it. Warning: do your own research [2]. Do not use it while diving (people died). It is not a free diving technique, no matter what anyone says. Train with someone watching you. Also consult your doctor before doing this, erring on the safe side here. Disclaimer: Msc. Computer Science (aka not a doctor). I do know the WHM since 2014 and have done crazy trials with the legend himself. I checked some YouTube courses and they teach the fundamentals needed. 1. See their PNAS publication on WHM. 2. Doctor’s opinion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23985020,"author":"throwaway9d0291","month":"2020-07","story":"One hour of slow breathing changed my life","stance":"reference","why":"UK cardiologist reviews Wim Hof method science","quote":"run by a practicing cardiologist in the UK, did a fairly long video reviewing the Wim Hof method and the science surrounding it","text":"The YouTube channel Medlife Crisis, run by a practicing cardiologist in the UK, did a fairly long video reviewing the Wim Hof method and the science surrounding it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21507739,"author":"erentz","month":"2019-11","story":"Meditation reduced an opioid dose needed to ease chronic pain by 75%","stance":"reference","why":"Claim that Hof is a physiological outlier","quote":"Wim Hof is not a normal human","text":"Wim Hof is not a normal human, it’s not clear that there is anything about his “method” so much as that he has remarkable physiology. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["kBdfcR-8hEY",1],["3Ea3pkTCYx4",1],["rgaTLrZGlk0",1]]},"IUDTlvagjJA":{"wy":"It is the classic demo HN people point to for showing positional 3D audio working on ordinary headphones.","q":{"x":"try this 3d demo. And don't forget to close your eyes.","i":7426459,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":27544476,"author":"aaomidi","month":"2021-06","story":"Spatial Audio","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of binaural audio adding a dimension to sound.","quote":"Give this a try with any normal headphone","text":"Because it adds another dimension to the sound you're experiencing and gives you a whole new appreciation for the medium. This combined with binaural audio should be the future of audio storytelling. Give this a try with any normal headphone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12005918,"author":"kuschku","month":"2016-06","story":"The Daredevil Camera","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how well sound sources can be located from a recording.","quote":"Listen to this with headphones","text":"> I wonder how much people can see in the dark through listening to sound sources by moving their heads for motion parallax. Listen to this with headphones: [VIDEO] It’s amazing how much you can geolocate just in even a recording."},{"id":10521174,"author":"oberstein","month":"2015-11","story":"24/192 music downloads make no sense","stance":"rec","why":"Classic recording where you hear someone moving around, on cheap headphones.","quote":"recording that works with the cheapest of headphones","text":"Here's a classic \"OMG I can hear him moving around\" recording that works with the cheapest of headphones: [VIDEO] Most of the audio experience quality comes out of the production phase, and artists can put as much or little effort into that as possible and consider several mediums of listening (headphones, TV, surround, concert, vinyl) and make tradeoffs for the medium's particular experience. I don't know about double blind trials but people do tests on their own. It's further complicated though because the hardware you use could be optimized for certain types of music, e.g. have a read through [LINK]"},{"id":7426459,"author":"deletes","month":"2014-03","story":"Sony announces Project Morpheus, a virtual reality headset coming to PS4","stance":"rec","why":"3D audio demo to try on ordinary stereo headphones.","quote":"try this 3d demo. And don't forget to close your eyes.","text":"60 Virtual speakers What does that even mean? It sounds like clever marketing talk. I bet they have just regular stereo and software. Here, take a pair of good stereo headphones and try this 3d demo. And don't forget to close your eyes. Virtual barber shop: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38999855,"author":"antongribok","month":"2024-01","story":"He spent his life building a $1M stereo","stance":"reference","why":"Binaural barbershop demo recalled in a stereo discussion.","quote":"(Use headphones and close your eyes.)","text":"Reading this for some reason reminded me of the Virtual Barber Shop: [VIDEO] (Use headphones and close your eyes.)"},{"id":34630355,"author":"ceedan","month":"2023-02","story":"Play Counter Strike 1.6, with full multiplayer, in the browser","stance":"reference","why":"Used to test directional audio on headsets.","quote":"I tested them all with this video -","text":"Surround sound was unnecessary. As long as you had stereo headphones you were fine. I had cheap headphones, expensive headphones and even won headphones at LAN events. Directional audio was never an issue on any headset I'd played with. I tested them all with this video - [VIDEO] The only headphone issue I had in my decades of playing CS were with open ear headphones (Sennheiser HD555) that reduced the punchiness of bass and made footsteps more difficult to hear in CSS which was a terrible game with terrible audio."},{"id":32885847,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2022-09","story":"Why do all these 20-somethings have closed captions turned on?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo that sounds incredible on cheap headphones.","quote":"sounds incredible on my cheap headphones","text":"For real enthusiasts, also X.Y.Z, adding ceiling/bounce speakers for vertical spatial audio. The real \"stuck so far behind\" that I don't get is why we use speaker arrays at all -- instead of binaural stereo tech. That virtual haircut demo, apparently from the 90s,[0] sounds incredible on my cheap headphones, yet the technology seems to have gone nowhere since. Is there even spatial audio in VR setups? If the difficulty is in simulating the head/ears, then existing spatial recordings can at least be converted using a binaural microphone in a surround sound setup, no? Though, I guess with headphones any visceral sensation of bass is still lost, so you'd need to keep the subwoofer or a seat shaker. 0 [VIDEO]"},{"id":27188547,"author":"Wowfunhappy","month":"2021-05","story":"Apple Music Announces Spatial Audio and Lossless Audio","stance":"reference","why":"Shows spatial effect working on ordinary earbuds without head tracking.","quote":"the effect on my run-of-the-mill earbuds is still incredible","text":"Does anyone happen to know of a non-headphone-specific solution for this, which actually does a good job? It doesn't need to work when I move my head, neither does the Virtual Barbershop[1] and yet the effect on my run-of-the-mill earbuds is still incredible. I'm open to little hardware boxes, software that modifies my computer's audio as it plays, a filter for ffmpeg, etc, as long as I can use whatever headphones I want and the spatial effect is high quality. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27206196,"author":"kuschku","month":"2021-05","story":"AirPods Max and AirPods Pro Don't Support Apple Music Lossless, Apple Confirms","stance":"reference","why":"Typical demo of HRTF-based positional audio over headphones.","quote":"The typical demo for this functionality is","text":"Headphones with a proper HRTF preserve that perfectly fine better than any actual speaker setup ever can. The typical demo for this functionality is [VIDEO]"},{"id":26775128,"author":"The_rationalist","month":"2021-04","story":"Surround Sound in Headphones","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as state of the art 3D audio from years ago.","quote":"It's been 14 years since the last state of the art 3D audio experience","text":"It's been 14 years since the last state of the art 3D audio experience: [VIDEO] has anybody done as good since then ? What's this Cetera algorithm ?"},{"id":23581292,"author":"Stevvo","month":"2020-06","story":"MakeSpace – Virtual Spatial Chat","stance":"reference","why":"Example of convincing positional audio using HRTF.","quote":"For more convincing positional audio you can use a HRTF;","text":"It's a somewhat limited approach. For more convincing positional audio you can use a HRTF; [LINK] Example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11879716,"author":"speeder","month":"2016-06","story":"USB-C and Lightning headphones aren't great news for everyone","stance":"reference","why":"Positional audio demo in a discussion of hardware audio processing.","quote":"","text":"Dedicated soundcard, and drivers with direct access to hardware have nothing to do with audio professionals. It is about processors on the card (that back then had about half of the amount of transistors that the PC main CPU had, so they could pack quite a punch in processing power) that could do realtime audio calculations. I am not talking just about \"effects\" from the audio professional sense, I am talking about realtime creation of audio according to the scene geometry and stuff happening, for example A3D could calculate actual reflections, not just simulated reverb/echo. If you want to see an example of this in real life: go near a window that has a barrier in front of it of some kind, in a relatively enclosed space. Then wait for some noise to happen outside, in the direction opposite to the window. Suppose you was facing \"forward\", the window (and the wall beyond it) is to your left. Then an airplane passes by your right. The naive way to do it (that is what most games today do without a soundcard to do it for them), is play sound from the right speaker (be it in a 5.1 or in the right speaker of a headphone), or to simulate it is \"outside\", play it muffled. The correct way to do it, is play from the left, because it reflected on the wall, and came inside through the window. Vortex soundcards for example, made by Aureal, could calculate that (back then, only one reflection, so sequences of reflections that could go around corners didn't worked, but back then was 1996, t"},{"id":6635992,"author":"garethadams","month":"2013-10","story":"Songs of Diridum: Pushing the Web Audio API to Its Limits","stance":"reference","why":"Classic binaural demo of positional audio over headphones.","quote":"I remember how much I was wowed by the binaural virtual barbershop[1]","text":"I like this! A great demo of new standards working in browsers. As far as 3D audio goes it would be amazing to see an example which delayed audio to one ear based on the different distances from each ear to the source of the sound. This example just pans the audio - which is still a great effect, but I remember how much I was wowed by the binaural virtual barbershop[1] [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WmId2ZP3h0c",1]]},"PIPjmmmh_os":{"wy":"HN commenters call it an entertaining, complementary philosophical take on surveillance, with a funny story about jamming secret police.","q":{"x":"I think Slavoj Žižek and Paul Holdengräber on \"Surveillance and whistleblowers\" is worth a watch.","i":9201929,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":12498001,"author":"RodericDay","month":"2016-09","story":"Google’s become an obsessive stalker","stance":"rec","why":"Zizek talk on surveillance, a fun watch.","quote":"Fun watch imo.","text":"Slavoj Zizek had a great talk with a talk show host from the Netherlands precisely about this matter. [VIDEO] Fun watch imo. There's also another session between them two."},{"id":9201929,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2015-03","story":"Reasons not to use Facebook","stance":"rec","why":"Complementary perspective on surveillance from Zizek.","quote":"I think Slavoj Žižek and Paul Holdengräber on \"Surveillance and whistleblowers\" is worth a watch.","text":"As a complimentary perspective: For those who haven't seen it, I think Slavoj Žižek and Paul Holdengräber on \"Surveillance and whistleblowers\" is worth a watch. The part about surveillance starts at 21:14 so this link will take you there [VIDEO] Žižek: \"The most dangerous unfreedom is the unfreedom you don't even experience, as such. If some of you are feminists ... every good feminist will tell you the first step of feminism is just to realise your situation of oppression... We need something similar here. That's the first point. The second point ... I'm a little bit sceptic about this paranoia 'ooh we are totally controlled' and so on and so on, I think we shouldn't take this too seriously. And this doesn't make the situation any easier if anything it makes it more... it [paranoia] is justified, but we should be aware, again we go in to metaphorics again the NSA the Big Eye knows everything, again the mega-machinery is extremely stupid, they don't know what they know. Do you remember, this is a very superficial anecdote ... a guy in LA was Googling the ways to kill your wife something like that, a couple days afterward the FBI brutally entered his house arrested him blah blah blah. Why? Because Google reported this to FBI. But do you know what was the result, this guy was a was one of the you know all the [TV] series CIS / police investigation, we was simply writing a scenario and wanted to check you know, and you have this again and again or form China they told me a wonde"},{"id":8414901,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2014-10","story":"Stop Australia's Data Retention Bill","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining Zizek and Holdengraber conversation on surveillance and whistleblowers.","quote":"is quite entertaining","text":"At this stage it has become apparent that opposing surveillance doesn't appear to be helping. It's pretty clear where this is headed, and it seems to me the only way out of it is through it and out the other side, whatever that might look like. My position on surveillance is now, and has been for a few months, what Slavoj Žižek said: \"Yeah but I don't care if we are watched. Here I preach arrogance. So what, let the big 'other' watch me maybe he will learn something and be less stupid, you know, this is not my problem.\" If you haven't seen it this video[1] of Slavoj Žižek and Paul Holdengräber on \"Surveillance and whistleblowers\" at the International Authors' Stage, published earlier this year, is quite entertaining. The quote above is at 35:54. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7993488,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2014-07","story":"Jamming XKeyScore","stance":"rec","why":"Zizek tells a funny story about dissidents jamming the secret police.","quote":"Even if you don't like Slavoj Žižek, he has a funny story related to this","text":"Even if you don't like Slavoj Žižek, he has a funny story related to this: [VIDEO] He talks about how he and his associates were dissidents and had meetings and they agreed on a protocol to talk gibberish at the end. Fake, military sounding code words and stuff. It was a fun thing to do. Years later they discovered the amount of head-aches and resource drain they caused on the secret police who tried in vain to discover the meaning in this nonsense, thinking that perhaps there was some serious stuff going on. That is why I hope one good thing comes out of it, and that is people might start taking cryptography slightly more seriously and they'll also start actively fighting back. This is one way and it is fun too. (for some strange value of \"fun\")."},{"id":12373699,"author":"TheSpiceIsLife","month":"2016-08","story":"Uber's Failure in Japan","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek on people wanting government torture done discreetly.","quote":"","text":"To follow up on this, here is the full quote from Slavoj Žižek in conversation with Paul Holdengräber: Zizek: Here I’m a little bit of anti-democratic pessimist in the sense that if you really ask people, cut the bullshit, what do you really want from government, I don’t think people really want that government should not torture people and so on, the point is do it but do it discretely I don’t want to know it. 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_x0eyNkNpL0",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["ZPoqNeR3_UA",1],["h3gwyHNo7MI",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["TVwKjGbz60k",1],["BpsMkLaEiOY",1],["XJ9ckqCMiKk",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1]]},"RUwS1uAdUcI":{"wy":"Cited as the classic data-driven argument that the first-world versus third-world split is outdated and the world is improving.","q":{"x":"There is a great TED talk by Hans Rosling showing the same improving global condition through different metrics.","i":15654397,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15654397,"author":"tastyfreeze","month":"2017-11","story":"Explore Wealth and Income Inequality in the World Wealth and Income Database","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the improving global condition through different metrics.","quote":"There is a great TED talk by Hans Rosling showing the same improving global condition through different metrics.","text":"There is a great TED talk by Hans Rosling showing the same improving global condition through different metrics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7104882,"author":"noselasd","month":"2014-01","story":"2014 Gates Annual Letter: Myths that block progress for the poor","stance":"rec","why":"Data on what has happened to the world.","quote":"And here's some data about what's happened to the world","text":"And here's some data about what's happened to the world (not so much \"why\", but \"what\") [VIDEO]"},{"id":2580520,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2011-05","story":"Wikipedia Growth Animation","stance":"rec","why":"Rosling animating charts with infectious enthusiasm.","quote":"These charts just don't feel right without Hans Rosling jumping up and down in front of them like a little kid.","text":"These charts just don't feel right without Hans Rosling jumping up and down in front of them like a little kid. For the baffled: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2257384,"author":"iuguy","month":"2011-02","story":"How do programmers in the west see programmers in the east?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the first/third world split is outdated.","quote":"perhaps best explained by Hans Rosling","text":"The First/Third World East/West split isn't as concrete or relevant as it used to be, perhaps best explained by Hans Rosling: [VIDEO] The first proper comment in TFA hits the nail on the head for India, and is relevant elsewhere. If you approach programmers in a non-Anglospheric country adopting the cultural and business manners and expectations of the Anglosphere then your project is doomed from the get go. This is doubly true for countries like India that technically are too big and diverse to form part of the Anglosphere but have a massive number of English speakers, often as a second language. I don't perceive programmers from any nation in any particular way, because there are good programmers, there are crap programmers and there are culturally different programmers. What I will say is that for programmers who have English as a second or third language or not at all it's much harder for them to work as most of the tutorials, books, online resources are written primarily in English for an English speaking populous. Even the languages themselves are written in English following English idioms. How would you feel about programming in a language that uses Mandarin or Hindi as a base language?"}],"r":[["3VZSJKbzyMc",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["o0fG_lnVhHw",1],["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["NHopJHSlVo4",1],["4XpnKHJAok8",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1],["IJcuQQ1eWWI",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["njVv7J2azY8",1],["vAHXK2wut_I",1],["DlkMs4ZHHr8",1],["KAWA1DuvCnQ",1]]},"RxD6H3ri8RI":{"wy":"Recommended as a favorite among practical Blender guides from a hugely productive solo artist.","q":{"x":"My favourite guides of his are not in the Lazy Tutorials playlist, though.","i":33274699,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47261250,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-03","story":"The L in \"LLM\" Stands for Lying","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Loved demos of green screening in Blender.","quote":"I love Ian Hubert's demos of green screening in Blender.","text":"I love Ian Hubert's demos of green screening in Blender. [VIDEO] His Blender Conference talk about photogrammetry / camera projection / projection mapping was fantastic: World Building in Blender - Ian Hubert [LINK]"},{"id":33274699,"author":"slavik81","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: Recommend me a Blender course","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite guide from an unusually productive Blender artist.","quote":"My favourite guides of his are not in the Lazy Tutorials playlist, though.","text":"Ian Hubert's Lazy Tutorials would be an excellent supplementary series once you know some of the basics. They're not a general guide to Blender, but they're great resources. He has his own style of working and he's shockingly productive with it. Lazy Tutorials: [LINK] My favourite guides of his are not in the Lazy Tutorials playlist, though. Perhaps they were not lazy enough: Blender Motion Tracking: Room Transformation - [LINK] Wild Tricks for Greenscreen in Blender - [VIDEO]"},{"id":24895076,"author":"strogonoff","month":"2020-10","story":"Adobe XD for Visual Studio Code","stance":"rec","why":"Samples of what a tiny team can do with Blender.","quote":"See Ian Hubert’s channel[0] for samples of what can be achieved with Blender by a tiny team.","text":"Hands down, Blender. See Ian Hubert’s channel[0] for samples of what can be achieved with Blender by a tiny team. His examples tend to be specific to VFX, but Blender is quite general-purpose as far as motion design goes with an NLE, support for the familiar “expressions” paradigm, and so on. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["4AfRAVcThyA",1],["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["PorfLSr3DDI",1],["lkgszkPnV8g",1],["fHNmRkzxHWs",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1]]},"nphxoUxSvgY":{"wy":"Recommended in threads about long-lasting structures and passing knowledge across generations, as a long but fascinating talk.","q":{"x":"this very long but fascinating video discusses it","i":21186682,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21186682,"author":"nyolfen","month":"2019-10","story":"Collapse OS","stance":"rec","why":"Long, fascinating discussion of building lasting subterranean structures","quote":"this very long but fascinating video discusses it","text":"it's extremely difficult to build waterproof subterranean buildings, especially if they're built into permafrost (which seems these days to be more like tempofrost). this very long but fascinating video discusses it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20811630,"author":"JoeDaDude","month":"2019-08","story":"Civilizational Collapse, Part 4","stance":"rec","why":"How ancient people carried knowledge across generations","quote":"they discuss how ancient people attempted to carry knowledge through the generations.","text":"As you probably know, the Long Now foundation has wrestled with the problem of extended time period preservation of physical buildings. In the video linked below, they discuss how ancient people attempted to carry knowledge through the generations. Some of my favorites: the stone markers for the high water mark of Tsunamis, and the temple that is physically rebuilt in exact duplicate every 20 years. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11855728,"author":"JoeDaDude","month":"2016-06","story":"This is not a place of honor","stance":"rec","why":"Research on how cultures built things to last thousands of years","quote":"There is a discussion of their research into various methods pursued by different cultures over the centuries","text":"This team should collaborate with the makers of the Clock of the Long Now ( [LINK] ), another device intended to last thousands of years. There is a discussion of their research into various methods pursued by different cultures over the centuries to pursue similar goals, see video here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10130633,"author":"oddsquare66","month":"2015-08","story":"Tsunami Warnings, Written in Stone (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on the Clock, with a mention of Japanese tsunami stones","quote":"The remark on the stone in Japan is at about 44:30 into the video.","text":"The Clock of the Long Now: [LINK] The lecture on the Clock that I saw was recorded. You can see it here: [VIDEO] The remark on the stone in Japan is at about 44:30 into the video."}],"r":[["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["rOzrJjdZDRQ",1],["q5SRrsr-Iug",1]]},"4-SGpEInX_c":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible way into Wolfram's physics ideas and his personality.","q":{"x":"A good discussion that touches on these ideas in a fairly accessible way","i":29216438,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30941374,"author":"Pilottwave","month":"2022-04","story":"We’ve got a science opportunity overload: Launching the Wolfram Institute","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long Wolfram interview showing his passion rather than arrogance.","quote":"I greatly enjoyed watching those those.","text":"Lots of comments about his \"arrogance\" but i just see someone passionate, judge for yourself! He has three long interviews on Lex Fridmans youtube channel! I greatly enjoyed watching those those. 1:[Stephen Wolfram: Cellular Automata, Computation, and Physics]( [LINK] ) 2:[Stephen Wolfram: Fundamental Theory of Physics, Life, and the Universe]( [LINK] ) 3: [Stephen Wolfram: Complexity and the Fabric of Reality ]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":34974736,"author":"barking_biscuit","month":"2023-02","story":"'I Worked on Google's AI. My Fears Are Coming True'","stance":"rec","why":"Wolfram's theory of everything and how algorithms might arise.","quote":"Stephen Wolfram has a pretty amazing Theory of Everything that explains how it can be arrived at.","text":">If our brains are no more than computers and our own consciousness is software, then there exists some algorithm or combination of that gives rise to sentience. If these models arrived at this special algorithm during their training, much like the optimization of evolution arrived at the same, then we may have created something sentient. Joscha Bach gives a pretty good explanation of the algorithm we follow through the lens of Control Theory, and Stephen Wolfram has a pretty amazing Theory of Everything that explains how it can be arrived at. Joscha Bach: Nature of Reality, Dreams, and Consciousness [LINK] Stephen Wolfram: Complexity and the Fabric of Reality [VIDEO]"},{"id":29216438,"author":"Yenrabbit","month":"2021-11","story":"The Concept of the Ruliad","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible discussion of Wolfram's physics ideas with Lex Fridman.","quote":"A good discussion that touches on these ideas in a fairly accessible way","text":"A really interesting topic, especially from a philosophical point of view. A good discussion that touches on these ideas in a fairly accessible way: [VIDEO] (Lex Friedman interviews Wolfram for the 3rd time) I enjoyed Sabine Hossenfelder's thoughts on this and other 'Theory of everything' projects (Do we need a Theory of Everything? [LINK] ). A little bit of a reality-check for those who get really invested in these ideas, but I, like her, think it's really cool that there are people like Wolfram et al thinking these interesting thoughts and trying to push Physics in new directions."}],"r":[["-t1_ffaFXao",1],["ez773teNFYA",1],["GyFkWb903aU",1]]},"Ee3EtYb8d1o":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a thorough look at low-level game programming by someone with deep knowledge, offered free.","q":{"x":"the amount of knowledge and experience on-show was incredible","i":24406597,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24406597,"author":"thorin","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: As a developer what are your aha moments?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Free spare-time project showing incredible knowledge and dedication","quote":"the amount of knowledge and experience on-show was incredible","text":"After watching the first few weeks of Handmade Hero [1] I realised my idea of hard work and dedication was different to a lot of people I was trying to emulate. Doing this as a spare time, free project and the amount of knowledge and experience on-show was incredible [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28922815,"author":"agentultra","month":"2021-10","story":"Why I Hate Frameworks (2005)","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough, interesting series with a decent C introduction for game dev","quote":"I've watched a fair bit of it and many folks agree it's thorough and interesting.","text":"It's hard for me to recall how I learned because my first \"game\" I wrote was in '89 and I wasn't very old at the time. Maybe check out [VIDEO] -- I've watched a fair bit of it and many folks agree it's thorough and interesting. If you don't know C already he has a decent introduction as well. Maybe not the best place to learn C but it suits well enough for game development projects. The nice thing I find with C is that bringing life back to older machines is definitely possible -- the homebrew scene for various old consoles is very much alive and well and there's probably a C compiler that targets it."},{"id":14460726,"author":"w0utert","month":"2017-06","story":"Compiler Optimizations are Awesome","stance":"rec","why":"Live-coded game from scratch with real gems despite questionable style","quote":"there's still a lot of interesting things to learn from watching these streams","text":"Handmade hero [1] mostly, and sometimes Jonathan Blow's compiler programming streams [2]. Not all of these video's are equally interesting, but there's some real gems in there, e.g. where Casey (of HH) hand-optimizes his bilinear bitmap interpolation code (somewhere between streams ~115 and ~120 if I remember correctly). I cringe a lot about their coding style and complete disregard of architecture and basic code hygiene they appear to have, but there's still a lot of interesting things to learn from watching these streams. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["OB-bdWKwXsU",1],["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["-4Yp3j_jk8Q",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1],["0Cu6EbELZ6I",1],["HnOix9TFy1A",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1]]},"UyyjU8fzEYU":{"wy":"Recommended as a vivid first-person view of the right hemisphere in control, often linked to meditation and non-dual experience.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video if you want to hear about what life is when the right hemisphere is in control","i":30536886,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30536886,"author":"dorfsmay","month":"2022-03","story":"Not everyone should meditate","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Life with the right hemisphere in control, parallels non-dual practice","quote":"I highly recommend this video if you want to hear about what life is when the right hemisphere is in control","text":"Yes, some forms of meditation, in particular \"open awareness\" is said to give more space to the right hemisphere. I highly recommend this video if you want to hear about what life is when the right hemisphere is in control, especially for people who have been doing non-dual practices (lots of parallel experiences IMO): [VIDEO]"},{"id":30089370,"author":"dorfsmay","month":"2022-01","story":"Meditation and the Unconscious","stance":"rec","why":"Brain researcher's left-brain stroke parallels deep meditative states","quote":"Please check this video [2] of a brain researcher describing having a stroke in her left brain","text":"This is like saying sleeping is a \"waste of time, how can I be more productive during my sleep?\". \"clearing the mind\", aka Shamatha without an object, has been compared to deep sleep but also a space where insights happen. You should give it a try, do it for 10 minutes a day for a month, just relax and don't hang onto anything (thought, feeling, noise, anything, just let them happen, relax some more). Some of the theories are that it lets our right brain take over, others that only by stopping trying to be in control can old memories and repressed emotion come up, and some others are about deeper of sleep/reparation. I'm as rational as the next guy, but this type of mediation has affected me more than other forms. Please check this video [2] of a brain researcher describing having a stroke in her left brain, and has a lot of parallel with some descriptions of deep meditative state. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1921661,"author":"bradley","month":"2010-11","story":"Human brain has more switches than all computers on Earth","stance":"rec","why":"First-person account of a brain researcher experiencing her own stroke","quote":"it's fascinating to hear this first-person account from a brain researcher","text":"Thank you for mentioning Stephenson. I haven't read him other than Snow Crash, and I don't think I finished it. Googling Anathem brought me to this blog review of it: [LINK] which reminded me of this NIH neuro anatomist who studied her own stroke, including during her multi-year recovery. Her TED talk: [LINK] YouTube of same: [VIDEO] She sees the right brain hemisphere as being our \"consciousness\" wetware connecting us to others. Parts of the video are esoteric, but it's fascinating to hear this first-person account from a brain researcher, especially of the morning of her stroke when her left hemishphere was damaged by a spontaneous brain hemorrhage. Edit: couple of typos"},{"id":40801369,"author":"carapace","month":"2024-06","story":"We must seek a widely-applicable science of systems","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I would say that the first-order Cybernetics (as exemplified IMO in Ashby's book) is all about symbolic formalism for \"circular loops in causality\", and that Second-Order Cybernetics is a species of mysticism (I hasten to add that I don't mean that in a derogatory way. I'm a Mystic myself.) You could imagine a spectrum from logic to cybernetics to philosophy to mysticism. It's all fine, just I believe that it's important to be clear where on the spectrum you're working. If you want to build machines that do things use Cybernetics and feedback/control theory, if you want to grok reality and self eat a mushroom and read \"Gödel, Escher, Bach\" or Tao Te Ching. In re: \"Laws of Form\", yeah, he identifies the boundary or distinction between the mystic realm (non-form, non-distinct, non-dual) and symbolic logic, and then builds a lovely binary Boolean logic directly off of that. It's a tour de force . The really interesting thing is that George Spencer-Brown figured out how to deal with circular logical systems by introducing the concept of imaginary Boolean values. It's a formal system for symbolic logic, and you can indeed build a lovely and efficient SAT solver with it using Bricken's Basis. E.g.: [LINK] (For reference, here's the LoF/Bricken formalism) Arithmetic (()) = ()() = () Calculus A((B)) = AB A() = () A(AB) = A(B) (That third rule in the calculus, discovered by Bricken, is the kicker!) cf. \"The Markable Mark\" George Burnett-Stuart [LINK] GBS (not GSB, they're two differen"},{"id":9833149,"author":"virtualwhys","month":"2015-07","story":"The man who saw time stand still","stance":"reference","why":"Describes awakening arising from a stroke","quote":"Makes you wonder how much awakening (as described in linked video) is more accident than act of volition/determination","text":"Surprised the article doesn't reference the stroke of enlightenment [0]. Makes you wonder how much awakening (as described in linked video) is more accident than act of volition/determination (e.g. dedicated meditation/spiritual practice). Perhaps the latter sets the stage for the ultimate accident to occur. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WwslBPj8GgI",1]]},"W1czBcnX1Ww":{"wy":"HN returns to it as the classic jaw-dropping demo of legged robots handling rough terrain, and as a historical marker for how long such robots have existed.","q":{"x":"Big Dog is truly awesome.","i":730936,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":730936,"author":"russell","month":"2009-07","story":"Toyota Humanoid Robot Runs At 7 Km/hr - Awesome Vid","stance":"strong_rec","why":"BigDog runs, jumps, recovers from slips and climbs rubble.","quote":"Big Dog is truly awesome.","text":"I thought Toyota was pretty good until I followed the link in the article to Big Dog. [VIDEO] Big Dog is truly awesome. It runs, jumps, recovers from slips, climbs over rubble, ..."},{"id":1372799,"author":"vitobcn","month":"2010-05","story":"The latest version of the LittleDog robot ","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive BigDog footage with a notable moment at 40 seconds.","quote":"I was really impressed 2 years ago by their video of the 'BigDog'","text":"I find amazing how difficult it is to successfully perform apparently trivial tasks such as walking. There's more information about the 'LittleDog' on the Boston Dynamics website: [LINK] I was really impressed 2 years ago by their video of the 'BigDog': [VIDEO] (check out t=40s)"},{"id":344129,"author":"lacker","month":"2008-10","story":"Ask HN: Awesome talks/video available online?","stance":"rec","why":"Four-legged robot navigating hills and recovering from falls.","quote":"a 4-legged robot that's pretty good at navigating hills and obstacles.","text":"Here's some specific suggestions. I was most recently fascinated by this video on head tracking \"virtual reality\". [LINK] Another cool thing if you're into DIY robots (not really educational, but I found it inspiring) is the Yellow Drum Machine. [LINK] Not sure why I got on the robot tangent but here's another good one, a 4-legged robot that's pretty good at navigating hills and obstacles. Especially the recovery from a fall sequence around 1:25. [VIDEO] And here is hacking the Wii Fit controller and a Roomba to do a sort of vacuum-by-surfing... thing? [LINK]"},{"id":20961794,"author":"krapp","month":"2019-09","story":"Walking Truck","stance":"reference","why":"BigDog cited as an example of awkward but progressing quadrupeds.","quote":"","text":"Progress takes time, and ambulatory motion is a finicky, delicate nightmare that only seems simple in humans and animals thanks to millions of years of evolution. CAM was created in the 1960s[0], but BigDog[1] and other recent Boston Dynamics quadrupeds that aren't designed for human passengers are much faster[2], but still awkward. Also remember these are expensive prototypes designed to test things other than speed. Making them faster only guarantees that they break themselves harder when they run into a wall or trip over a cliff or something. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] >I think Neal Stephenson described a skateboard with hundreds of tiny feet, moving faster than wheels on any surface. I want to see that. I am neither Neal Stephenson nor a physicist but I'm pretty sure the friction caused by the increased surface contact of \"hundreds of tiny feet\" versus wheels would still make the wheels faster and more efficient. Geckos and things can stick to vertical surfaces and move quickly because they're lightweight. Anything capable of sticking to an arbitrary surface and supporting human weight is going to be slow. And if moving downhill, wheels don't really need to do work - gravity does the work. Something with \"hundreds of tiny feet\" either has to slide or \"walk.\""},{"id":17098446,"author":"Izkata","month":"2018-05","story":"What the History of Math Can Teach Us About the Future of AI","stance":"reference","why":"BigDog handling rough terrain for over a decade.","quote":"BigDog has been handling rough terrain for over a decade","text":"> > Meanwhile, many of the tasks that seem most basic to us humans—like running over rough terrain or interpreting body language—are all but impossible for the machines of today and the foreseeable future. > Wow no. ...wow indeed. No research at all. Often I just read the comments here and skip the article, but that made me go and look to be sure. For anyone somehow still unaware (BigDog creepiness factor was nearly viral back when I was in college around 2008), BigDog has been handling rough terrain for over a decade: [VIDEO] Here's BigDog getting abused to see what it can recover from (including being kicked in the side): [LINK] Here's a running cheetah robot jumping over unexpected obstacles (2015): [LINK] And here's a two-legged robot walking around outside (2017): [LINK]"},{"id":15599268,"author":"Johnny555","month":"2017-11","story":"Sony Aibo","stance":"reference","why":"Robot slipping on ice evokes sympathy.","quote":"I definitely sympathize with this dog-like robot slipping on ice","text":"Just because he ruled it out doesn't mean that it's not a factor. People become attached to lots of inanimate items, and can feel emphathy for robots [1] [2], though perhaps not as strongly as with living creatures. US Military soldiers can develop an emotional connection with bomb disposal robots. [3] I definitely sympathize with this dog-like robot slipping on ice: [VIDEO] Even though it's clearly a robot with no attempt to make it look or act like a dog. I can definitely believe that people can become attached, even fall in love with, their robot dogs. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":8519098,"author":"ilaksh","month":"2014-10","story":"Breakthroughs that have unleashed AI development","stance":"reference","why":"Example of robots walking over uneven terrain.","quote":"Walking over uneven terrain, (also completely unrelated to artificial general intelligence)","text":"[LINK] Insight, creativity, and general intelligence are all different and active areas of research. There is quite a lot of progress in creativity. Google for 'creative software'. Insight can mean many things, but Watson can now provide insights into cancer diagnosis. Consciousness is actually fairly well understood in terms of attention, focus, and other aspects. Artificial general intelligence is a very active field seeing quite a bit of progress. Here is a bird that flies with flapping wings: [LINK] (By the way, that is completely unrelated to AI). Walking over uneven terrain, (also completely unrelated to artificial general intelligence): [LINK] [VIDEO] Deep learning is beyond statistical pattern matching. It does involve both supervised and unsupervised learning. Deep learning is currently the most successful technique, but not the most ambitious approach in AGI. Google for 'AGI', 'deep learning', 'sparse autoencoder', 'hierarchical hidden Markov model', 'OpenCog', 'spiking neural network', 'Hierarchical Temporal Memory'"},{"id":4249648,"author":"jamesaguilar","month":"2012-07","story":"And everyone gets a robot pony","stance":"reference","why":"Early example of robot pony-like locomotion.","quote":"We've already got something that's kind of moving in that direction","text":"On a more flippant level, isn't it pretty obvious that robot ponies are going to be a thing that is possible to have some time in the next century or two? We've already got something that's kind of moving in that direction, and this was a few years ago: [VIDEO]"}]},"fTjhHrcyiQI":{"wy":"HN points to it as a model for firing or laying off people clearly and respectfully.","q":{"x":"This is probably the greatest video clip on the subject of all time","i":22759624,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22759624,"author":"davidu","month":"2020-04","story":"Planning and Managing Layoffs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clip on delivering bad news without trying to soften it","quote":"This is probably the greatest video clip on the subject of all time","text":"One of the hardest things about this process for a sensitive and compassionate leader is knowing when to not try and \"make it better.\" This is probably the greatest video clip on the subject of all time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29461900,"author":"notpeter","month":"2021-12","story":"Better.com CEO fires 900 employees on Zoom call","stance":"rec","why":"Moneyball scene modeling straight, no-fluff firing","quote":"Moneyball had some solid advice","text":"Getting fired is bad enough, but if it's being done by someone bad at firing people it's the worst. Moneyball had some solid advice: \"Be straight with them, no fluff, just facts. 'Pete I gotta let you go. Jack's office will handle the details.'\" That's it. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29029186,"author":"jaggederest","month":"2021-10","story":"Programmer Moneyball (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Scene on professional, straightforward parting of ways","quote":"this wonderful scene I refer to often when I'm working as a consultant","text":"It's extra funny, because the same movie has this wonderful scene I refer to often when I'm working as a consultant: [VIDEO] TL;DW: \"We're professionals, treat me like a professional. If it's not working out, say so, and I'll make it right or we'll shake hands and part ways on good terms\". I used to provide clients with a \"how to fire me\" document because it made them feel comfortable hiring me in the first place. (Tell me, I return materials, pay me outstanding balances, here's the keys you change, here's how we wrap up the contract terms, etc etc) I've been \"fired\" a bunch of times, and at times have had those same clients that \"fired\" me refer me to new clients. It's the same as with most technical work, I find: if you're not failing at least sometimes, you're not pushing the limits of what is possible."},{"id":32424081,"author":"smcl","month":"2022-08","story":"CEO's LinkedIn crying selfie about layoffs met with backlash","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It reminded me of the scene when Jonah Hill's character in Moneyball learned how to tell a player they were cut","text":"> Apologize to the employees leaving as a company. Explain why. Tell them what happens next. Apologize again. 100% bang on. I've been through a layoff, it sucked but the company were straight-forward, didn't mince their words and didn't fuck around. If I had to sit through some spiel about how the manager or CEO felt, I would've been upset. It reminded me of the scene when Jonah Hill's character in Moneyball learned how to tell a player they were cut/traded from their baseball team, and then had to actually do it ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":22748598,"author":"klenwell","month":"2020-04","story":"Planning and Managing Layoffs","stance":"reference","why":"Dramatizes handling firing crisply and directly","quote":"I think your advice is well dramatized by this Moneyball scene","text":"Agree with you. Saw something like this not too long ago where it was not handled crisply. There were no long-lasting consequences as far as I know and I can't get into the details but it was just sloppy and leaked all over the place. I think your advice is well dramatized by this Moneyball scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21336495,"author":"orangepenguin","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: Never been fired, what should I know before I fire someone?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm reminded of a clip from the movie Moneyball","text":"I'm reminded of a clip from the movie Moneyball: [VIDEO] I really like your suggestion that someone check in with the person in a couple days. That helps address the charitable desire without compromising the need for performance at work."}],"r":[["BzhbAK1pdPM",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["S0mviKhVmBI",1]]},"iGbC5j4pG9w":{"wy":"It is the standard citation in HN housing debates for showing that dense cities can still have affordable single-family homes.","q":{"x":"a fantastic overview of housing for a nuclear family in Tokyo","i":22992262,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22992262,"author":"bane","month":"2020-04","story":"An average family in Tokyo can own a new house for $850/month","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic overview of housing for a nuclear family in Tokyo.","quote":"a fantastic overview of housing for a nuclear family in Tokyo","text":"Decent family houses in Tokyo are definitely more expensive than this, but not hugely (also Kyoto surprisingly). There's some interesting things about the Japanese housing market, for example, many homes become \"worthless\" after the end of the mortgage -- so they depreciate and are more like buying cars. Tokyo is also an absolutely enormous city with even more massive metro area. There's very few examples on the planet that come close. Tokyo is sometimes called \"the infinite city\" for good reason. This means that yes, you can pay huge money for a coffin in some areas, and little money for an entire house elsewhere in the same city. New York is kind of the same way in that respect, but is actually still smaller and less diverse. I could write a ton more, but I think this series from \"Life Where I'm From\" which is pointed out in the article is a fantastic overview of housing for a nuclear family in Tokyo. How an Average Family in Tokyo Can Buy a New Home - [VIDEO] Brand New Japanese House Tour - [LINK] Are Japanese Homes Really Worthless After 30 Years? - [LINK] Old Japanese Apartment vs. New House - [LINK] What a Very Old Japanese House is Like - [LINK] Living in Traditional Japanese Townhouses: Kyo-machiya - [LINK] Checking Out New Japanese House Design and Technology - [LINK] I'd also point out this young couple who are expecting their first child (Paolo from Tokyo) What a Japanese Moving Company in Tokyo is like - [LINK] $1500 Japanese Apartment Tour in Tokyo Shibuya - [LIN"},{"id":30095526,"author":"seanmcdirmid","month":"2022-01","story":"The history of the end of poverty has just begun","stance":"rec","why":"Nice explanation of housing affordability in Tokyo.","quote":"Here is a nice video explaining housing affordability in Tokyo","text":"Here is a nice video explaining housing affordability in Tokyo: [VIDEO] Notes: * No one thinks of residential housing as an investment, so there is no speculation. * Building codes are such that residential can be built almost anywhere. Japan has zoning, it is just really simplified. * There is no resistance to rebuilding, so it is done a lot (why houses last only 30 years, the market demands new housing). * Housing can be built on really small lots, only 2 meters of clearance is required. * A $300k low end house is around 6-700 SQF, which might not make sense from an American context."},{"id":16088864,"author":"monkeypizza","month":"2018-01","story":"Why Is 'Affordable' Housing So Expensive to Build?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why new detached homes in Tokyo cost about 300k USD.","quote":"Check out this video explaining why you can buy new detached homes in Tokyo for about 300k usd.","text":"Check out this video explaining why you can buy new detached homes in Tokyo for about 300k usd. [VIDEO] * Land owners have an absolute right to develop their own land - neighbors can't block you. * You can build a 60 m^2 x 3 floor house on 100m^2 of land, or more. So basically they build way denser, and reduce opportunities to block/rentseek."},{"id":38310290,"author":"houseatrielah","month":"2023-11","story":"280M e-bikes and mopeds are cutting demand for oil far more than electric cars","stance":"reference","why":"Detached single-family homes exist in Tokyo for about 300k.","quote":"There's more urban dwelling options than just skyscraper.","text":"How an Average Family in Tokyo Can Buy a New (detacted single-family) Home (for about $300k in Tokyo Proper) [VIDEO] There's more urban dwelling options than just skyscraper. Low Japanese fertility appears to be a product of their work culture; you don't have time to go on dates if you leave the office at 10pm."},{"id":35236811,"author":"sgu999","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"reference","why":"Tokyo avoids housing issues through regulations and culture.","quote":"","text":"I think the main drives are real estate investments and centralisation... Tokyo doesn't really have these issues in part due to cultural differences but also due to better regulations on urbanism. [ ] [ ] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31210182,"author":"Manuel_D","month":"2022-04","story":"Is everything falling apart?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for dense cities like Tokyo keeping housing affordable.","quote":"","text":"Building taller, denser housing doesn't pop the real-estate bubble. As cities grow denser, the land gets more expensive because land is finite. The solution to housing shortages is to build more, denser housing. The existing homeowners aren't getting a bad deal - they'll still be able to sell their houses at an immense gain. It's just that the same plot of land would occupy more units of housing. This is largely how dense cities like Tokyo manage to keep housing affordable [1]. When population increases you keep housing affordable by building more houses per unit of area. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30376036,"author":"filereaper","month":"2022-02","story":"Investors bought a record share of U.S. homes in 2021","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of Japan's approachable housing.","quote":"I've seen videos about Japan's approachable housing.","text":"Oh yes, I've seen videos about Japan's approachable housing. [1]. I'm sure Korea and other Asian nations have similar approaches. I mainly wanted to highlight a market driven approach that works with the other industries in the US as opposed to a government directed one. Albeit the government provides stimulus of some type to kick start it, like how NASA drove the privatization of Space and now we have lots of launch vehicles via different companies. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25632429,"author":"endori97","month":"2021-01","story":"Letting Go of Nostalgia Urbanism","stance":"reference","why":"Shows freestanding houses in dense Tokyo for about 300k.","quote":"","text":">>curious why this is so. I think because real estate investors have enough capital to take over cool areas. A stark counterexample is Japan where you can own a freestanding house in a dense urban environment near public transit for $300k -- what would that cost you in London, NYC, Berlin? It seems like Japan is one of the few major cities where you could have a large family (or large studio) and live an urban life -- everywhere else you are stuck with a 2BR or 3BR apt (if your rich). How an Average Family in Tokyo Can Buy a New Home [VIDEO]"},{"id":20947632,"author":"manfredo","month":"2019-09","story":"California Approves Statewide Rent Control","stance":"reference","why":"Supports housing as depreciating asset keeping Tokyo homes affordable.","quote":"","text":"Housing cannot be both affordable and and a good investment. The definition of a good investment is a piece of property that has a price that increases over time. The nature of exponential growth means that eventually housing will become unaffordable if it is perpetually a good investment. Society's expectation that housing is a good investment results in people taking on significant debt to buy increasingly expensive homes. The consequence is that homeowners have a huge incentive to suppress the construction of additional housing, to inflate the values of their homes. And when housing prices do fall, people's financial lives are ruined. Contrast metros like SF, Portland, and Seattle with places like Tokyo where homes can be bought for < $400,000. A big part of this is that housing is expected to be a depreciating asset: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19935675,"author":"manfredo","month":"2019-05","story":"California bill to add more housing in single-family neighborhoods blocked","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tokyo houses cost 300-400k as depreciating assets.","quote":"Houses in the Special Wards of Tokyo can be bought for $300-400k","text":"I think a lot of it boils down to an ingrained belief that real estate should be an investment. Everyone buys a house with the expectation that its value will go up. This means that homeowners are extremely adverse to policies that might reduce home prices (like building more housing). Contrast this with houses in Japan, where it's expected that a home is a depreciating asset. Houses in the Special Wards of Tokyo can be bought for $300-400k: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16646092,"author":"patio11","month":"2018-03","story":"California should emulate Tokyo, where housing stayed ahead of population growth","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim about cheap Tokyo houses and low down payments.","quote":"","text":"Pay ~$300k for a house in Tokyo, which will cost you +/- $10k down [+] and ~$1k a month for a mortgage. Japanese underwriting standards would consider this doable at a household income of approximately $2.8k a month or so. c.f. [VIDEO] [+] I realize that number is surprising, and I would have found it surprising up until ~3 weeks ago, but I can assure you that this product is presently commercially available in Tokyo, including to people who do not exactly smash Japanese banks' definition of \"desirable client.\""},{"id":14636632,"author":"maxsilver","month":"2017-06","story":"The Unaffordable Urban Paradise","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that single-family homes in dense cities can be affordable and scalable.","quote":"","text":"> (single family homes with reasonable space) they make those cities unaffordable, they can't possibly scale, they are more hostile to walking, biking, and transit. None of that is actually true. [VIDEO] and [LINK] Apartments are great for those who want them, but there's nothing wrong or anti-urban about family housing. You can easily give everyone (edit: everyone who wants one) their own single-family detached housing (with privacy, and parking), inside a major city, for really cheap. It would still be 100% supportive of walking, biking, and transit, and can scale for at least the next 100 years. I can state this as a simple fact, because it is, it already exists on Earth today. Wealthier Americans just refuse to let that happen in our own cities. We simply don't build urban housing for families (even in instances where zoning rules do allow it) so families literally can't make that choice. Cities force sprawl, and then complain that decisions must be made to service the sprawl they themselves made."},{"id":14285895,"author":"bane","month":"2017-05","story":"Japan Shows the Way to Affordable Megacities (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Eye-opening look at affordable housing within Tokyo.","quote":"One of the really eye opening things I've learned in the last few years is how relatively affordable housing can be","text":"When I was young I remember reading about \"Tokyo, the city of $11 hot dogs\"...this was when a normal hot dog in the states was a buck or two. I never really stopped to figure that maybe it was just foreign stuff that was crazy expensive. One of the really eye opening things I've learned in the last few years is how relatively affordable housing can be...even within the Tokyo city limits. [VIDEO] -- older homes can be much much cheaper. Here's a $1600/mo house [LINK] This needs to be considered against local incomes of course, so they're a bit less tempting to locals than to American foreigners from ultra expensive coastal cities. Part of this seems to be the particular Japanese combination of the shrinking and aging population + continued attempts at maintaining GDP via construction works + cultural preference for new living arrangements instead of old construction. But like anyplace, you pay for where you live and there are certainly incredibly expensive places to live. Outside of living arrangements, public transport, food, clothes, and so on...everything except for personal vehicles is relatively affordable on a foreigner's salary. However, salaries in Japan are surprisingly (sometimes shockingly low). I think I've heard that a person working in a regular \"salaryman\" job can expect to earn around their age * 10,000 USD. So if you're 30, you'll make around $30k USD. This information is a bit old, so expect it to be a little higher, but this table [1] from 2014 suggests a 32"}],"r":[["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["ouAG4vXFORc",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["S4gU2Tsv6hY",1],["ZXJWO2FQ16c",1],["EFRUL7vKdU8",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1]]},"z8MVKianh54":{"wy":"HN points to it as the main argument from the array-language camp that types are unnecessary there, and as a nuanced take on typed versus untyped programming.","q":{"x":"which argues that APL already provides the development ergonomics that are typically acribed to type systems.","i":21373433,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20300358,"author":"kadblas","month":"2019-06","story":"Why I think Haskell is the best general purpose language as of June 22 2019","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nuanced take on whether APL needs a type system.","quote":"To explain why, I recommend watching Aaron Hsu's videos on Dyalog APL","text":"Qualification: I was introduced to FP a number of years ago and have finally reached a point where I feel that I have a deep understanding of FP in general. In my mind, Haskell is the \"best\" language out there (\"best\" in that it provides the best overall gain when all tradeoffs are considered). I'm no longer confident that Haskell is the \"best\" language anymore. I was recently introduced to a language that might be even better than Haskell: Dyalog APL. ( [LINK] ). Try it out yourself ( [LINK] ) To explain why, I recommend watching Aaron Hsu's videos on Dyalog APL: - Does APL need a Type System? ( [VIDEO] ) - This is actually a very interesting question that is more nuanced than upon first glance. - Design Patterns and Anti-Patterns in APL ( [LINK] ) - The main takeaway from this video for me was the principle of \"Idioms over libraries.\" - Higher Performance Tree-Wrangling, the APL way ( [LINK] ) - Or how to model Trees without using pointers by using \"Inverted Tables.\" (An inverted table is a table where the columns are the rows and the rows are the columns)"},{"id":22838379,"author":"eggy","month":"2020-04","story":"Ode to J","stance":"rec","why":"Hsu talk on usability and scaling of APL.","quote":"I particularly like this one","text":"The right tool for the job. If you are building a huge website with input forms, videos, data collection, ML algorithms, yes then you wouldn't do the whole thing in APL or J even if you could. Python is big in ML because packages for working with data in array language ways were developed. Pandas by Wes McKinney is one example, and he studied J or q, and even tweeted: IMHO J is the best APL for data analysis because of its broad suite hash table-based functions. I like APL and J as a scratchpad where arrays are the basic unit and not scalars. J is functional and it turned me on to that world before I touched Haskell or F#. Aaron Hsu has a lot of great videos that speak to a lot of the usability and scaling out you mention: [LINK] I particularly like this one: [VIDEO] I am able to grasp concepts or own them after coding them in APL or J even if the code isn't as fast such as how well APL applies to Convolutional Neural Networks [1,2]. I really understood the mechanics of CNNs better after working through this paper a lot more than books I had read on ANNs in general since the late 80s/early 90s. By contrast, I have coded ANNs in C and Python, and I get lost in the PL, not the concept, if that makes sense. Anyway, I am a polyglot and find people criticize J/APL/k etc. from a brief look without really trying to learn the language. I learned assembler and basic back in 1978 to 1982, and I felt the same way when I first looked at opcodes. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":21373433,"author":"xelxebar","month":"2019-10","story":"Dex: A research language for array processing in the Haskell/ML family","stance":"rec","why":"Argues APL already provides ergonomics usually attributed to type systems.","quote":"which argues that APL already provides the development ergonomics that are typically acribed to type systems.","text":"Just starting to learn J, myself, and this brings to mind Aaron Hsu's \"Does APL Need A Type System?\" talk: [VIDEO] which argues that APL already provides the development ergonomics that are typically acribed to type systems. I.e. why type check your tacit function when it's implementation is about the same length as its type annotation? Anyway, as someone trained in Category Theory and a huge fan of type systems, I found Hsu's argument surprisingly pursuasive. I would love to hear any counterpoint arguments and the motivations behind attempts at giving J a type system."},{"id":42680457,"author":"gsf_emergency","month":"2025-01","story":"Flattening ASTs and other compiler data structures (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Added to commenter's to-watch list.","quote":"I did push the following to my to-watch stack","text":"I've given up on finding relevant low-level CUDA(? tacit? asm??!!) code from Hsu*, but I did push the following to my to-watch stack [VIDEO] Does APL need a type system Guess it's time to reverse whatever else I can find [LINK] *Bad faith, or just a run o' the mill, aka compleatly forgiveable profiteering?"},{"id":34390612,"author":"tluyben2","month":"2023-01","story":"A Dynamic Forth Compiler for WebAssembly","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the view that types are unnecessary in array languages.","quote":"Most people think it's nonsense anyway [0]","text":"> I am a Lisp, April, APL/J/BQE, and Forth[2] aficionado. Same taste here, although I have the quirk that I also like types & proofs. Because of Lips, APL(ish) & Forth are so nice and terse, I feel the need, every so many years, to try to combine them. Usually I quickly build something (I build a lot of Forth-likes for embedded and other compiled languages to speed up work without having to compile) that combines these 3. Usually I find that Arthur already did that kind of with Shakti and before with k. For my, however, APL chars are better. Since recently, I use the BQN keymappings to create new things. So then I go into struggling how to add types to something like the above (outside Lisp/Scheme as that has been done). Most people think it's nonsense anyway [0], so i'm not quite looking to have the user add actual types (mostly), but more-so with global static analysis. Anyway, bit offtopic, but nice to see there are more people (outside Arthur [1]) who like some of these combinations of languages. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":28970412,"author":"rscho","month":"2021-10","story":"Notation as a Tool of Thought (1986) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Prominent APLers argue APL does not need a type system.","quote":"A video on the topic","text":"Prominent APLers think it doesn't need one. I agree, because APL tries to be close to math, and you don't write types when doing math. You use alternate and overloaded operators. A video on the topic [VIDEO]"},{"id":22011241,"author":"tluyben2","month":"2020-01","story":"ngn/k, an AGPL K interpreter","stance":"reference","why":"Video on mixing typed functional programming with APL.","quote":"Video on that topic is here[0].","text":"And I find q verbose :) However my poor statically typed brain is keeps struggling on how to mix Haskell (...) with APL/k. Video on that topic is here[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v7Mt0GYHU9A",2],["hzPd3umu78g",1],["V-1RhQ1uuQ4",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1],["1PGm8LslEb4",1],["FY_NtO7SIrY",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1],["lMcRBdSdO_U",1],["aNR4n0i2ZlM",1],["tQpqsmwlfY0",1]]},"2xcZS7arcgk":{"wy":"Cited in climate and carbon-removal threads as an inspiring example of re-greening deserts.","q":{"x":"Geoff Lawtons work in Jordan is inspiring","i":19415118,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":23885356,"author":"splittingTimes","month":"2020-07","story":"Hugelkultur: Raised Garden Beds","stance":"rec","why":"Permaculture project greening a desert.","quote":"They have developed specialized techniques for all climate zones.","text":"I think you need a more encompassing approach. Permaculture might be something for you. They have developed specialized techniques for all climate zones. For example check out the \"Greening the desert\" project. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19415118,"author":"2sk21","month":"2019-03","story":"Nitrogen-fixing trees “eat” rocks, play pivotal role in forest health","stance":"rec","why":"Permaculture work turning Jordanian desert green.","quote":"Geoff Lawtons work in Jordan is inspiring","text":"Permaculture is going to become a very important subject for study. I have recently gotten interested in the subject and the results have been amazing even in very unpromising conditions. Geoff Lawtons work in Jordan is inspiring: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14176749,"author":"schiffern","month":"2017-04","story":"UK launches program to explore removal of greenhouse gases from the atmosphere","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary showing deserts being reforested and regreened.","quote":"I would also recommend the excellent Greening the Desert documentary for an example of the reverse process.","text":">You don't mention that trees also regulate the local microclimate; they tend to make rain, for example. Good point. I'm usually so eager to mention that, too! Trees regulate not only the microclimate, but the macroclimate as well. Isotopic analysis shows that about 80% of all terrestrial rainfall is caused by transpiration from trees and other plants. Trees essentially act as a giant inland irrigation network, using solar power to transport water via the airstream to power the water cycle. [LINK] So trees cause rain. This explains why deforestation causes desertification. I would also recommend the excellent Greening the Desert documentary for an example of the reverse process. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18289476,"author":"schiffern","month":"2018-10","story":"Carbon Removal Technologies","stance":"reference","why":"Example of re-greening deserts.","quote":"we can re-green those same deserts[1]","text":">most forest owners in the world don't know or don't have incentive to care So why limit yourself to existing forests? Instead of flooding deserts (using energy-intensive/land-intensive/wealth concentrating desalinization), we can re-green those same deserts[1], which restarts the \"atmospheric river\" that brings water to the interiors of continents. Isotopic analysis has revealed that trees powers the water cycle (by recycling rainfall that would otherwise flow off into the ocean) and causes 80% of Earth's terrestrial rainfall.[2] Compared to desalination this is far less costly (downside being, it's harder for Nestle et al. to profit off it). Yes Virginia, rain literally comes from trees! This partly explains why deforestation leads to desertification. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13458055,"author":"schiffern","month":"2017-01","story":"Turning CO2 to Stone","stance":"reference","why":"Example of greening deserts, cited as a source.","quote":"","text":">Trees certainly work as a buffer in the sense that carbon in plants is carbon not in the atmosphere, but the amount you could hold is tiny in comparison to the amount that must be sequestered Is it? Let's do the math. Absorbing the 350 teratonnes of human emitted carbon[1] over the 8.4 billion hectares of Earth's non-tundra / non-desert land surface area[2] works out to... 42 tonnes per hectare. If we can green deserts[3] that drops to 30 tonnes. That's 30-40 trees per hectare, at 1 tonne per tree.[4] Or 3 kg per square meter of soil carbon, the equivalent of 25-35 cm of topsoil. More likely some combination of the two. Soil carbon is stored up to 40 meters down by deep rooted plants.[5] Most of our land management is via agriculture, so agriculture seems to be the only lever long enough to make a dent. Practically this implies transitioning from soil destroying tillage to soil building cover crops, long-distance imported fertilizer to in-situ fertility produced by soil organisms, and ecologically unstable monocultures to resiliant polyculture, agroforestry, and rotational grazing systems. The \"gotcha\" question is not whether we can replace our existing agriculture with these systems. The question is whether we can replace our existing agriculture with itself. Any unsustainable system is, by definition, not a viable replacement for itself. [1] [LINK] . [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] . [5] [LINK]"}],"r":[["QHPmRJIoc2k",1],["xKCuDxpccYM",1],["fjbWr3ODbAo",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["2lisQQmWQkY",1],["x2H60ritjag",1]]},"DlkMs4ZHHr8":{"wy":"HN points to it as a vivid demo that plain A* search can beat fancier machine-learning approaches in game AI.","q":{"x":"It's pretty interesting, a lot of people wrote/are writing high falutin' machine learning algorithms","i":2796852,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2796852,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2011-07","story":"Infinite Mario in HTML5","stance":"rec","why":"Winning entry shows A* beating machine learning approaches","quote":"It's pretty interesting, a lot of people wrote/are writing high falutin' machine learning algorithms","text":"The original java infinite mario was used in the fascinating Mario AI competition. Details and papers here: [LINK] Video of last years winning entry at work here: [VIDEO] It's pretty interesting, a lot of people wrote/are writing high falutin' machine learning algorithms, but they are consistently trounced by good old A* techniques."},{"id":1871620,"author":"feral","month":"2010-11","story":"Using genetic algorithms to train a Mario AI","stance":"rec","why":"Winning A* bot displaying trajectory calculations","quote":"cool to watch, as they display its trajectory calculations.","text":"Reminds me of the AI competition on infinite mario, recently: Here is a video of the winning bot (A* based approach), cool to watch, as they display its trajectory calculations. [VIDEO] GA is cool and everything, but I am sometimes sceptical about its merits. Yes, it gives you a natural problem representation, but would it really have done a better job in this particular domain than using a technique like simulated annealing to move through the Mario AI parameter space? We just don't know without seeing benchmark comparisons. Maybe its much worse. So, its a cool application, but we should be cautious about how quickly we decide GA was a particularly suitable search algorithm."},{"id":745029,"author":"Radix","month":"2009-08","story":"Mario AI competition might have a strong contender","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive Mario AI bot demo","quote":"If you are impressed with this one follow the link in the video to here","text":"If you are impressed with this one follow the link in the video to here: [VIDEO] For all my hours as a kid I was never this good, I never even realized I could kill the pipe fish by jumping from below."},{"id":4480530,"author":"a1k0n","month":"2012-09","story":"Game AI vs Traditional AI","stance":"reference","why":"Winning A* solution in Infinite Mario AI contest","quote":"The winning solution? A* of course","text":"There was even an Infinite Mario AI contest in which you had to write a player which gets through as many levels of increasing difficulty as possible. The winning solution? A* of course: [VIDEO] (I had an also-ran entry in this contest that took 3rd place, but my solution wasn't significantly different than Robin's -- his was able to anticipate bullets firing but mine wasn't) Writeup: [LINK]"},{"id":1631164,"author":"albertzeyer","month":"2010-08","story":"Time lapse screencap video of a games programmer","stance":"reference","why":"Winner of Mario AI competition presented with A* algorithm","quote":"the winner has succeeded with an A* algorithm which he presented in a nice video","text":"I seem to miss such contests most of the time and only hear about them when they ended. I would love to attend to some, just for the fun (because I don't know if I would have any chance to actually win). Where would be a good source for such competitions? Another fun competition I read about was the Super Mario AI competition in 2009 where the winner has succeeded with an A* algorithm which he presented in a nice video: [VIDEO] The 2010 Mario AI championship also seems to be over already. Another fun competition I have heard about is the International Obfuscated C Code Contest. However, while reading this is very interesting and quite fun, I am not sure if I would have fun to do this myself. (Somehow I am more focused on the output/result of the code and I like to have clean, elegant and intelligent code, as less obfuscated as possible. :))"}],"r":[["NHopJHSlVo4",1],["4XpnKHJAok8",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1],["IJcuQQ1eWWI",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["njVv7J2azY8",1],["vAHXK2wut_I",1]]},"I845O57ZSy4":{"wy":"HN mines it for Carmack's views on AGI, debuggers, tooling and the metaverse, and recommends it as worth the full length.","q":{"x":"The entire video is worth viewing, an impressive 5:15h!","i":32526194,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36904586,"author":"fernandotakai","month":"2023-07","story":"Commander Keen's Adaptive Tile Scrolling","stance":"rec","why":"Link to Carmack interview plus other recommended interviews","quote":"i would also recommend another two of his interviews","text":"link for people that want to watch it: [VIDEO] i would also recommend another two of his interviews: Todd Howard [LINK] Guido van Rossun [LINK]"},{"id":35221874,"author":"joblazny","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: Which tech podcasts would you recommend to a friend?","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended Lex Fridman episode with Carmack","quote":"one episode I'd like to recommend is episode #309 with John Carmack.","text":"Lex Fridman. He does long form interviews (~4 hours) on more than just tech, and all of the guests he interviews are fascinating. He simultaneously approaches his interviews from a scientific background (MIT AI lecturer) and a love for humanity. If you like tech, one episode I'd like to recommend is episode #309 with John Carmack. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32526194,"author":"kken","month":"2022-08","story":"John Carmack's new AGI company, Keen Technologies, has raised a $20M round","stance":"rec","why":"The interview behind Carmack's AGI company, 5h15m","quote":"The entire video is worth viewing, an impressive 5:15h!","text":"The interview with Lex Fridman that he was referring to: [VIDEO] The entire video is worth viewing, an impressive 5:15h!"},{"id":42397831,"author":"owenpalmer","month":"2024-12","story":"A ChatGPT clone, in 3000 bytes of C, backed by GPT-2 (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's timestamped AGI prediction before ChatGPT","quote":"it is likely that the code for artificial general intelligence is going to be tens of thousands of lines of code","text":"\"I am not a madman for saying that it is likely that the code for artificial general intelligence is going to be tens of thousands of lines of code, not millions of lines of code. This is code that conceivably one individual could write, unlike writing a new web browser or operating system.\" - John Carmack, Lex Fridman Podcast (August 4th, 2022) This was around 3 months before ChatGPT's initial release. Timestamped: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38421068,"author":"Geee","month":"2023-11","story":"Daily Coffee Habits May Affect Brain Plasticity and Learning","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's diet coke habit and sharpness","quote":"John Carmack drinks 8-9 cans of diet coke a day [0]","text":"Sorry, but I don't believe this. Surely someone would have noticed that coffee drinkers are slow learners. I'd say that on the contrary many life-long learners like computer programmers are known to consume copious amounts of caffeine. John Carmack drinks 8-9 cans of diet coke a day [0], and he said that he is still as sharp as he used to be [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36905093,"author":"adamddev1","month":"2023-07","story":"Commander Keen's Adaptive Tile Scrolling","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on releasing early games quickly using a framework","quote":"Having a previous framework and pipeline seemed to help get them out really quick.","text":"Carmack talked about how they released those first games on the Lex Friedman podcast. Having a previous framework and pipeline seemed to help get them out really quick. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35487756,"author":"rajman187","month":"2023-04","story":"John Carmack on the Similarity of Human Learning and LLMs Training","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's claim AGI needs only thousands of lines of code","quote":"AGI is going to be a few thousands of lines of code, ultimately","text":"Carmack is no doubt brilliant and it shows through Oculus as a product. But AGI is not about algorithm optimization the way 3D graphics were in the 90s. I am not sure the approach he takes (let’s find a way to write this in assembly) can apply here. He’s said before that AGI is going to be a few thousands of lines of code, ultimately [1], and it’s hard to believe that’s really the case. That said, I did get a chance to speak with him one on one last year and he really emphasized a few things; the need for being product oriented and giving customers what they want rather than chasing cool engineering (über)solutions; not being careless with resources just because we have more power with modern hardware (he poked fun at React where you spin up a new thread just for an interactive button); and being aware of the inefficiencies brought on by infinite resources (# of engineers and/or funding, which make you think less critically about timelines and delivering within bounded means) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33350914,"author":"poszlem","month":"2022-10","story":"Vim After 15 Years (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack saying almost nobody uses IDEs at Meta","quote":"almost nobody uses IDEs at Meta","text":"This is something I've been thinking about a lot recently, especially since listening to John Carmack interview ( [VIDEO] ) and him saying that almost nobody uses IDEs at Meta. I think it is totally possible to be (even very) productive in VIM. I do think, though, that a person who is productive in VIM would be more productive if he used a proper IDE. This is my experience after I had been using VIM for about 10 years and switched to an IntelliJ IDE (and actually learned it) (This, of course, assumes that there is a proper IDE for your language)"},{"id":32362606,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2022-08","story":"Lex Fridman Podcast #309 – John Carmack","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on who first found a technique","quote":"Carmack says he wasn't the first to find it[1]","text":"Carmack says he wasn't the first to find it[1], and the other person had \"done that somewhat before\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32479152,"author":"pengaru","month":"2022-08","story":"Apple tells employees to work at the office three times per week starting Sept","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote for AI assistants joining meetings","quote":"","text":"Lately I've been wondering if the army of 100% remote folks realize their shift to entirely virtualized interactions creates opportunity for AIs to compete on equal grounds. An AI assistant won't be taking your place in the office anytime soon. But the writing's on the wall for AI assistants to start appearing in your Zoom meetings [0]. Once they can churn out code 24x7 without eating or sleeping, glwt. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32514703,"author":"vlod","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Why are we not using debuggers more?","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on big tech hostility toward debuggers","quote":"He doesn't understand why a lot of SV big-tech companies are hostile to debuggers.","text":"John Carmack's view [0] on debuggers (from Lex Fridman Podcast podcast video) TLDR: He doesn't understand why a lot of SV big-tech companies are hostile to debuggers. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32532375,"author":"flat-pluto","month":"2022-08","story":"Zuckerberg unveils new metaverse avatar after getting mocked for creepy version","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on how hard but desirable the metaverse is","quote":"building the metaverse is insanely difficult but also desirable for a variety of reasons.","text":"I was listening to the Lex Fridman interview of John Carmack[1] the other day and find myself agreeing with Carmack. Essentially, building the metaverse is insanely difficult but also desirable for a variety of reasons. I'm glad that someone (sucks that it is FB) is investing heavily into the R&D to make it a reality. As for the new avatar, it's just the first draft so to speak. Given the talent at Reality Labs, I'm certain that things will look much better a few years from now. People often have this vision of what VR/AR should look like and will be disappointed that v1.0 does not live up to those expectations. As long as incremental progress is made, it will compound over time and eventually we'll get there. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4XpnKHJAok8",1],["-DVyjdw4t9I",1],["u4ZoJKF_VuA",1]]},"JsVtHqICeKE":{"wy":"It is a repeat pick in 'favorite tech talk' threads as a funny, eye-opening look at how easily physical security and people can be bypassed.","q":{"x":"The absolute funniest tech talk I've seen","i":26341950,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26341950,"author":"crazypython","month":"2021-03","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funniest tech talk the commenter has seen","quote":"The absolute funniest tech talk I've seen","text":"The absolute funniest tech talk I've seen \"Steal Everything, Kill Everyone, Cause Total Financial Ruin!:\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":16841048,"author":"nikisweeting","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Listed as a favorite tech talk","quote":"Jayson E. Street: Steal Everything, Kill Everyone, Cause Total Financial Ruin!","text":"Jayson E. Street: Steal Everything, Kill Everyone, Cause Total Financial Ruin! [VIDEO] Van Albert and Banks: Looping Surveillance Cameras through Live Editing [LINK] Ken Thompson: Reflections on Trusting Trust [LINK] And of course, Simple Made Easy, Wat, etc."},{"id":22701321,"author":"will_pseudonym","month":"2020-03","story":"Vulnerability Reporting Is Dysfunctional","stance":"reference","why":"Example of pen testing physical company security","quote":"","text":"They do do that, if I understand correctly. Penetration testing handles the physical security of the company. [DEFCON 19: Steal Everything, Kill Everyone, Cause Total Financial Ruin! ]( [VIDEO] ) Social Engineering [Hacking the Wetware: Compromising Companies with Social Engineering]( [LINK] )"},{"id":20789893,"author":"brianpgordon","month":"2019-08","story":"Apple contractors listened to 1k Siri recordings per shift: former employee","stance":"reference","why":"Red team pros show security melting under social engineering","quote":"you see red team pros talk about how the whole apparatus melts before the gentlest of social engineering","text":"All of that sounds impressive, until you see red team pros talk about how the whole apparatus melts before the gentlest of social engineering- [VIDEO] I'd also be concerned with leaking of mass amounts of records at once due to a security lapse on servers where it's a bit easier for grossly inadequate protections to lurk unnoticed- [LINK] I think I've read that Google doesn't even store the original recordings, but distorts them randomly to make them unrecognizable but still intelligible by their models. That seems like a pretty reasonable way to protect people, provided they're informed and there's no link back to the original account."},{"id":15521841,"author":"ATsch","month":"2017-10","story":"Socially Engineering Myself into High Security Facilities","stance":"reference","why":"Talk that names the jedi wave technique","quote":"refers to as the \"jedi wave","text":"This something that the speaker in the famous \"steal everything, kill everyone...\" [1] defcon talk refers to as the \"jedi wave\" and makes similar remarks about. I think it's a great name. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"QVuU2YCwHjw":{"wy":"HN uses it as a vivid demonstration that Fourier series and epicycles can fit anything, and as a warning about models with too much wiggle room.","q":{"x":"This is my favourite visualisation of this fact","i":12653453,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12653453,"author":"jordigh","month":"2016-10","story":"Opposition to Galileo was scientific, not just religious","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes how epicycles can fit any periodic orbit via Fourier series.","quote":"This is my favourite visualisation of this fact","text":"I tl;dr'ed the article (sorry), but I wanted to say one thing about epicycles. They are not wrong and certainly can be used to explain motion, but the problem with them is that you can keep adding epicycles to fit any continuous orbit at all (this actually is because a Fourier series can converge to any continuous periodic function). This is my favourite visualisation of this fact: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10402019,"author":"JadeNB","month":"2015-10","story":"Fourier visualised","stance":"rec","why":"Reconstructs Homer Simpson's face with Fourier epicycles, a convincing demo of Fourier transforms.","quote":"the reconstruction of Homer Simpson's face","text":"I'm only seeing an image, with no explanation, and it took me a while to realise what it was showing; so, in case anyone else is puzzled by it, each image is the sum of the previous one with a new factor (indicated by the \"wheels upon wheels\"). I think that the reconstruction of Homer Simpson's face (which I think I originally saw thanks to OP ColinWright!) may be a more convincing demonstration of the power of Fourier transforms: [VIDEO] . There's a Nautilus article about it, too: [LINK] (EDIT: and an HN post discussing that: [LINK] )."},{"id":6686137,"author":"TheMakeA","month":"2013-11","story":"Fourier Toy","stance":"rec","why":"Math video that exemplifies love of mathematics.","quote":"These guys are one of the reasons I love mathematics. Check this out too","text":"These guys are one of the reasons I love mathematics. Check this out too: [VIDEO] Edit: Didn't realize that this was also linked to in another thread on the front page as well."},{"id":30270181,"author":"codethief","month":"2022-02","story":"The best proof that the Earth spins","stance":"reference","why":"Impressive example that epicycles are just a Fourier series describing any movement.","quote":"For a very impressive example see","text":"> This is not an assumption, there is no circular reasoning. Of course it is an assumption. A star moving across the sky is not a proof by any means. > And we observe different parallaxes for different stars, meaning that stars are not all on the same distance from earth. No, we don't measure different parallaxes for different stars. We measure different trajectories for different stars across our night sky and then, using the assumption that Earth rotates and that it is on an orbit around the sun, we conclude that there must be a parallax effect involved in the observed trajectory of the star which we can then use to estimate its distance to us. > So, this way you prove that the earth moves and stars have different parallaxes and are not all attached to a crystal sphere. I don't see a problem here. To see that what you're referring to as \"proof\" is no proof at all, consider that Ptolemy's epicycles (i.e. the elaborate version of the crystal spheres) are just a Fourier series and can therefore be used to describe any periodic movement across the sky. (Without you having to know anything about whether Earth rotates or not.) For a very impressive example see [VIDEO] ."},{"id":29030637,"author":"whatshisface","month":"2021-10","story":"Is the great neutrino puzzle pointing to multiple missing particles?","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates fitting with many epicycles, analogous to the elephant fitting principle.","quote":"This isn't an elephant but it demonstrates the principle","text":"This isn't an elephant but it demonstrates the principle: [VIDEO] Epicycles were originally considered a good idea because it takes exactly two circles to make an ellipse, and Kepler's laws were satisfied by such an ellipse. They were \"falsified,\", in the two-cycle form, when closer observations revealed that Kepler's laws were not obeyed exactly. At that point, the choice was to either add more epicycles (working your way up to an elephant), or for Newton to discover universal gravitation."},{"id":26617593,"author":"witherk","month":"2021-03","story":"Intriguing new result from the LHCb experiment at CERN","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates that flexible models can fit anything.","quote":"If your model has a lot of wiggle room it can be consistent with anything.","text":"If your model has a lot of wiggle room it can be consistent with anything. Maybe the solar system really does move in epicycles [VIDEO]"},{"id":12673271,"author":"mannykannot","month":"2016-10","story":"Opposition to Galileo was scientific, not just religious","stance":"reference","why":"Cites the video as showing epicycles can model heliocentric motion.","quote":"","text":"I cannot edit my post any more , but I can still reply to it, and it seems the answer is yes: [VIDEO] - but you need eccentricity to get the speeds right: [LINK] (10 minutes in.) Furthermore, the result is not equivalent to the heliocentric model because it has many more parameters."},{"id":11888593,"author":"jerf","month":"2016-06","story":"Why not string theory? Because enough is enough","stance":"reference","why":"Shows epicycles can be made to draw anything given enough terms.","quote":"with very long series of cycles, you can \"predict\" anything","text":"The characteristic of metaphorical epicycles is that the make simple, testable predictions that are then wrong , which are fixed with another epicycle. Another more subtle aspect of epicycles, real ones this time, is that they are too powerful and can be used to prove anything. You can predict the motion of the planets with epicycles, it's just that the required series is very long or infinite. And with very long series of cycles, you can \"predict\" anything: [VIDEO] Thus, one of the problems with epicycles both real and metaphorical is that they are indeed not refutable. Because epicycles can predict anything, they aren't that useful; they exclude far less than meets the eye at first. It isn't hard to see that characterist showing up in string theory. The theory has for a very long time had problems with excluding possibilities, and each new metaphorical \"epicycle\" seems to come with more parameters than the last, rather than fewer. Now, this isn't unique to string theory since all the current theories seem to have that problem, but then, the point is, why does this problematic theory have so much more support and money than the other problematic theories?"},{"id":9765025,"author":"jordigh","month":"2015-06","story":"Fourier series","stance":"reference","why":"Epicycles drawing Homer Simpson, explained via Fourier series.","quote":"you can also draw Homer Simpson","text":"Huh, so I never though about it this way. The reason you can use epicycles to draw anything is because the trig functions form a complete orthonormal system. Because you can write a Fourier series for any piecewise continuous function, you can also draw Homer Simpson: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5XDTQLa3NjE",1],["bSfe5M_zG2s",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1]]},"bAUVCn_jw5I":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best explanation of why engine start is hard and what can go wrong.","q":{"x":"Here's a 1hr video from the Everyday Astronaut explaining the process and everything that can go wrong.","i":48322298,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48322298,"author":"HarHarVeryFunny","month":"2026-05","story":"The most spectacular rocket explosion since N1 just happened in Florida","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long explanation of engine start and failure modes.","quote":"Here's a 1hr video from the Everyday Astronaut explaining the process and everything that can go wrong.","text":"Starting/igniting a liquid fueled rocket engine is an inherently complex process - everything has to be sequenced just right to get engines chilled, turbo pumps up to speed, any gaseous fuel vented and harmlessly ignited before it build up, ignition of fuel, etc. Here's a 1hr video from the Everyday Astronaut explaining the process and everything that can go wrong. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38321410,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2023-11","story":"SpaceX Loses Starship Spaceraft During Test Flight","stance":"rec","why":"Why rocket engines are hard.","quote":"Everyday Astronaut had a couple of (IMHO) great videos","text":"Everyday Astronaut had a couple of (IMHO) great videos which goes into why rocket engines are hard[1] and the Starship many-engine approach[2] specifically. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":36450189,"author":"frederikvs","month":"2023-06","story":"Full ignition for ESA’s reusable rocket engine","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why igniting a rocket engine is hard.","quote":"For more detail, I can recommend watching \"Why starting a rocket engine is so hard\" by Tim Dodd","text":"I'd say it's pretty impressive. Building and igniting a rocket engine is hard. For more detail, I can recommend watching \"Why starting a rocket engine is so hard\" by Tim Dodd [0]. It's not \"never been done before\" impressive, they're a few years behind e.g. SpaceX's Raptor engine, which uses the same propellants, and is also reusable. But still, a new rocket engine is no small feat - the engine is probably the hardest part of a rocket. This is quite literally rocket science. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["_bDXXWQxK38",1],["qv5b1Xowxdk",1]]},"0lJKucu6HJc":{"wy":"Recommended as the single Startup School video worth watching, a short and dense talk on what makes a startup work; one commenter dismisses it as platitudes.","q":{"x":"If you watch one SUS video ever then watch this 16 minute video from Sam Altman","i":21758613,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21758613,"author":"davej","month":"2019-12","story":"Applications Are Now Open for YC Startup School – Starts in January","stance":"strong_rec","why":"16 minute Altman talk, the one to watch from Startup School","quote":"If you watch one SUS video ever then watch this 16 minute video from Sam Altman","text":"I did the very first Startup School and the most recent Startup School. When I did the first Startup School I made a number of mistakes. The most costly was that I didn't launch early enough. When I did eventually launch my product, I launched with too many features and I found it difficult to explain to my beta testers. It was \"cool\" but my users found that it changed their workflow too much and they didn't want to make the investment to see if it was worthwhile. I got disheartened and gave up. The funny thing was that I was aware that what I was doing was considered \"common startup pitfalls\" even as I was making the mistakes. From talking to other founders, it seems that a lot of us only learn by making the mistake for ourselves. At the most recent Startup School, I started again with a new idea. I was determined to build something that solved a specific problem that was easy-to-explain and I wanted to launch quickly . I'm still very early-on in the journey but I have customers, revenue and growth. I would particularly recommend solo founders to give Startup School a go. As a solo founder, the weekly updates with peers really helped keep me honest with myself. edit: Old (failed) Startup: [LINK] New Startup: [LINK] edit2: If you watch one SUS video ever then watch this 16 minute video from Sam Altman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20255703,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2019-06","story":"Startup idea checklist","stance":"rec","why":"Short Altman talk on what a startup is","quote":"plainly covers why this is so","text":"This is very much my line of thought. I have come to use different terms for these: - Startup is what I now call VC funded companies. A startup can and often does 'make the world better' for some definition of better - A profitable side-business that could grow into a large stable company starts as a side-project/side-business. This short Sam Altman \"How to Succeed with a Startup\" video[0] plainly covers why this is so. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18215863,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2018-10","story":"Android: The word Google didn't say during the Pixel 3 event","stance":"reference","why":"Altman talk on startup advantages over big companies","quote":"Sam said that an advantage that startups have over big corp","text":"I’d heard of it but didn’t know it was open source. Is Fuchsia in a state where you can boot it in a VM? Wikipedia mentioned how to develop applications for it with Dart. Did anyone try developing applications for it? In a talk [1] by Sam Altman I saw on the YC channel on YouTube, Sam said that an advantage that startups have over big corp is that the latter are not able to “turn their battleship around” as fast, developing software for new platforms. Hmmm... (pondering). [1]: I think it was this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25532250,"author":"augustt","month":"2020-12","story":"How to Start a Startup (2014)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"all I hear are mind-numbing platitudes","text":"Maybe I'm not seeing the right material but when I watch stuff like [VIDEO] from him, all I hear are mind-numbing platitudes. Having a \"sensible business model\" might have been my favorite piece of advice from that one."}],"r":[["rgaTLrZGlk0",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["ELeZAKCN4tY",1]]},"9NOncx2jU0Q":{"wy":"HN cites it as an astonishing glimpse of advanced thought-controlled prosthetics.","q":{"x":"This particular thing from Johns Hopkins is just incredible","i":10926348,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10926348,"author":"jmsdnns","month":"2016-01","story":"New book argues we can’t expect new tech to rekindle rapid economic growth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Glimpse of advanced thought-controlled prosthetics","quote":"This particular thing from Johns Hopkins is just incredible","text":"That's a great point. I described the next revolution as possibly one of automation, but that might not be enough. Maybe automation and robotics is better... hmm. This particular thing from Johns Hopkins is just incredible and gives us a glimpse of what might be in 50 years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10408272,"author":"tim333","month":"2015-10","story":"What Would Real Brain-To-Brain Communication Look Like?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how nerves rerouted to the chest control a prosthetic limb","quote":"There's an interesting video explaining a bit","text":"I'm sceptical the school kid project shown in your link actually works significantly. Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory made a real one, I think by surgically moving 'spare' nerves to various points at the inside surface of the patients chest and then putting electrodes on the exterior surface. There's an interesting video explaining a bit: [VIDEO] from [LINK]"},{"id":10928582,"author":"eli_gottlieb","month":"2016-01","story":"New book argues we can’t expect new tech to rekindle rapid economic growth","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Who's going to be able to afford those?","text":">This particular thing from Johns Hopkins is just incredible and gives us a glimpse of what might be in 50 years: [VIDEO] Who's going to be able to afford those?"},{"id":8771633,"author":"mparlane","month":"2014-12","story":"Amputee controls prosthetic arms by thinking about them [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the prosthetic arms are not attached to the amputee","quote":"This part of the video shows the arms are not attached.","text":"Must not have watched the video: [VIDEO] This part of the video shows the arms are not attached. I'm sure he was trained to use the arms when they were not on him first."}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1]]},"CB-SCA1reqE":{"wy":"HN people cite it as a practical test for pan temperature and as the way to get a stainless steel pan relatively non-stick.","q":{"x":"This youtube video explains the process","i":42401025,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":7390589,"author":"abcd_f","month":"2014-03","story":"How a Light Saber Works","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Leidenfrost effect as a test for proper pan temperature","quote":"If you are into cooking, I must insist you check this out -","text":"Leidenfrost effect is how you test if your stainless steel skillet is at the right temperature for cooking. If you are into cooking, I must insist you check this out - [VIDEO]"},{"id":42401025,"author":"jmcclell","month":"2024-12","story":"A simple math error sparked a panic about black plastic kitchen utensils","stance":"rec","why":"How to heat a stainless steel pan so it is relatively non-stick","quote":"This youtube video explains the process","text":"Carbon steel pans are seasoned and generally will be stored with a light coat of oil. You'll add oil while cooking, as well. My carbon steel pans don't stick – an egg will happily glide on the surface. Stainless steel pans are not seasoned, but can still be relatively non-stick as long as they are heated properly prior to use. Heating them closes the pores in the pan's surface, making the surface smoother. Add oil after the pan is properly heated. This youtube video explains the process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32446311,"author":"harrydehal","month":"2022-08","story":"Study finds link between 'forever chemicals' in cookware and liver cancer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is called the \"Leidenfrost effect.","text":"This is called the \"Leidenfrost effect.\" The other possible health downside to doing this in a stainless steel pan, as referenced elsewhere in these comments, is the overheating of certain cooking oils beyond their smokepoint and into temperatures that turn them potentially bad for your health. See also: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23459363,"author":"asguy","month":"2020-06","story":"Maillard reaction","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's pretty common knowledge for professional cooks[1]","text":"This is how I cook them. It's pretty common knowledge for professional cooks[1], they just don't realize what it's called. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"hIZxTQP1ifo":{"wy":"HN points to it as the definitive explanation of why coherence and the orphan rule matter, in debates over Haskell, Rust traits, OCaml modules and Elm.","q":{"x":"Ed Kmett's fantastic \"Typeclasses v. the World\" talk covers","i":10306544,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10306544,"author":"shadytrees","month":"2015-09","story":"Haskell's Type Classes: We Can Do Better","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kmett explains benefits of global coherence for refactoring and reasoning.","quote":"Ed Kmett's fantastic \"Typeclasses v. the World\" talk covers","text":"The point is a little more subtle than this, or how the article made it out to be. Global coherence and confluence (which GHC attempts to enforce unless told otherwise) is great for refactoring and equational reasoning and (yes) mathematics and programming at large, as Ed Kmett's fantastic \"Typeclasses v. the World\" talk covers: [VIDEO] Global uniqueness of instances (which GHC does NOT enforce) is bad for modular programming, or programming at large, which is a point some of the commenters here are making. [LINK] The submitted HN link here has a terrible title. What the author is proposing is enforcing typeclass laws, which is a great idea (but difficult in practice to implement). Practice and time has shown that lawless classes are the ones that people hate, the ones that don't add any benefit and in fact make it harder to reason about large Haskell programs. I hope people don't take away from this link that classes in Haskell are bad. Haskell is three things: pure, functional, and typeclasses. They're part of the identity of Haskell; they're the reason we set off on this wonderful journey into the uncharted oceans of functional programming back in 1990. Typeclasses are AWESOME. And they can be improved. Postscript: Kmett also has a package that persuades the compiler to let you have dynamically generates instances. It has the same user experience as local implicits, which I think a lot of people here are asking for: [LINK] λ> using (Monoid (+) 0) $ mappend mempty 12 12 GHC"},{"id":37281775,"author":"Twey","month":"2023-08","story":"This isn’t the way to speed up Rust compile times","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why alternatives to the orphan rule hurt reasoning.","quote":"Edward Kmett has a great explanation[1] of the considerations around this space.","text":"Edward Kmett has a great explanation[1] of the considerations around this space. It's not that alternatives to the orphan rule are hard to implement, it's that they lead to things being much harder to reason about for the programmer. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42213465,"author":"themk","month":"2024-11","story":"Traits are a local maximum","stance":"reference","why":"Kmett talk believed to cover the topic.","quote":"I believe Ed Kmett covers some of this in his talk","text":"It's been a while now, but I believe Ed Kmett covers some of this in his talk \"Typeclasses vs The World\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11403154,"author":"lomnakkus","month":"2016-04","story":"An OCaml manifesto – Problems and what's needed for widespread adoption","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why global uniqueness matters for higher-level code.","quote":"global uniqueness is pretty essential for sanity when writing higher-level code[1]","text":"AFAIUI modular implicits don't give you global uniqueness. While this can be \"worked around\" in some cases[0], it's not really possible in the general case, and global uniqueness is pretty essential for sanity when writing higher-level code[1]. [0] The paper gives set-union as an example. [1] [VIDEO] . (Sorry for throwing video at you -- there's probably a write-up somewhere, but I'm in a bit of a rush, and this was the thing that came to mind.)"},{"id":11403826,"author":"airless_bar","month":"2016-04","story":"An OCaml manifesto – Problems and what's needed for widespread adoption","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> [1] [VIDEO] Now we just need a Haskell compiler which actually bothers to provide the \"pretty essential for sanity\" for global uniqueness. Currently, I count zero compilers in common use which do."},{"id":10598427,"author":"ablesearcher","month":"2015-11","story":"Making Elm faster and friendlier in 0.16","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue type classes are not incidental complexity.","quote":"","text":"I've seen Evan's talk. Evan confuses the presentation of features with the value of those features. (I suppose one could solve the difficulties people have in learning Mathematics by throwing out everything after Algebra I; another solution, however, might be to do a better job teaching the more difficult stuff.) Just because you shouldn't expose new programmers to advanced concepts on the first day doesn't mean they shouldn't be a part of the language. Similarly, the fact (<~) might confuse someone new to Elm isn't a reason for removing it from the language. I'm sympathetic to the concern that some code is too \"clever\" or dense; but Elm swings the pendulum too far. (Btw, type-classes are not incidental complexity: [LINK] [VIDEO] )"}]},"iqddnwKF8HQ":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a convincing primary-source interview showing Zig's cohesive design vision and the creator's level-headed reasoning.","q":{"x":"convinced me more than any design doc or blogpost could","i":48633488,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48633488,"author":"nilsherzig","month":"2026-06","story":"Pledging Another $400k to the Zig Software Foundation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing interview with Zig's creator on why to learn Zig.","quote":"convinced me more than any design doc or blogpost could","text":"If you're unsure about spending the time to learn Zig, I really recommend watching the following interview with the creator of Zig [VIDEO] convinced me more than any design doc or blogpost could"},{"id":48894141,"author":"randusername","month":"2026-07","story":"Zig Creator Calls Spade a Spade, Anthropic Blows Smoke","stance":"rec","why":"Interview explaining the Zig Software Foundation's AI stance; informative primary source.","quote":"are interesting and informative.","text":"TFA has an odd perspective. > Views are my own. I have no history with Zig. I’ve never spoken to Andrew Kelley There are better primary sources to explain what ZSF is trying to achieve with its AI stance. Kelley's rant [0] was a bit much but the ZSF AI Policy [1] and Kelley's interview on it [2] are interesting and informative. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48423825,"author":"norman784","month":"2026-06","story":"Zig Zen Update","stance":"rec","why":"Interview showing Zig's cohesive vision from its creator.","quote":"I recommend you to watch Andrew Kelly interview","text":"I recommend you to watch Andrew Kelly interview[0], while I'm not the target audience of Zig, I don't see him driving away any user. Also Jai as for now is a non existing language, just a selected few has access to it, but Jai approach is a kitchen sink, from what I saw it is all over the place in terms of features, now Zig vision feels cohesive. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48447314,"author":"Erenay09","month":"2026-06","story":"Zig by Example","stance":"rec","why":"Interview where Andrew Kelley describes Zig's design philosophy.","quote":"I’d prefer that you watch this video interview with Andrew Kelley.","text":"I’d prefer that you watch this video interview with Andrew Kelley. He is describing that. [VIDEO] Personal thoughts: I think Zig will become more stable once 1.0 tag released. Currently I cant find a fix to my error in net. But the folks on Zig Discord server helping me out."},{"id":48729039,"author":"auraham","month":"2026-06","story":"Zig – SPIR-V Backend Progress","stance":"rec","why":"Kelley explains Zig features, compares with C, Rust, Go, and why he created it.","quote":"It is a really interesting interview.","text":"I have the same feeling about Zig. In this interview [1], Andrew Kelley, creator of Zig, explains a lot of the features of the language; compares it against c, rust, go; and explains why he created the language. According to him, the killer feature of zig is the tool chain (compiler, linker, build system) since it has no dependencies. So, it will work in any OS/target you choose. It is a really interesting interview. However, Zig code is a bit hard for me to read. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48845174,"author":"pornel","month":"2026-07","story":"My Thoughts on the Bun Rust Rewrite","stance":"reference","why":"Interview showing Kelley as chill and level-headed.","quote":"where he seemed much more chill and level-headed.","text":"I've watched an interview with Andrew Kelley: [VIDEO] where he seemed much more chill and level-headed."},{"id":48339265,"author":"latexr","month":"2026-05","story":"Zig: Build System Reworked","stance":"reference","why":"Andrew Kelley addresses Zig's unused variable errors.","quote":"Andrew Kelly on that","text":"> It doesnt let you have unused variables Andrew Kelly on that: [VIDEO] Looks like you might be able to get rid of the error easily with annotations."}],"r":[["ELeZAKCN4tY",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"oAVjF_7ensg":{"wy":"Recommended as a way to grasp the scale of the universe and what the Hubble deep field image means.","q":{"x":"This [0] IMHO gives more perspective","i":3758305,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3758305,"author":"lloeki","month":"2012-03","story":"The universe is big. This big.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"3D flythrough of Hubble Ultra Deep Field gives perspective on cosmic scale","quote":"This [0] IMHO gives more perspective","text":"This [0] IMHO gives more perspective (don't be taken aback by the flashy images, the good stuff comes after, ~2:50 mark) When watching, remember this is when looking at the absolute darkest spot of our sky. [0] Hubble Ultra Deep Field in 3D [VIDEO] PS: Inevitably, the title reminded me of this: Space is big. You just won't believe how vastly, hugely, mind- bogglingly big it is. I mean, you may think it's a long way down the road to the chemist's, but that's just peanuts to space. -- Douglas Adams, The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy"},{"id":17168859,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2018-05","story":"Ask HN: What's one book that changed your life?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"But I would say that other media also had an impact.","text":"Maybe not one, but 3 books: Into the Wild (book and movie): [LINK] When I was in high school I was considering the same path as Christopher took, but then I heard about him and I learned from his story. \"Happiness only real when shared\" Born to Run: A Hidden Tribe, Superathletes, and the Greatest Race the World Has Never Seen [LINK] This is how I got really hooked up to triathlon and ultramarathons. Great book, I lent it to many people and changed some of their lives too. Learning PHP and MySQL: [LINK] The book is ok, not great, but it changed my life. This is how I learned to program. But I would say that other media also had an impact. Like poems: \"If—\" by Rudyard Kipling: [LINK] Then there are some short videos: The Race: [LINK] Godspeed: [LINK] \"The Hubble Ultra Deep Field\" by Deep Astronomy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32064056,"author":"lovehashbrowns","month":"2022-07","story":"Deepest infrared image of universe","stance":"reference","why":"Explainer video that educates millions on what the Hubble image means","quote":"help educate people on what the picture means and why it's significant.","text":"You're taking all the context out of the two pictures. These pictures are not viewed in isolation. This post is full of comments of people asking questions about things like the gravitational lensing. And the picture will then end up in videos like these [VIDEO] which gather millions of views and which help educate people on what the picture means and why it's significant."}],"r":[["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["ZfuierUvx1A",1],["AD5aAd8Oy84",1],["3GVO-MfIl1Q",1],["msN87y-iEx0",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["p7bzE1E5PMY",1],["xuCn8ux2gbs",1],["l3whaviTqqg",1],["7ImvlS8PLIo",1]]},"w-I6XTVZXww":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard reference for the -1/12 meme, with commenters split between enjoying it and calling it a cheat.","q":{"x":"Watch this entertaining Numberphile video about this topic","i":7057102,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7075185,"author":"alternize","month":"2014-01","story":"1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + ... = - 1/12","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining simple proof of the sum, with bonus advanced video","quote":"you should definitely subscribe to numberphile","text":"this infinite sum was featured in a numberphile video a few days ago with a entraining and simple \"proof\": [VIDEO] and a more advanced explanation & proof in the bonus-video: [LINK] you should definitely subscribe to numberphile ( [LINK] ) and their sister channel computerphile ( [LINK] ) on youtube :)"},{"id":28768820,"author":"barbs","month":"2021-10","story":"Conterintuitive facts in mathematics, CS, and physics","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of the -1/12 result","quote":"This is a good video explaining it","text":"This is a good video explaining it: [VIDEO] Although it relies on a particular interpretation of the left side of the equation, it's used in some areas of physics."},{"id":7057102,"author":"yelnatz","month":"2014-01","story":"1 + 2 + 3 + .. = -1/12","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining Numberphile explanation of the -1/12 sum","quote":"Watch this entertaining Numberphile video about this topic","text":"Watch this entertaining Numberphile video about this topic: [VIDEO] Apparently it's used in many areas in Physics."},{"id":25856259,"author":"9000","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Anyone know any funny programming jokes?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the -1/12 joke's origin","quote":"It's referencing a theorem from string theory, as discussed in this Numberphile video","text":"It's referencing a theorem from string theory, as discussed in this Numberphile video: [VIDEO] The catch is that this isn't valid in what we consider standard mathematics, and you can find many discussions of this online, but this one is fairly short and straightforward: [LINK]"},{"id":24706111,"author":"jyriand","month":"2020-10","story":"How I remember what I learn","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Turns out the proof in the video is incorrect or at least not concise enough","text":"Few days back I stumbled upon a video about Ramanujan [0] and the: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 ..(to infinity) = -1/12.(Turns out the proof in the video is incorrect or at least not concise enough, because you can't do that with divergent series). So that got me curious and I wanted to understand what's going on. How can 1 + 2 .. etc equal to -1/12 and not infinity. So I went on small binge watching videos to get an intuition and understanding about this. This led me to Riemann Hypothesis and Euler's identities and complex numbers etc. Now I have a list of books that I want to skim/read to get a historical context around these problems. I don't take any notes just yet, because I don't know what is important and what is not, I just jump into the stuff and hope that when I come out of the other end I will at least have a understanding why it equals -1/12. It's like panning out gold, there might be some gold nuggets. And maybe, just by pure chance, I come up with some ridiculous ideas how to disprove Riemann Hypothesis ;P (I think I have to revisit Gödel, Escher, Bach and Gödel's Incompletness Theorems to prove that it's not provable). - [0] [VIDEO] EDIT: Sorry to all facepalming mathematicians who happen to read this comment."},{"id":15830070,"author":"ctchocula","month":"2017-12","story":"Advances in number theory inspired by physics","stance":"reference","why":"Where commenter first learned of the sum","quote":"I learned about it through this Numberphile video","text":"I learned about it through this Numberphile video: [VIDEO] There's also a nice write-up from Terry Tao: [LINK]"},{"id":15202484,"author":"hencq","month":"2017-09","story":"The Equifax breach may be the worst leak of personal info ever","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions Numberphile channel as home of Brady Haran","quote":"","text":"There are a lot. I thought this one was really funny [0]. This one I found very interesting [1]. Somewhat different, is the Hello Internet [2] podcast, which is by Grey and Brady Haran who you might know from the Numberphile youtube channel [3]. It's basically the 2 of them chatting about random stuff, but I find it very entertaining. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37357666,"author":"OldGuyInTheClub","month":"2023-09","story":"A Duodecade of Numberphile","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The 1+2+3+4+... = -1/12 video still seems a bit of a cheat, though!","text":"I look forward to every Numberphile (and Sixty Symbols and Deep Sky and ...) video. I am pleased to hear that Brady's questions are off-the-cuff. They're so on-point that I thought they may have been discussed with the guest beforehand. The 1+2+3+4+... = -1/12 video still seems a bit of a cheat, though! [VIDEO]"},{"id":32223689,"author":"DemocracyFTW2","month":"2022-07","story":"Why falling birthrates might be a bigger deal than dominants narratives imply","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"You are right and I am glad I didn't write 'because Maths'. The sum you're quoting from is monotonically growing but at an ever shrinking clip. However, the series does not describe the kind of growth that our economy seemingly needs and our politicians like to promise, nor does it AFAICS describe the OP's idea of continued population growth given his recommendation to shun shrinking populations with fertility below replacement rate; theirs is a constant or in the midterm increasing growth and notwithstanding [minority opinions]( [VIDEO] ) claiming that 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + ... equals -1/12 such a series cannot have a finite sum, hence is physically unfeasible to represent."},{"id":9640129,"author":"j2kun","month":"2015-06","story":"Goals and use cases for a system that stores, queries, and manipulates equations","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the sum of natural numbers being -1/12, perpetuated with reckless abandon in this video","text":"I love the idea, but I am concerned that if run by and for physicists, it will perpetuate the lack of rigor and \"shorthands\" physicists use to abuse mathematics [1]. I would propose an additional feature: that entries be highlighted in an additional way if they are mathematically false as written (or misleadingly overloaded, or just a huge abuse). [1] e.g. the sum of natural numbers being -1/12, perpetuated with reckless abandon in this video [VIDEO] to the dismay of mathematicians everywhere. I also have personal gripes with the way physicists use delta functions, and hear many physicists say (about some topic in mathematics), \"I don't want to actually do the mathematics here, I just want to get intuition about it.\""},{"id":8718565,"author":"asdfjkln","month":"2014-12","story":"Almost all numbers contain the digit “3” [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The worst offender with \"magic\" is the one with the Riemann zeta function","text":"I think the quality of numberphile videos vary quite considerably. The one you linked is a rather honest explanation of the idea of comparing the size of sets through bijections and a good explanation of Cantors diagonal argument. As a mathematician I'm quite fine with it. The one linked in the submission is more of an obvious statement hidden by obscure use of language. Slightly silly, but not very bad. The worst offender with \"magic\" is the one with the Riemann zeta function [1], which went viral a while ago. The problem here is that they get people started off on the wrong foot, confusing them with wrong arguments and hidden definitions. Now, if people really want to understand why this can be made meaningful, you first have to explain that the better portion of the video is absolutely wrong, and only then can you explain what is actually going on. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["k7X7sZzSXYs",1]]},"07o-TASvIxY":{"wy":"Pointed to in helmet-law debates as a watchable argument that helmet mandates may cost lives by discouraging cycling.","q":{"x":"If you would rather watch than read, there's this","i":12595245,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12595245,"author":"Dove","month":"2016-09","story":"Study Explains Why We Arrest Moms for Putting Kids in Nearly Non-Existent Danger","stance":"rec","why":"Watchable introduction to why helmets may do more harm than good.","quote":"If you would rather watch than read, there's this","text":"If this is a new topic to you (I don't mean, you , SturgeonsLaw, as you are clearly familiar with it :) -- I mean the reader), you would do well to consider both sides. I personally find the arguments that bike helmets cause more harm than good persuasive, and a good and thorough introduction may be found here: [LINK] If you would rather watch than read, there's this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12334927,"author":"m0nty","month":"2016-08","story":"Guerrilla Bike Lanes in San Francisco","stance":"rec","why":"Argues helmets distract from poor road design and unsafe driving.","quote":"Anyone just blindly downvoting snovv_crash should check out the video above","text":"[VIDEO] Anyone just blindly downvoting snovv_crash should check out the video above and any number of other resources, such as this one: [LINK] There are strong feelings on both sides, but many cyclists feel helmets are a distraction from the real issues which make cyclists unsafe: poor road design and unsafe driving. Cycling itself is (counter-intuitively) a very safe activity which looks dangerous; everyone assumes driving and walking are safe but often they are not. Yet nobody seriously proposes helmets for pedestrians or drivers, just cyclists."},{"id":40243801,"author":"m463","month":"2024-05","story":"Cardio fitness is a strong, consistent predictor of morbidity and mortality","stance":"reference","why":"Talk arguing helmet laws kill more through lost fitness.","quote":"There was a ted talk that said bicycle helmet laws would kill more people than save.","text":"reminds me of the bicycle helmet stuff. There was a ted talk that said bicycle helmet laws would kill more people than save. The reasoning was preventing people from riding would also prevent increased fitness, and more lives were lost from that than saved from accidents. EDIT: I think this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38912582,"author":"m463","month":"2024-01","story":"Value of life","stance":"reference","why":"Talk arguing helmet laws may cost lives through reduced exercise.","quote":"helmet laws might be costing lives","text":"reminds me of the ted talk where the speaker said that helmet laws might be costing lives. The idea was that more people would be saved by the opportunity to exercise than were saved by the helmets. I think this one? [VIDEO]"},{"id":12574734,"author":"ownagefool","month":"2016-09","story":"Study Explains Why We Arrest Moms for Putting Kids in Nearly Non-Existent Danger","stance":"reference","why":"TED talk disputing that helmets are safer.","quote":"TED talk discussing the issues","text":"There are studies and talks that would suggest the opposite is true. For example:- For example: [LINK] TED talk discussing the issues: [VIDEO] Common sense would dictate that the helmet is safer, but we could argue the science leaves a lot to be desired."},{"id":4594287,"author":"cshesse","month":"2012-09","story":"To Encourage Biking, Cities Lose the Helmets","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a factor in whether helmet requirements reduce injuries.","quote":"There are multiple factors that could affect this, here are two such things","text":"When I was a kid, my best friend's brother died because he was not wearing a bike helmet. I do, in fact, always wear a bike helmet. The argument is not whether, if hit in the head, you are more likely to get a head injury. It is clear that bike helmets reduce those injuries. The question is, if we required everyone to wear helmets, or required everyone to not wear helmets, which would result in less injuries/fatalities. And secondarily, if it turns out that bike helmets reduce injuries and fatalities, has it been worth the cost, vs other things we could spend money on, say legally required non-slip surfaces for bathtubs. There are multiple factors that could affect this, here are two such things: * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":37472688,"author":"satellite2","month":"2023-09","story":"In Germany, 27 are in 'preventive detention' b/c they might do climate protests","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The illusion of safety prevents people getting armed to their teeth","text":"The illusion of safety prevents people getting armed to their teeth and as such participate to their safety. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XuBdf9jYj7o",1]]},"1YbcB6b4A2U":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible overview of Kernel's technology whenever its brain-sensing helmets come up.","q":{"x":"this recent podcast episode with the CEO is a good start","i":27569287,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27888701,"author":"indigodaddy","month":"2021-07","story":"I Fired Myself","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast on Johnson's brain monitoring device","quote":"I watched the video first then poked around on Google a little and found his webpage.","text":"Following blog post talks more in detail about his meals/daily diet: [LINK] See also where he talks about this a little bit with Lex Fridman. I watched the video first then poked around on Google a little and found his webpage. [LINK] Full podcast which is more about Johnson’s brain monitoring device: [VIDEO] Note I don’t have any affiliation with Johnson or Kernel, just enjoy Fridman’s podcasts."},{"id":27569287,"author":"meowface","month":"2021-06","story":"Mind reading technology: helmet aims to unlock brain's secrets","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of what Kernel's devices can do today and planned","quote":"this recent podcast episode with the CEO is a good start","text":">No one cares about someone’s improvised broad generalizations and “one day it’ll be great” patronizing speeches. I was just matching the patronizing, brusque tone in your original post. >The question was what’s it good for as it is today, based on people with knowledge about it. I apologize, then, because I don't think that was clear from your post, which contained only the text \"So what is this tech good for in the first place.\" If your question were \"So what are the device's current capabilities?\" or something similar, then I would've responded differently. >In particular the only thing you demonstrated here is that you have absolutely no knowledge about it. I do, actually. Your question (ending in a period) came across as extremely condescending and dismissive, and as if you were questioning the value of BCIs in general. So my goal with that post was to try to make an argument for the potential of what BCI tech is good for in the first place, as I interpreted your post. If you want an overview of what their company's devices can do today and what's planned, this recent podcast episode with the CEO is a good start: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37928823,"author":"Obertr","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: AI Mind Cloning, Are We There Yet?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A few links which can helped me dive into the topic rewind wearable device rewind.ai/pendant \"Her\" movie imdb.com/title/tt1798709/ Neuralink neuralink.com/ Bryan Johnson: Kernel Brain-Computer Interfaces [VIDEO] The Diamond Age by Neal Stephenson"},{"id":27543948,"author":"sxp","month":"2021-06","story":"Mind reading technology: helmet aims to unlock brain's secrets","stance":"reference","why":"Bryan Johnson discusses Kernel's Flow helmet and its IR sensing","quote":"The most interesting thing is that one of their helmets, Flow, is using IR backscatter sensing","text":"Johnson was on the Lex Fridman podcast recent and discussed the helmet there: [VIDEO] The most interesting thing is that one of their helmets, Flow, is using IR backscatter sensing rather than the electric or magnetic sensing that most other BCI devices (including their other helmet, Flux) use. The system is still pricey at $50-100k or $3.6k/mo, but they're also planning on selling a simplified version at $5k: [LINK]"}],"r":[["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["JLb5hZLw44s",1],["F6FR08VylO4",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["UANN2Eu6ZnM",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1],["4hZxo96x48A",1],["HBluLfX2F_k",1]]},"4Vw6uF2LdZw":{"wy":"HN people offer it as a free-market counterpoint to the standard robber baron narrative.","q":{"x":"Burton Folsom on the myth of robber barons and the great american entrepreneurs of the 19th century.","i":22702858,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22702858,"author":"rollinDyno","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: Share your favourite talks on YouTube","stance":"rec","why":"Folsom on robber barons myth and 19th century entrepreneurs.","quote":"Burton Folsom on the myth of robber barons and the great american entrepreneurs of the 19th century.","text":"Recently I have really enjoyed: * Joseph Stiglitz on the introduction of informational mechanisms in Economics [1] * Burton Folsom on the myth of robber barons and the great american entrepreneurs of the 19th century. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8034786,"author":"timtas","month":"2014-07","story":"Free markets killed capitalism","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as an antidote to the article's thesis.","quote":"Here's at least a bit of an antidote","text":"Sigh. Ideologues drinking and re-drinking their own bathwater. The thesis of this article is nearly opposite of the truth. Capitalism -- the kind we have evolved (call it socio-crony-capitalism) -- has badly wounded free markets. Here's at least a bit of an antidote: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14249586,"author":"civilian","month":"2017-05","story":"Why Chinatown Produce Is Cheap (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Supports view that damaging monopolies come from government power.","quote":"The really damaging monopolies only ever come from government-granted powers.","text":"You might get something out of this article: [LINK] If \" Capitalism in America means as close to monopolization and price gouging as possible. \" is your definition of capitalism, then you're off. (And I think that may be why you're being disagreed/downvoted on.) If you think that monopolization and price gouging is an emergent property of capitalism in America-- well, then, I wanna see some citations. My (radical, libertarian) view that a free market would only leads to monopolies when they're _market monopolies_-- meaning they've already providing the best service, and will continue to do so. The really damaging monopolies only ever come from government-granted powers. [VIDEO]"}]},"8T6ClX-y2AE":{"wy":"HN uses it as the primary source for Microsoft's Rust adoption and its reasoning on TPM and security requirements.","q":{"x":"The speaker covers a bunch of areas and the final part of the talk (around 10 minutes) is about Microsoft introducing Rust in some self-contained areas in Windows.","i":35739300,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42949206,"author":"Hawxy","month":"2025-02","story":"Microsoft deletes official Windows 11 CPU/TPM bypass for unsupported PCs","stance":"rec","why":"Microsoft's thinking around Windows security","quote":"There's a pretty interesting video from 2023 that goes through much of Microsoft's thoughts around Windows security.","text":"> Security There's a pretty interesting video from 2023 that goes through much of Microsoft's thoughts around Windows security. It flew under the radar unfortunately: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35737239,"author":"senectus1","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft is busy rewriting core Windows library code in memory-safe Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long announcement including adminless Windows","quote":"its about an hour long but much of it is very interesting.","text":"[VIDEO] This is the announcement video. its about an hour long but much of it is very interesting. \"Adminless windows\" sounds very interesting."},{"id":35739300,"author":"nindalf","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft is to enable Rust use for Windows 11 kernel","stance":"rec","why":"Rust in font parser and Win32k, plus killing bug classes","quote":"The speaker covers a bunch of areas and the final part of the talk (around 10 minutes) is about Microsoft introducing Rust in some self-contained areas in Windows.","text":"Link to the talk - [VIDEO] (timestamped to the part about Rust) The speaker covers a bunch of areas and the final part of the talk (around 10 minutes) is about Microsoft introducing Rust in some self-contained areas in Windows. Some highlights: - Their focus is on \"killing bug classes\". More context in this post by Microsoft Research from 2019 - A proactive approach to more secure code. - They want to do this with memory safe languages, CPU architectural changes and safer language subsets. This talk focussed on memory safe languages, specifically Rust. - First area they've introduced Rust in - a cross platform rewrite of a font parser called DWriteCore. The team reported that parsing was \"incredibly easy\". Font shaping performance increased by 5-15% compared to the C++ version. - It took about 2 devs working for half a year to complete this. The speaker says this is pretty good value for an area that is notorious for security bugs. - Second area is the REGION data type in Win32k GDI. Currently in consumer Windows, disabled by feature flag. Will be enabled in insider builds soon. Performance has been good, some small wins for the Rust version. - There is now a Windows SysCall implemented in completed safe Rust. TLDR - Rust is inside the Windows Kernel, will be enabled widely soon."},{"id":35759139,"author":"Hawxy","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft quietly supported legislation to make it easier to fix devices","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Microsoft requires modern security CPUs","quote":"There's a pretty great talk from Bluehat a few days ago that goes into why Microsoft is doing this","text":"> just to make existing CPUs obsolete and keep Intel in business. Bizarre take. It's not random, it's specifically CPUs that support modern hardware security mitigations. There's a pretty great talk from Bluehat a few days ago that goes into why Microsoft is doing this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38859148,"author":"shortsunblack","month":"2024-01","story":"Bitwarden Heist – How to break into password vaults without using passwords","stance":"reference","why":"Explains filesystem restriction for apps in Windows","quote":"a new filesystem driver that transparently restricts file system access to app","text":"Microsoft is trying to do that with msix and a new filesystem driver that transparently restricts file system access to app. Should land into Windows 11 this year. See [VIDEO] for the functionality explaination."},{"id":38067017,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-10","story":"The Windows installer of ImageMagick will no longer be signed","stance":"reference","why":"Windows default security direction","quote":"Security is coming anyway, unless you stay on old Windows versions.","text":"Security is coming anyway, unless you stay on old Windows versions. \"BlueHat IL 2023 - Default Security\" [VIDEO] \"Modernize your Win32 application for security and privacy\" [LINK]"},{"id":36061442,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-05","story":"Win32 App Isolation","stance":"reference","why":"Context on bringing UWP security model to Win32","quote":"This is the continuation of bringing UWP security model into Win32","text":"This is the continuation of bringing UWP security model into Win32, and make Windows security features all enabled by default. See BlueHat IL talk on the matter, [VIDEO] There are no plans for Windows 10, beyond security fixes until 2025, it is done. EDIT: There is a BUILD 2023 talk on the matter as well. [LINK]"},{"id":35730426,"author":"mustache_kimono","month":"2023-04","story":"BlueHat IL 2023: Microsoft rewriting core Windows libraries in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Microsoft rewriting Windows components in Rust with performance gains","quote":"2 devs, 6 months to rewrite 152KLOC C++ to 90KLOC Rust re dwrite","text":"Rust discussion starts at: [VIDEO] Highlights: * 2 projects: dwrite and Win32k * 2 devs, 6 months to rewrite 152KLOC C++ to 90KLOC Rust re dwrite * Performance increases of 5-15% re dwrite * Only about 10% of Win32k code is unsafe * No perf regressions re Win32k * Syscall in the Windows kernel now written in Rust"},{"id":35736358,"author":"pohl","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft is busy rewriting core Windows library code in memory-safe Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Microsoft engineers talking about Rust adoption","quote":"Listen to them talking about it.","text":"Listen to them talking about it. This isn’t the sound of rogue group of developers focused on creating job security for themselves [VIDEO]"},{"id":35739811,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft is to enable Rust use for Windows 11 kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Announcement of signed binaries and admin levels","quote":"Windows is in the process of requiring signed binaries, and two admin levels like on macOS.","text":"Which is why if you watch the talk where this was announced, Windows is in the process of requiring signed binaries, and two admin levels like on macOS. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35741932,"author":"mustache_kimono","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft is to enable Rust use for Windows 11 kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Primary source on Rust in Windows","quote":"The primary source material is this talk","text":"> BlueHat IL 2023: Microsoft rewriting core Windows libraries in Rust (youtube.com) 89 points by mustache_kimono 23 hours ago | past | 45 comments The primary source material is this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35767567,"author":"pl4nty","month":"2023-04","story":"Microsoft quietly supported legislation to make it easier to fix devices","stance":"reference","why":"Weston explains the TPM decision","quote":"David Weston was very involved in the TPM decision, and spoke about it here","text":"David Weston was very involved in the TPM decision, and spoke about it here: [VIDEO] After msft's experiences with Win10, they decided Windows wouldn't become more secure unless they enabled features by default. And the majority of high-impact features depend on a TPM. I'd argue that home users do need these features in the current security environment. TPMs have been required in new devices since 2016, so people are more likely to have thrown out incompatible devices already (CPU/RAM if not disk)"}],"r":[["rStL7niR7gs",1],["UjZYMI1zB9s",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1]]},"EP9F-AZezCU":{"wy":"HN uses it to argue that mechanical difficulty and limited attention are integral to RTS strategy, especially in StarCraft AI threads.","q":{"x":"day[9] (ex Broodwar pro, current caster of many games) probably makes the argument better than I can here","i":14973172,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14973172,"author":"mattb314","month":"2017-08","story":"DeepMind and Blizzard Open StarCraft II as an AI Research Environment","stance":"rec","why":"Day9 argues repetitive RTS mechanics make attention a strategic resource.","quote":"day[9] (ex Broodwar pro, current caster of many games) probably makes the argument better than I can here","text":"I think the idea of spending to upgrade your helper AI sounds really interesting and potentially novel, but it's important to push back a little bit on the idea of \"removing all the rote\" from an RTS. day[9] (ex Broodwar pro, current caster of many games) probably makes the argument better than I can here: [VIDEO] , but one of the important implications of having repetitive aspects in an RTS is that they turn the player's attention into a resource. You can either concentrate on microing your units to squeeze the most possible value out of them, or you can focus on hitting every single production cycle back at base, but you can't do both at the same time, so deciding when to focus on what becomes part of the game. Additionally, it allows for some crazy \"overcoming the odds\" scenarios where a small number of units can pull off a surprising win because the player was willing to donate far more attention to them than usual. Basically, an RTS where you don't feel like you have too much to do all at once is really more like a turn based strategy game (which can be fun too)."},{"id":14222724,"author":"hkmurakami","month":"2017-04","story":"A History of Starcraft AI Competitions","stance":"rec","why":"Day9 argues mechanical difficulty is integral to RTS strategy.","quote":"Broodwar's mechanical difficulty being an integral part of what makes it a great \"realtime\" strategy game","text":"This reminds me of the recent Day[9] rant about Broodwar's mechanical difficulty being an integral part of what makes it a great \"realtime\" strategy game, and that the mechanics are inseparable from the strategy part of the game. [1] His analogy of taking out mechanics from Broodwar being equivalent to taking out athleticism from Football was particularly insightful. BW AI (in its culmination) to me is what Broodwar looks like when you take out the mechanics from the game and make it 100% about strategy and the execution of said strategy. It's really fascinating to see the differences between AI play and human bounded play. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20406738,"author":"Nuzzerino","month":"2019-07","story":"DeepMind’s StarCraft II agent will play anonymously on battle.net","stance":"reference","why":"Day9 on Brood War's greater micro dependence versus SC2.","quote":"Brood War is much more dependent on micro than Starcraft 2.","text":"Brood War is much more dependent on micro than Starcraft 2. Sean \"Day[9]\" Plott has mentioned this a lot in his videos. You can only select 12 units at a time and cannot select more than one building in Brood War. And there is a bit of latency in Brood War that doesn't exist in SC2. One such video (skip to about 4:30 for opinions on mechanics): [VIDEO]"}]},"NcUJnmBqHTY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best overview of Cursorless, likened to vim with your voice, and relevant to accessibility and voice coding discussions.","q":{"x":"If you want a general overview of cursorless, along with seeing what it looks like in practice, the talk from Strange Loop this year was great","i":38221949,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48117584,"author":"chamomeal","month":"2026-05","story":"AI Pointer from DeepMind","stance":"rec","why":"A voice-controlled text editing language, like vim with your voice.","quote":"It's kinda like vim with your voice. Super cool talk here","text":"Kinda related, but this reminded me of the guy who made a voice controlled text editing language. It's kinda like vim with your voice. Super cool talk here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38221949,"author":"citruscomputing","month":"2023-11","story":"Cursorless is alien magic from the future – Xe Iaso","stance":"rec","why":"General overview of Cursorless with a look at it in practice.","quote":"If you want a general overview of cursorless, along with seeing what it looks like in practice, the talk from Strange Loop this year was great","text":"If you want a general overview of cursorless, along with seeing what it looks like in practice, the talk from Strange Loop this year was great: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38814838,"author":"sargstuff","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Text-to-speech recommendations for code editors?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"treesitter[1] is one behind the scenes approach to 'blocking' text to reduced/hi-light what is under review. VSCode is one program that makes use of treesitter. Related alternate discussions of editors using treesitter aerial.neovim[2] / outline.vim[3]. One can setup voice equivalents of macro's to do the verbal equivalent of regular expression(s) to search/sound out tagged list of sections of interest to further voice in detail. Cursorless[4][5][6] AI assistant with jetbrains[7] speach related editing sources: \"Ask HN: What are the best / most accessible languages for blind programmers?\" covers some ideas.[0] ---- [0] : [LINK] [1] : [LINK] [2] : [LINK] [3] : [LINK] [4] : Cursorless talk : [VIDEO] [5] Cursorless : [LINK] [6] : cursorless examples : [LINK] / [LINK] [7] : [LINK]"},{"id":37880467,"author":"pokeyrule","month":"2023-10","story":"Cursorless: A spoken language for editing code [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses Cursorless through the lens of programming language paradigms.","quote":"I talk a bit about Cursorless from the perspective of programming language paradigms starting at 31:30","text":"Ok awesome thanks I kept waiting for someone to call me on this one and I was starting to worry that no one would :). I don't believe it's Turing complete yet, so I think you may be right to call me on it. Kind of depends how you define \"programming language\", I guess. For what it's worth, I talk a bit about Cursorless from the perspective of programming language paradigms starting at 31:30 ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":37989364,"author":"sargstuff","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: What are the best / most accessible languages for blind programmers?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"=== unicorn[0] fits in the 'easy to keep everything in memory' mental model language department without loosing expressiveness/power. language documentation page[3]. --- very high level / 6th generation level language. Makes extensive use of generators for doing goal directed programming. === Cursorless is spoken editor[1]. Throat mike useful in cubical office.[2] --- type one thing. speak another. X2 productivity. === [0] : [LINK] --- [1] : [VIDEO] --- [2] : [LINK] --- [3] : [LINK] --- ==="},{"id":38037065,"author":"sargstuff","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Do I need a special keyboard layout to learn array programming?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Cursorless[3] uses spoken language to edit code.","text":"Traditional physical keyboard options: * Spend $$ for apl specific keyboard [0] * Standard keyboard modifications: use macros/insert unicode chacters via keyboard[1]; stickers[2], change keycaps. --- \" ... another way?\" : Most things in life can be approached in different ways (doesn't mean it's practical / realistic for given situation). \"setting up keyobard layout\": No, but do need to pick a specific method/approach to use. Some examples: a) Cursorless[3] uses spoken language to edit code. b) Using and IDE/editor that does apl auto complete [4] c) Pick from on-screen keyboard doesn't require a physical keyboard setup. Knowing the layout of the virutal screen keyboard may help to quickly find/narrow down the key(s) for selection (eyeball, mouse) --------- Above results found with basic web search. --------- [0] : [LINK] [1] :a) [LINK] :b) [LINK] :c) [LINK] :d) [LINK] [2] : [LINK] [3] : cursorless (audio editor) : [VIDEO] [4] : [LINK] ***"}],"r":[["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1]]},"TPuJsgyu87U":{"wy":"HN cites it as the language creator's honest account of Julia's weaknesses, useful for anyone evaluating the language.","q":{"x":"A good opportunity to point out this Juliacon 2019 video: What's Bad About Julia ( ) TL;DR: Julia is not that bad.","i":20731500,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":47095503,"author":"seertaak","month":"2026-02","story":"Show HN: A physically-based GPU ray tracer written in Julia","stance":"rec","why":"Julia designer on compile-time and typechecking problems with type unification","quote":"There's an excellent video about it by the designer of the language, Jeff Bezanson --","text":"Just to clarify the above: 1. I use the term \"performance\" slightly vaguely. It's comprised of two distinct things: the time it takes to compile the code, and the execution time. The issue is the compilation time: there are certain cases where it's exponential in the number of types which could unify with the callsite's type params. 2. IIRC, Julia compiler has heuristics to ensure things don't explode for common cases. If I'm not mistaken, not only do compile times explode, but certain (very common) things don't even typecheck. There's an excellent video about it by the designer of the language, Jeff Bezanson -- [VIDEO] . Note: Julia experts, please correct me if this has been fixed. 3. The difficulty in intuiting which combinations of types will unify at a given callsite isn't theoretical; there are reports of libraries which unexpectedly fail to work together. I want to qualify this statement: Julia is light years ahead of any language lacking multimethods when it comes to library composability. But my guess is that those problems would be reduced with non-symmetric multimethods. 4. The non-symmetric multimethod system I'm \"proposing\" isn't my idea. They are referred to variously as encapsulated or parasitic multimethods. See [LINK] I have huge respect for Jeff Bezanson, for the record!"},{"id":20731500,"author":"chappi42","month":"2019-08","story":"Giving up on Julia (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Julia's creator candidly covers the language's weaknesses","quote":"A good opportunity to point out this Juliacon 2019 video: What's Bad About Julia ( ) TL;DR: Julia is not that bad.","text":"A good opportunity to point out this Juliacon 2019 video: What's Bad About Julia ( [VIDEO] ) TL;DR: Julia is not that bad."},{"id":27962069,"author":"ChrisRackauckas","month":"2021-07","story":"What's bad about Julia?","stance":"reference","why":"Julia's creator publicly listing the language's flaws","quote":"The language's creator did a nice Youtube video \"What's bad about Julia?\" at JuliaCon 2019 ( )","text":">I’ve had a lot of frustration with the Julia community being unwilling to see faults in the language. What? It's basically a meme at this point for major contributors to write a list of what they think is bad. The language's creator did a nice Youtube video \"What's bad about Julia?\" at JuliaCon 2019 ( [VIDEO] ), one of my most popular blog posts of all time is \"7 Julia Gotchas and How To Handle Them\" ( [LINK] ), etc. What the community pushes back about is things that are factually incorrect or just silly and repetitive, like the n+1th discussion about 1-based indexing. It's stuff below like, \"I want to do an interactive workflow but not use the REPL, so it's not working well\"... well the REPL is literally the interactive piece that caches the compilation so just use it if that's what you're trying to do? Yes, nobody has time for that nonsense, but everyone chimes in for real substantive discussions like changing the Base iterator API, programming patterns that would reduce recompilation, etc."},{"id":26475122,"author":"sischoel","month":"2021-03","story":"How C++ Resolves a Function Call","stance":"reference","why":"Touches on Julia function overloading and dispatch","quote":"there is something about that in this talk","text":"Julia is dynamically typed, but if the compiler can deduce the type of your expressions, it will usually generate specialized code that avoids dynamic dispatch. Getting rid of type instability (as it is called) is usually one of the first things I do when trying to optimize a Julia function. The discussion about C++ lookup rules actually really reminded me of Julia, where functions are quite often overloaded and it is not always trivial to figure out which one should be called. For lack of a better source, there is something about that in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20731273,"author":"Certhas","month":"2019-08","story":"Giving up on Julia (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Core team's laundry list of Julia's problems","quote":"All my top complaints are already mentioned in the laundry list","text":"Indeed, and while there is plenty to still be worried about, a lot has been addressed, too. Further we are now starting to see the pay-off with amazing algorithmic innovations being implemented in Julia. We have working code to code automatic differentiation: [LINK] Finally, the core team is paying attention to the problems and addressing them. All my top complaints are already mentioned in the laundry list: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20568610,"author":"chrispeel","month":"2019-07","story":"Julia: Come for the Syntax, Stay for the Speed","stance":"reference","why":"Julia creator lists known problems including time-to-first-plot","quote":"Jeff Bezanson gave a talk [1] at the just-completed JuliaCon that listed this as the first on a list of known problems with Julia.","text":"The \"time-to-first-plot\" (compiler latency) problem is something that the Julia gurus know about and are working on. For example, Jeff Bezanson gave a talk [1] at the just-completed JuliaCon that listed this as the first on a list of known problems with Julia. One immediate solution is to use PackageCompiler.jl [2] to build a Julia image that includes a pre-compiled version of the slow code (packages). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"Th4VlqQyVr4":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a skeptical debunking of the Pentagon UFO videos.","q":{"x":"See this video by \"Thunderf00t\" which gives really good earthy reasons for what's been seen.","i":27550909,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27550909,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2021-06","story":"Rep Tim Burchett Says New UFO Videos Aren't Russians, 'Out of Our Galaxy'","stance":"rec","why":"Gives mundane explanations for the fuzzy triangle and tic-tac videos.","quote":"See this video by \"Thunderf00t\" which gives really good earthy reasons for what's been seen.","text":"Roswell was a cover up, so the Soviet Union wouldn't know we could detect their above ground nuclear tests via sound carried in the stratosphere. [LINK] I strongly agree that if the Nazis, Soviets, or any other opponent had technology of the sort that allowed antigravity, history couldn't have unfolded the way it did. I have zero faith that space aliens have anything to do with the fuzzy triangles or tic-tac videos. Those have far simpler explanations. See this video by \"Thunderf00t\" which gives really good earthy reasons for what's been seen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27315255,"author":"aliasEli","month":"2021-05","story":"In 2019 US Navy warships were swarmed by UFOs","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the leaked Pentagon UFO videos.","quote":"Debunking by Thunderf00t","text":"Debunking by Thunderf00t: [VIDEO] (There are currently at least three threads that ultimately refer to the same video)"},{"id":27742665,"author":"oceanghost","month":"2021-07","story":"UFOs: 'These objects show physics we don't understand'","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among debunkings of the UFO videos.","quote":"these videos are all explained and all have been debunked","text":"I've said it once, I'll say it again-- these videos are all explained and all have been debunked. This is most likely a advertisement for the US Military's ability to track very small things such as drones. If there are videos of things that might be alien-- these are not them. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] Bob Lazar debunk: [LINK]"}],"r":[["PaypcVFPs48",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["ajGX7odA87k",1],["xMP_AfiNlX4",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1]]},"a1rC79DHpmY":{"wy":"Recommended as the canonical introduction to tree-sitter and why fast incremental parsing enables live editor support.","q":{"x":"There is a long (42 minutes) but worth to watch talk about tree-sitter","i":30955317,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30955317,"author":"ris58h","month":"2022-04","story":"Zas Editor","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk on tree-sitter and error-tolerant parsing.","quote":"There is a long (42 minutes) but worth to watch talk about tree-sitter","text":"They use tree-sitter [LINK] It's ok with errors in AST and just marks such nodes as invalid. There is a long (42 minutes) but worth to watch talk about tree-sitter [VIDEO] Or the shorter version (20 minutes) [LINK]"},{"id":16597521,"author":"lewisl9029","month":"2018-03","story":"Atom 1.25","stance":"rec","why":"Tree-sitter creator shows fast incremental parsing for syntax highlighting.","quote":"fast enough to run on every keystroke","text":"Tree-sitter is not just for code folding, it's a full-on incremental parser that's fast enough to run on every keystroke. See their expand selection example for another really compelling use-case. If you're interested in more details, there's also this talk from the developer behind tree-sitter ( [VIDEO] ), where he showcases how they could use tree-sitter to quickly parse entire multi-megabyte minified JS files to provide complete syntax highlighting for them that updates as you edit, and how the accuracy and usefulness of the actual syntax highlighting can improve over regex based implementations due to having access to the fully specified grammar of the language. I think tree-sitter is also a game changer for extension development once it stabilizes and the APIs are opened up. Extensions like swackets will no longer have arbitrary performance constraints that break user experience ( [LINK] ), and it opens the door to a whole new set of intelligent extensions that understand the languages you write at a much deeper level that were previously unfeasible due to parsing performance, or required some external heavy-weight language server."},{"id":27878345,"author":"bhl","month":"2021-07","story":"Diffsitter: A tree-sitter based AST difftool to get meaningful semantic diffs","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among tree-sitter resources.","quote":"","text":"`tree-sitter` has been post to the website a bunch of times so I remembered from those submissions: [LINK] Also these resources: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19778537,"author":"imoldfella","month":"2019-04","story":"A Brief and Brisk Overview of Compiler Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Tree-sitter talk cited as parser option for new languages.","quote":"Tree-sitter gives you live editor support for free.","text":"100% agree. If starting now I think you should consider targeting mlir[1]. It fixes many problems you will have with llvm, and allows you to create language specific dialects and transformations. Impressive new work by a top-notch team led by LLVM creator Chris Lattner. It has LLVM as an optional target, so you get that for free. For a parser, you should consider using tree-sitter[2]. Tree-sitter gives you live editor support for free. Impressive work by Max Brunsfeld. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WxK11RsLqp4",1],["yCcWpzY8dIA",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["Tb823aqgX_0",1]]},"fmF7HpU_-9k":{"wy":"HN points to it in beginner-programming-education threads for its distinction between easy to learn and easy to teach.","q":{"x":"One of the themes they talk about is the difference between \"easy to learn\" and \"easy to teach\" which I found insightful.","i":33671306,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34290313,"author":"dndn1","month":"2023-01","story":"Show HN: A 100% free and interactive Python course for coding beginners","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk by Hedy's author on the gradual approach","quote":"Theres an excellent talk on the approach by the author","text":"For 8yo, maybe you can consider bringing her through Hedy: [LINK] It's gradual (starts easy but limited, then with progress through levels gets more precise and powerful), and I read that it gets to full blown Python programming. Theres an excellent talk on the approach by the author: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33671306,"author":"ZeroGravitas","month":"2022-11","story":"Show HN: Easylang – A browser-based first programming language","stance":"rec","why":"Hedy talk on gradual language and easy to learn versus easy to teach","quote":"One of the themes they talk about is the difference between \"easy to learn\" and \"easy to teach\" which I found insightful.","text":"This reminded me a little of Hedy language: [LINK] It starts off super simple, like: print something and then introduces all the fiddly concepts like quoting, escaping etc. 1 by 1 until it turns into python. The designer had a neat talk about it at the strange loop conference. One of the themes they talk about is the difference between \"easy to learn\" and \"easy to teach\" which I found insightful. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39708084,"author":"sitkack","month":"2024-03","story":"Show HN: PyKidos, Teach Your Kid Python in the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"Felienne Hermans's Strange Loop talk on Hedy","quote":"Hedy: A Gradual programming language\" by Felienne Hermans (Strange Loop 2022)","text":"[LINK] is spearheaded by the wonderful [LINK] you might know her from her spreadsheet related research. She gave a wonderful talk at Splash 2023 on Hedy, I'll see if I can find it. *edit, I don't think it was recorded but I found these two which will do Hedy: Creating a Programming Language for EveryoneFelienne Hermans • YOW! 2023 [LINK] \"Hedy: A Gradual programming language\" by Felienne Hermans (Strange Loop 2022) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["sKudSeqHSJk",1],["D99qI42KGB0",1],["AtPA6nIBs5g",1],["PmTUW-owa2w",1],["doz1QJ7LwjA",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1]]},"ghDvyItIHTY":{"wy":"It is linked in laptop thermal threads for a layman's overview of why Apple limits surface temperature and why the mod is not simple.","m":[{"id":35028184,"author":"Dalewyn","month":"2023-03","story":"Fix Intel CPU Throttling on Linux","stance":"rec","why":"Layman's overview of safety regulation and cooling in M1 MacBook Air.","quote":"","text":"For a layman's overview of this, Linus from LinusTechTips gives a basic rundown of the relevant safety regulation and some numbers in one of his videos featuring the M1 Macbook Air and its cooling solution.[1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29347538,"author":"maximus-decimus","month":"2021-11","story":"How can MacBooks be so slim compared to others laptops","stance":"rec","why":"Explains regulated shell temperature limits as the reason MacBooks need fans.","quote":"","text":"Interesting video about how the only reason you even need a fan in the mac book pro is because how hot the shell of a laptop is allowed to get is legislated so you don't burn yourself on it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32195455,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-07","story":"Thermal Pad ‘Mod’ Makes M2 MacBook Air Faster Than 13″ M2 Pro","stance":"reference","why":"Prior LTT video on same thermal pad mod; regulations explain why not.","quote":"","text":"LTT Tech literally did a video with an identical thermal pad mod a year and a half ago with the M1 Air. [VIDEO] TLDR - Adding the thermal pads wouldn't pass regulatory muster."},{"id":32198407,"author":"derefr","month":"2022-07","story":"Thermal Pad ‘Mod’ Makes M2 MacBook Air Faster Than 13″ M2 Pro","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for IEC surface temperature limits that prevent Apple from doing the mod.","quote":"","text":"To be clear, the real reason Apple don't do this themselves, is because there are legal regulations preventing them from selling products that are any hotter to the touch. According to the IEC, metal contact surfaces on machines are considered unsafe above 55.5C. And unmodded, Apple's notebooks already scrape up against that upper limit. With the mod, they go quite a bit over it — well into \"causes pain to touch\" territory. (See: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":32206221,"author":"dijit","month":"2022-07","story":"The new MacBook Air runs so hot that it affects performance","stance":"reference","why":"LTT covered same M1 Air issue and did not recommend the mod.","quote":"","text":"The same “issue” was present on the M1 air. LTT did a video on it. Same as this article. He didn’t recommend the mod. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["l6T9xIeZTds",1]]},"i9MXYZh1jcs":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a clear demonstration of Amiga-era sampling and tracker workflows.","q":{"x":"Here's a good recap of what sampling looked like in the time of Amiga","i":27570007,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27570007,"author":"aasasd","month":"2021-06","story":"Renoise – A DAW with a tracker-like interface","stance":"rec","why":"Good recap of Amiga-era sampling and its memory limits.","quote":"Here's a good recap of what sampling looked like in the time of Amiga","text":"Here's a good recap of what sampling looked like in the time of Amiga: [VIDEO] “Ta-da-da-da... aaaaaaand we're out of memory.” Likewise apparently DJ Shadow pulled off quite a feat with his Akai MPC60 on ‘Endtroducing’, because the sampler could store something like twelve samples, also not too long. A sorta related anecdote is how the British ‘mind the gap’ was this terse because it was recorded on a solid-state memory, costing dearly in '68 even for the small amount by today's standards."},{"id":22664199,"author":"uk_programmer","month":"2020-03","story":"Someone Is Stealing Tracker Songs and Selling Them","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how tracker software works.","quote":"There is a great video on youtube that explains how it all works.","text":"There is a great video on youtube that explains how it all works. [VIDEO] He gets into the explanation of how the tracker software works about 8min 30seconds in. Typically though if you got a cracked game at that time it would have the crackers group name and there would be a little graphical demo and some tracker music."},{"id":31229173,"author":"aasasd","month":"2022-05","story":"When hits were stored on floppy disk and created with an Atari ST (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates sampler plus tracker on an Amiga around 1990.","quote":"Here's also a demonstration of using a sampler and a tracker with an Amiga, all from around 1990","text":"Here's also a demonstration of using a sampler and a tracker with an Amiga, all from around 1990: [VIDEO] “Aaaand we're out of memory.” (There's also the humor of sampling Coldcut, who themselves were wizards of audiovisual sampling, at least for their time.)"},{"id":31230135,"author":"mastazi","month":"2022-05","story":"When hits were stored on floppy disk and created with an Atari ST (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a cheap Amiga sampler interface.","quote":"maybe using a cheap Amiga sampler interface[3]","text":"This is what I remember from those times: if you had external gear (synth, sampler etc.) then the Atari was your best bet because of the built-in MIDI ports[1] and consequently having a good ecosystem in terms of MIDI sequencers. (Cubase, for example, was available for ST)[2]. If you didn't have external gear, then you would go for Amiga (maybe using a cheap Amiga sampler interface[3], and a music tracker[4] as the software) due to the fact that Amiga had better onboard audio, courtesy of Paula[5]. (IIRC, it wasn't until the Falcon that Atari caught up with Amiga in terms of onboard audio). [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":31234764,"author":"mastazi","month":"2022-05","story":"‘It was the poor man’s studio’: how Amiga computers reprogrammed modern music","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a cheap audio sampling interface.","quote":"an audio sampling interface, however these were generally very cheap [3]","text":"I used to make music on the Amiga. There is one aspect that the article doesn't explain too well - what allowed the Amiga to flourish as a music making platform, is that it had onboard audio which was much more capable than most other computers of the time, thanks to Paula, which allowed multi channel, high sample rate audio playback [1] These hardware capabilites in turn allowed the development of the Amiga music software scene, in particular music trackers [2] The Amiga was the first mass produced computer where you could make music without plugging in expensive synths or samplers (like was common on the Atari ST for example - I remember my uncle connecting his ST to an Akai sampler and a Roland synth, as a kid I could never have afforded a setup like that). But, if you wanted to sample on the Amiga, you actually needed one external piece of hardware: an audio sampling interface, however these were generally very cheap [3] Just a few hours ago an article about making music on the Atari ST made it to the HN home page (I also commented there), might be an interesting read for those interested [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":31299910,"author":"mastazi","month":"2022-05","story":"‘It was the poor man’s studio’: how Amiga computers reprogrammed modern music","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a sampler model's price in 1992.","quote":"this particular model was 30 pounds in 1992","text":"Some models were cheaper than that. As an example you can see here[1] this particular model was 30 pounds in 1992, which is equivalent to about 50 dollars[2]. Depending on the time or place they may have been a bit more expensive so it makes sense that in your case you had to spend $100... but let's not forget that a stand-alone sampler was at least 10 times more expensive, so the Amiga was still a way cheaper option than e.g. an Atari ST + MIDI-connected stand-alone sampler. Also we have to consider that there were sample libraries distributed on floppy - so with an Amiga you could still have \"cool sounds\" even without a sampler interface - the main point being that audio playback on Amiga was higher quality than most competitors. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29315625,"author":"aasasd","month":"2021-11","story":"Trackers: The Sound of 16-Bit [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows grabbing sounds with a sampler for a tracker.","quote":"Don't forget to use a sampler to grab sounds for the tracker","text":"Don't forget to use a sampler to grab sounds for the tracker: [VIDEO] > “Ta-da-da-daa”... aaaaand we're out of memory."}],"r":[["x_q50tvbQm4",1],["8CAafjodkyE",1],["atvtBE6t48M",1],["b6URa-PTqfA",1],["8PwwRR8deHk",1]]},"vBiIDwBOqQA":{"wy":"HN commenters point to Nick Lane as an excellent science communicator whose talks on mitochondria and the chemistry of life are worth watching.","q":{"x":"excellent science communicator; you can watch some of his talks online.","i":44008434,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44008434,"author":"wfn","month":"2025-05","story":"Transformer: The Deep Chemistry of Life and Death","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk from an excellent science communicator on the chemistry of life.","quote":"excellent science communicator; you can watch some of his talks online.","text":"I've read part of Nick Lane's other book, The Vital Question , cannot comment on this new one; TL;DR competent biochemist (from complete amateur standpoint at any rate), excellent science communicator; you can watch some of his talks online. e.g. the one linked on this new book's page is good: [VIDEO] He's really fascinated by the overall transformation process of inorganic matter -> organic matter, a sort of scientific fixation - which is always enjoyable when it's done by a competent scientist - and it's really captivating stuff. (The fact I haven't finished his previous book has nothing to do with the book material itself, if anything it really captivated me; it's just my not-amazing new habit of not finishing books...)"},{"id":32395198,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2022-08","story":"A biochemist’s view of life’s origin reframes cancer and aging","stance":"rec","why":"Nick Lane's Royal Institution talk on the Krebs cycle.","quote":"You can watch his presentations at the Royal Institute.","text":"You can watch his presentations at the Royal Institute. How the Krebs cycle powers life and death - [VIDEO] Why is Life the Way it Is? [LINK]"},{"id":47959399,"author":"morphle","month":"2026-04","story":"Biology is a Burrito: A text- and visual-based journey through a living cell","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture slides pointing to underlying origin-of-life research papers.","quote":"You could start with the slides in this lecture and read the underlying papers","text":"The research into the origen of life looks at bottom up fundamentals (how they work) of all cells since the solar system was formed. You could start with the slides in this lecture and read the underlying papers and all the references in all those papers. You probably can find these references also in all the books he wrote. [VIDEO] Maybe an educational text for the laymen has summarised this recently but I'm not aware of one. Most Biology from your school days have been rewritten."},{"id":40549967,"author":"diroussel","month":"2024-06","story":"Can ketones enhance cognitive function and protect brain networks?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on mitochondria and respiration beyond making ATP.","quote":"Interestingly I was just watching talk from Nick Lane, presented at the Royal Institution about mitochondria and respiration.","text":"Interestingly I was just watching talk from Nick Lane, presented at the Royal Institution about mitochondria and respiration. What is amazing to me is that respiration isn’t just burning glucose to make ATP, it also makes many other essential building blocks needed for health cells. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33258486,"author":"zoogeny","month":"2022-10","story":"Intelligence Beyond the Brain [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Nick Lane on the Krebs cycle, relevant to ideas about cells.","quote":"Nick Lane is another interesting person who is exploring alternative ideas related to cells.","text":"Nick Lane is another interesting person who is exploring alternative ideas related to cells. Although their work is not exactly the same, I find it interesting to ponder these ideas together. Some videos: 1. Krebs cycle and Transformers at Royal Institute: [VIDEO] 2. A wide-ranging discussion on Lex Friedman podcast: [LINK]"}],"r":[["OmJ-4B-mS-Y",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["dW8Cy6WrO94",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["cTsEzmFamZ8",1]]},"QyQvfaW54NU":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a fantastic talk and cite it in climate threads for the carbon debt framing and tree-planting cost estimates.","q":{"x":"gave a fantastic talk on carbon debt","i":20140539,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20140539,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2019-06","story":"The toll climate change is taking on oceans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on carbon debt and how to pay it down.","quote":"gave a fantastic talk on carbon debt","text":"Related: GoogleX co-founder Tom Chi gave a fantastic talk on carbon debt (with a rather depressing 238 views at the moment; I hope more people watch...): [VIDEO]"},{"id":20141455,"author":"symplee","month":"2019-06","story":"The toll climate change is taking on oceans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Frames CO2 as debt to repay; tree-planting by drone costed.","quote":"Agreed, this is an excellent talk.","text":"Agreed, this is an excellent talk. Main takeaway is to view the problem as debt which has to be paid off. Simply stopping new purchases will not pay off the debt. Above point summarized here, timestamp 22:15 - [VIDEO] We currently have a one trillion ton carbon debt. As a though experiment, for a starting solution to iterate upon, this can be \"paid off\" with 20 billion trees / year, for 50 years. It would take 9000 drones, 450 staff, $80 mil/year. And a total land area half the size of Brazil. Using current technology. Compare this with alternative approaches being thrown around with price tags of 1 trillion+."},{"id":29988959,"author":"dataflow","month":"2022-01","story":"Direct conversion of CO2 to solid carbon by Gallium-based liquid metals","stance":"reference","why":"Cost estimate for planting 20B trees a year.","quote":"planting 20B trees/year requires only $80M/year (!)","text":"Related, for comparison: apparently planting 20B trees/year requires only $80M/year (!) and would pull down a trillion tons of carbon over 50 years (!): [VIDEO]"},{"id":24467368,"author":"0x402DF854","month":"2020-09","story":"Earth may temporarily pass dangerous 1.5 C warming limit by 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the roughly one trillion ton carbon debt figure.","quote":"our current carbon debt is around 1 trillion tons","text":"Yes, even if we completely eliminate carbon emissions, our current carbon debt is around 1 trillion tons [1]. We'll need to remove that much carbon from the atmosphere within 50 years or so, given we stop all of our emissions today. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24195792,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2020-08","story":"We should discuss soil as much as coal (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Chi on climate change and sustainable agriculture.","quote":"see GoogleX co-founder Tom Chi's video on climate change, sustainable agriculture, etc.","text":"Related: see GoogleX co-founder Tom Chi's video on climate change, sustainable agriculture, etc.: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20426508,"author":"mlinksva","month":"2019-07","story":"Reforestation effort could capture 205 gigatons of CO2 in 40-100 years","stance":"reference","why":"Recent talk advocating large-scale tree planting using drones.","quote":"A more recent (this year) talk advocating for similar, but using drones","text":"It looks like that article is 10 years old. Has anyone done this at scale? A more recent (this year) talk advocating for similar, but using drones [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rHIkrotSwcc",1],["0rCUF-V1esM",1],["97t7Xj_iBv0",1],["PpG0z-npFIY",1],["fDqBeXJ8Zx8",1]]},"wvfR3XLXPvw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a clear, well-made treatment of convergent evolution and crabs.","q":{"x":"Can't endorse their style of science journalism enough.","i":31616063,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31616063,"author":"kjeetgill","month":"2022-06","story":"Crabs evolved at least five times from separate groups of crustaceans (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers how we know, who found it, and what we got wrong.","quote":"Can't endorse their style of science journalism enough.","text":"Eons did a great piece on this recently: [VIDEO] One of the things I love about the way Eons cover topics is how they don't just focus on what we know, but also how we know, who figured things out, and what we got wrong along the way. Can't endorse their style of science journalism enough."},{"id":34634985,"author":"jcfrei","month":"2023-02","story":"The search for extraterrestrial life as we don’t know it","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example of convergent evolution toward crab-like forms.","quote":"countless species have evolved into something crab-like","text":"Counterpoint: Alien life will probably look very similar to life on earth. If you look at evolution then countless species have evolved into something crab-like: [VIDEO] Turns out that their form is pretty good for survival. While biology can take on a myriad of forms, the competitive nature of life will weed out a lot of them. And since the rules for biochemistry are the same across the universe similar traits that are beneficial here will probably be beneficial there."},{"id":27541468,"author":"erulabs","month":"2021-06","story":"Why is everything evolving into crabs","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions another video on the same topic released that month.","quote":"","text":"Funny this came out the same month as SciShow’s “Why does everything keep evolving into crabs?” Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24931154,"author":"yeetman21","month":"2020-10","story":"Coincidence of Noticing Things, in Action","stance":"reference","why":"Noted as coincidence that the same topic appeared on trending.","quote":"this youtube video on trending ( ) talking about this very same concept","text":"I made a post 11 days ago ( [LINK] ) about a wikipeida page regarding things evolving into crabs. Then today I see this youtube video on trending ( [VIDEO] ) talking about this very same concept. This is a very niche subject so I am surprised by its relative frequent appearances. This thing has happened to me before, so I finally decided to look up what its called."}],"r":[["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["r0plv_nIzsQ",1]]},"-kFOXP026eE":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as a classic of physics lectures for laypeople that they watch and rewatch, though the audio quality is noted as poor.","q":{"x":"If you struggle with calculus, I found Herb Gross to be partucularly good","i":44912398,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44912398,"author":"jackdoe","month":"2025-08","story":"Death and What Comes Next (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman physics lecture in a list of lectures for laypeople","quote":"Now I watch and re-watch them","text":"Ignoring the moral question in the story, many people are satisfied by saying \"haha funny, the cat is both dead and alive\" and they just move on. Reading Pratchett for almost 30 years, it has fueled quite an interested in quantum mechanics in me, he subtly introduces QM teasers in almost every book I read. Particularly Mort. As I dropped out of highschool, I didnt have the luck to properly study physics, but in my 30s, I my existential crisis hit, as they say, a man dies twice, and the stupid half dead cat wouldn't leave me alone. And one night the omnipotent youtube algorithm recommended Susskind's lectures to me, then Feynman, Shankar, .. it just kept going. Now I watch and re-watch them but take screenshots and send them to claude when I am confused (which is still most of the time). If you are a layman like me and just want to know what is up with the cat, I encourage you to watch Sean Carroll's lectures (dont miss the Q&A videos) and it will for sure allow you to ask at least better questions. I love that cat so much. --- Sean Carroll: [LINK] Richard Feynman: [VIDEO] and [LINK] Leonard Susskind: [LINK] Ramamurti Shankar: [LINK] Barton Zwiebach: [LINK] Carl Bender: [LINK] If you struggle with calculus, I found Herb Gross to be partucularly good Herb Gross: [LINK] Of course watch 3blue1brown, alphaphoenix, styropyro etc as well"},{"id":37621570,"author":"gozzoo","month":"2023-09","story":"MIT 6.5940: TinyML and Efficient Deep Learning Computing","stance":"reference","why":"Classic Feynman lecture with poor audio, cited as example","quote":"especially some old like Feynman lectures[1]","text":"It's ironic that the audio quality of most AI online courses is terrible. I'm expectirng the moment when AI can be used to improve the audio of these lectures especially some old like Feynman lectures[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28941142,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2021-10","story":"Greater than 99% consensus on human caused climate change in the literature","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman lecture cited in a thread on scientific consensus","quote":"","text":"> Take your example about StyleGAN vs BigGan, I assume once it became clear that the latter was superior to the former that likely resulted in changes to existing architectures that then found additional improvements. This change in consensus is what enables that and is a good thing. Well, no. :) But I think the \"no\" is because of the uniqueness of ML rather than a \"no\" to your point in general. You might be right about other fields; I don't have experience there. In ML, there an enormous number of techniques. Style mixing was presented as a core feature of StyleGAN ( [LINK] ) and was enabled by default in the codebase ( [LINK] ). So there's a lot of \"inertia\" -- for example, when StyleGAN 2 came out, style mixing was still the default ( [LINK] ). I haven't had time to dig into StyleGAN 3, but I suspect that style mixing might still be enabled by default. It wasn't until we did a detailed, methodical analysis side-by-side with BigGAN, specifically to answer the question \" Why is BigGAN so much better for diverse datasets?\" that, on a whim, I turned off style mixing and was astonished to see BigGAN type quality pop out of a StyleGAN type arch. Discoveries like that usually go unnoticed, frankly because it's a lot of effort to write a paper specifically to say \"Hey, if you're training StyleGAN, definitely turn off style mixing. It only seems to work well on faces.\" However, if such a paper were to be written, and accepted into a peer-reviewed journal, then your original point w"}],"r":[["HI09kat_GeI",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1]]},"0QNiZfSsPc0":{"wy":"Referenced for years as the go-to example of fine gesture input, often in discussions of keyboard replacements and later its appearance in the Pixel 4.","q":{"x":"Google finally ships a research project in a real product","i":21271415,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21271415,"author":"melling","month":"2019-10","story":"Soli","stance":"rec","why":"The 2015 announcement showing Soli's potential.","quote":"Google finally ships a research project in a real product","text":"Google finally ships a research project in a real product and people don’t seem to understand the usefulness. Human nature for some reason often devolves to “what’s the point?” Here’s the 2015 announcement. There’s a lot of potential. Maybe we’d all benefit from “imagine what’s now possible “ [VIDEO]"},{"id":21260431,"author":"cbolton","month":"2019-10","story":"Google Announces Pixel 4","stance":"reference","why":"Original presentation of the radar chip.","quote":"The radar chip is especially interesting.","text":"The radar chip is especially interesting. I remember being intrigued when it was presented by the ATAP team years ago: [VIDEO] The team showed an impressive amount of cool stuff back then, it's nice to see one of these projects shipping on a flagship product. There's more info here on how it's used: [LINK] EDIT: found the original presentation: [LINK] . I think the touch sensitive cloth project also made it into a few products."},{"id":15452507,"author":"melling","month":"2017-10","story":"Introducing Oculus Go, Plus Santa Cruz Updates","stance":"reference","why":"Hand tracking for soft keyboards.","quote":"","text":"In my dreams the keyboard disappears. My eyes, or fingers, move the cursor, I can speak interactively with the computer, gesture, etc. Can fingers/hands be tracked sufficiently to provide an accurate soft keyboard? [VIDEO]"},{"id":14998731,"author":"melling","month":"2017-08","story":"The future of the human-computer interface?","stance":"reference","why":"Gesture tech that could replace the keyboard.","quote":"","text":"How old are you? It’s really not that hard to imagine the keyboard being replaced by one, or a combination, of technologies in the next decade or two. Voice has gotten dramatically better in the past few years. Another decade? There are also gesture technologies: [VIDEO] Anyway, if you’ve got 3 or 4 decades of life, you still think we won’t have sufficient advances to replace the keyboard?"},{"id":14796509,"author":"melling","month":"2017-07","story":"Model F Keyboards project – Bringing back the buckling spring keyboard","stance":"reference","why":"Soli chip idea for keyboards.","quote":"","text":"How about a little more innovation in keyboards? It is 30 years later. Split keyboards? [LINK] Tenting capable? Add a Soli chip? [VIDEO]"},{"id":14826683,"author":"melling","month":"2017-07","story":"Dell 38 inch UltraSharp monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Gesture control as an alternative to typing.","quote":"","text":"Voice, eye tacking, and gesturing don’t offer possibilities? [VIDEO] [LINK] And yes, if you’re clicking away in a room of programmers, it won’t work for you, but with a Hololens I’ll find a quiet location."},{"id":12425486,"author":"melling","month":"2016-09","story":"Blind Apple engineer is transforming the tech world at only 22","stance":"reference","why":"Subtle gestures to complement voice and keyboard.","quote":"","text":"i'm disappointed that we haven't made more progress with voice interfaces. Many people are happy with the keyboard/mouse as their primary input devices but combined with subtle gestures like you might get with Google's Soli and we should get a better user interface for everyone. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11666733,"author":"melling","month":"2016-05","story":"SignAloud: Gloves That Translate Sign Language into Text and Speech","stance":"reference","why":"Soli recognizes subtle hand movements.","quote":"Google's Soli would recognize small movements","text":"Someone could devise a more subtle set of movements for text entry. Google's Soli would recognize small movements: [VIDEO] There are others: [LINK]"},{"id":11459357,"author":"melling","month":"2016-04","story":"Running Emacs on Android","stance":"reference","why":"Soli as gesture input option.","quote":"","text":"Now we need a gesture keyboard so we can type, or twitch, instead of using an onscreen keyboard. Finger IO: [LINK] Google's Soli: [VIDEO] Perhaps a modified game controller: [LINK]"},{"id":11203754,"author":"melling","month":"2016-03","story":"An iPhone Is an Extension of the Mind","stance":"reference","why":"Hand tracking demo for organizing information.","quote":"","text":"Paper notebooks might be. [LINK] The cell phone, etc will be a much bigger extension. However, we still need better ways to input, organize and access the information. Handwritten notes, voice memos, pictures, drawings, unstructured and structured data, etc. Cortana/Siri with hand tracking ( [VIDEO] ) and a HoloLens will allow people to organize everything in creative ways."},{"id":11212853,"author":"melling","month":"2016-03","story":"My Journey From Blindness to Building a Fully Conversational User Interface","stance":"reference","why":"Chip that could interpret gesture series.","quote":"Google's new chip could probably help interpret a series of gestures","text":"you can provide audible feedback when you move your hands. Many gestures don't provide anyone tactile feedback. Move your hands or fingers then something happens on the screen. Google's new chip could probably help interpret a series of gestures: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11311234,"author":"IanCal","month":"2016-03","story":"FingerIO: Using Active Sonar for Fine-Grained Finger Tracking","stance":"reference","why":"Radar-based finger tracking comparable to sonar approach.","quote":"Reminds me of SOLI (which is radar rather than sonar)","text":"Really interesting. Reminds me of SOLI (which is radar rather than sonar): [VIDEO] Is there a way of trying this out? I know it'd only be demo line drawing applications but it'd still be interesting to try."},{"id":11025936,"author":"melling","month":"2016-02","story":"Ultrasound technology lets you touch and manipulate virtual objects","stance":"reference","why":"Soli among industry gesture efforts.","quote":"Gesture based computing is almost here in some form.","text":"Gesture based computing is almost here in some form. Apple, Google, Intel and Microsoft are all working on it: Apple: [LINK] Intel Real Sense - [LINK] Google's Soli chip - [VIDEO] Microsoft shipped the Kinect 6 years ago. The need for hand tracking with VR headsets should give it another boost. By the 20th anniversary of Minority Report? [LINK]"},{"id":11189445,"author":"melling","month":"2016-02","story":"An engineer replaced four monitors with Meta glasses","stance":"reference","why":"Soli as a path to dropping the keyboard.","quote":"Hopefully, Google ships this silicon this year","text":"\"The keyboard is the only thing I need, except for the glasses.\" I think this loses a lot of appeal until we can throw away the keyboard. We should be able to do better. Hopefully, Google ships this silicon this year: [VIDEO] I'd rather have those monitors and no keyboard."},{"id":10314989,"author":"melling","month":"2015-10","story":"Using a Custom Keyboard for CSS","stance":"reference","why":"Soli demo of subtle gesture control.","quote":"Google's Soli project might get us there","text":"It would be great if some of the repetitive tasks didn't even require a keyboard but rather use subtle gestures, eye tracking, and voice commands. Leap Motion, for example, never panned out but Google's Soli project might get us there: [VIDEO] Throw in better autocompletion ( [LINK] ) and software development becomes more precise and less repetitive."},{"id":10377263,"author":"melling","month":"2015-10","story":"How Long Will We Keep Typing?","stance":"reference","why":"Small gestures as faster input.","quote":"Throw in some small gestures and that'll be faster than reaching for a key.","text":"No one said that the solution has to be all speech. Throw in some small gestures and that'll be faster than reaching for a key. [VIDEO] Throw in eye-tracking and you can pick out any word quickly."},{"id":10199178,"author":"melling","month":"2015-09","story":"OptiKey – Full computer control and speech with your eyes","stance":"reference","why":"Precise gesture sensing as keyboard replacement.","quote":"Throw in eye tracking and precise gestures","text":"This tool is for people who can't use their hands, of course. For the average person, we're pretty close to being able to use voice instead of typing. [LINK] [LINK] Throw in eye tracking and precise gestures ( [VIDEO] ) and the keyboard isn't necessary."},{"id":10010330,"author":"melling","month":"2015-08","story":"Proton: Multitouch Gestures as Regular Expressions [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Soli radar chip as an example of gesture tech.","quote":"Google's Soli chip - 2016 -","text":"How long before gestures move off the screen surface? This technology got off to an exciting start but it quickly hit a wall. It was disappointing, for example, when Microsoft had to unbundle the Kinect. Microsoft's Kinect - 2010 Leap Motion - 2010 Intel Real Sense - [LINK] Google's Soli chip - 2016 - [VIDEO] Google's Touch Sensitive Fabric: [LINK]"},{"id":9848140,"author":"melling","month":"2015-07","story":"Pens Are Making a High-Tech Comeback","stance":"reference","why":"Radar-based gesture sensing as better input.","quote":"Google's Soli project might lead to better input","text":"Better pen input would be great, especially for drawing. How about some form of chorded keyboard or glove? A chorded keyboard was demonstrated in 1968 by Engelbart: [LINK] Google's Soli project might lead to better input: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16183442,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2018-01","story":"Controlling a Quadcopter with Hand Gestures","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"none of these replace haptical feedback.","text":"Hand Gesture controls bother me. I see all these fancy implementations enabling hand gestures such as the Google ATAP project called Soli [1], but none of these replace haptical feedback. One of the major aspects of any haptical human-to-machine interface is feedback and robustness. When you turn a high quality 30 indent encoder from Alps [2] or avionics grade Elma encoder [3]; you'll realize the value of haptic feedback. There is a reason why cockpits are full of knobs and dials, although unfortunately, that's changing due to highly integrated multi-function glass instruments. Problem with hand gestures is robustness. Theramins (hand gesture based music instrument) are cool, but they take forever to master and I am not saying they are trying to replace pianos, but let's just say - iPad pianos are not fun precisely because of the lack of feedback. I really think gesture based controls are inferior. They have no place in my living room, my car or anywhere in my life. That said, I think the implementation of hand gesture based systems as pointed out in this article are truly interesting. It is a challenging problem. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["21EiKfQYZXc",1],["pqzcCfUglws",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"0yXwnk8Cr0c":{"wy":"HN commenters use it in Ada discussions, including for Dewar's remark on how much costlier bugs are when found late.","m":[{"id":16669272,"author":"feelin_googley","month":"2018-03","story":"Is Ada a failed language?","stance":"rec","why":"MIT lecture on Ada by a respected programmer.","quote":"Heres a lecture at MIT about Ada from a favorite programmer of mine.","text":"Heres a lecture at MIT about Ada from a favorite programmer of mine. [VIDEO] I do not understand the Medium authors definition of \"success\". I get similarly confused when web commentators use the term \"won\" (e.g., language X \"won\") when discussing programming languages. Won what? IMO, the presenter (RIP) was quite successful. Im sure Ada \"won\" many contracts."},{"id":19579908,"author":"ajxs","month":"2019-04","story":"Haskell, Ada, C++: An Experiment in Prototyping Productivity (1994) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Dewar's remark on rising cost of late-found bugs.","quote":"Here's the video I got the Dr Dewar quote from","text":"I believe it was Dr Robert Dewar of AdaCore fame who I first heard remark that ( paraphrased ) every time a bug gets missed at a stage of development ( compile-time to alpha, to beta, to production, etc ) the cost of it's resolution increases by an order of magnitude. I'm certainly by no means dismissing the value of concise languages for expressing ideas fluently, but brevity and expressiveness are only two factors influencing the value of a programming language in a specific domain. It's definitely an interesting paper, every time I see it I can't help but think that it's a little disingenuous to conduct an apples to oranges comparison of languages on these particular merits. It's much more difficult to weigh up the relative investment of time necessary to write code that is equally robust, maintainable and fit for purpose. Here's the video I got the Dr Dewar quote from: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39996720,"author":"Rochus","month":"2024-04","story":"Ada, Past Present and Future","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I like this part about the Ada hype","text":"I like this part about the Ada hype: [VIDEO] Compare it to the hype around a current (intentionally corroded) language /s"}],"r":[["9xCJ3BCIudI",1]]},"19zm1Fn0S9M":{"wy":"HN points to it as a real-world example of Julia used in safety-critical aerospace design.","q":{"x":"See these talks on how a collision avoidance system was designed with Julia.","i":31884144,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31884144,"author":"ViralBShah","month":"2022-06","story":"Why I still recommend Julia","stance":"rec","why":"How a collision avoidance system was designed in Julia.","quote":"See these talks on how a collision avoidance system was designed with Julia.","text":"A detailed discussion is on Julia discourse: [LINK] Bugs are everywhere - including Python and R libraries. You would not design a safety critical system in Python using libraries and codepaths that are not heavily tested. Even then, you still have to carefully design testsuites that give you confidence that what you are computing is what is expected. See these talks on how a collision avoidance system was designed with Julia. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40402820,"author":"agucova","month":"2024-05","story":"Calculus with Julia","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is true, but even engineers see the advantages of Julia. My engineering school has went from almost pure Matlab usage to many key engineering courses switching to Julia due to its simplicity and friendliness. It's also SOTA for many engineering applications, particularly for acausal modelling and scientific machine learning (see [LINK] ), which has led to big companies like Pfizer adopting it [1]. And for engineers writing novel libraries, it clearly has a strong edge. See for example the work by NASA's JPL [2, 3], the FAA [4] or the CliMa project [5]. [1]: [LINK] (see also [LINK] ) [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO] [5]: [LINK]"},{"id":12643852,"author":"ViralBShah","month":"2016-10","story":"An Introduction to Machine Learning in Julia","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"An avionics application","text":"While many of Julia Computing's customers already use Julia in production, JuliaCon has had many talks as well. Here are a few: A Big Data application: [LINK] A finance application: [LINK] An avionics application: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11076130,"author":"chappi42","month":"2016-02","story":"Static, Ahead of Time Compiled Julia","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation on TCAS and ACAS X.","quote":"about TCAS (and its successor, ACAS X)","text":"In case you are interested, here are slides ( [LINK] ) and a presentation ( [VIDEO] ) about TCAS (and its successor, ACAS X)"}],"r":[["vWkgEddb4-A",1],["mSgXWpvQEHE",1],["jEbRVNxL44c",1]]},"3KdlJlHAAbQ":{"wy":"HN drops it into Microsoft threads as a quick, quotable jab that people feel still holds.","q":{"x":"It's great vintage Steve Jobs.","i":40047429,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40047429,"author":"kemayo","month":"2024-04","story":"Windows 11 adding ads to Start Menu","stance":"rec","why":"Vintage Jobs interview snippet with digs at Microsoft","quote":"It's great vintage Steve Jobs.","text":"The whole snippet, for anyone who hasn't seen it: [VIDEO] (It's great vintage Steve Jobs. He's trying to sound high-minded for a documentary-interview, but he really can't resist getting some digs in.)"},{"id":47058795,"author":"bschwindHN","month":"2026-02","story":"15 years later, Microsoft morged my diagram","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Money can't buy taste. He was right","text":"> The AI rip-off was not just ugly. It was careless, blatantly amateuristic, and lacking any ambition, to put it gently. That pretty much describes Microsoft and all they do. Money can't buy taste. He was right: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24715407,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2020-10","story":"PS5 Teardown [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on Microsoft having no taste","quote":"as true now as it was in the early 90's.","text":"> Microsoft who drew a box in AutoCad and called it a day \"Microsoft has no taste\": [VIDEO] as true now as it was in the early 90's."}],"r":[["upzKj-1HaKw",1]]},"3MWpHQQ-wQg":{"wy":"HN quotes it jokingly when talk turns to nonsense language, and one commenter points out its underlying linguistics.","q":{"x":"behind the silliness and ridiculous characterization, sets out Ferdinand de Saussure’s distinction of langue versus parole","i":36266185,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36266185,"author":"OfSanguineFire","month":"2023-06","story":"Eastern philosophy says there is no “self.” Science agrees","stance":"rec","why":"Silly sketch setting out Saussure's langue versus parole.","quote":"behind the silliness and ridiculous characterization, sets out Ferdinand de Saussure’s distinction of langue versus parole","text":"Fry & Laurie had a sketch[0] that, behind the silliness and ridiculous characterization, sets out Ferdinand de Saussure’s distinction of langue versus parole that is the foundation of modern linguistics. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48603256,"author":"gib444","month":"2026-06","story":"How many of the 170k English words do you know?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(fantastic sketch!)","text":"> stephen fry \"May I compartmentalise? I hate to, but may I? may I?\" \"Hold the newsreader's nose squarely, waiter, or friendly milk will countermand my trousers\" \"...saying the same weary things time after weary time: I love you. Don't go in there. Get out. You have no right to say that. Stop that. Why should I. That hurt. Help. Marjorie is dead\" [VIDEO] (fantastic sketch!)"},{"id":43274870,"author":"gessha","month":"2025-03","story":"NCSC, GCHQ, UK Gov't expunge advice to “use Apple encryption”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this sketch from A bit of Fry and Laurie","text":"Reminds me of this sketch from A bit of Fry and Laurie: Hugh: And by demagoguery you mean ...? Stephen: I mean demagoguery, I mean highly-charged oratory, persuasive whipping up rhetoric… Transcript: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35183904,"author":"jareklupinski","month":"2023-03","story":"Not by AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Hold the newsreader's nose squarely, waiter, or friendly milk will countermand my trousers.","text":"Our language, Tiger, our language, hundreds of thousands of available words, frillions of possible legitimate new ideas, so that I can say this sentence and be confident it has never been uttered before in the history of human communication: \"Hold the newsreader's nose squarely, waiter, or friendly milk will countermand my trousers.\" A Bit of Fry & Laurie Concerning Language: [VIDEO]"}]},"9pOiOhxujsE":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a humorous but pointed look at gun control and at learning from other countries' policies.","q":{"x":"you should watch John Oliver's 3 part Daily Show special on gun control.","i":6988518,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6988518,"author":"Intermernet","month":"2013-12","story":"ATF uses rogue tactics in storefront stings across nation","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver's comedic take on gun control.","quote":"you should watch John Oliver's 3 part Daily Show special on gun control.","text":"For an alternative view, you should watch John Oliver's 3 part Daily Show special on gun control. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9955838,"author":"grecy","month":"2015-07","story":"Raising Floor for Minimum Wage Pushes Economy into the Unknown","stance":"reference","why":"Segment mocks dismissal of learning from other countries.","quote":"watch this John Oliver video on gun control [1]","text":"This is absurd. The results of raising the minimum wage are perfectly known. Learn from the other countries that have done it in the last 10-15-25 years. Australia, Scandinavian countries, etc. etc. Why can't America look outside it's own borders and learn from what other countries have done and are doing? i.e. watch this John Oliver video on gun control [1] where it's stated Australia is not on this planet and America can't learn anything from what they've done, because Australia is not the real world. Why do people think this way? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40451106,"author":"modernpacifist","month":"2024-05","story":"The push to ban ransom payments is gaining momentum","stance":"joke","why":"Comedic clip used as punchline.","quote":"","text":"I don't see how banning payments would inherently create more opportunity for ransomware attacks. Assuming that the operators are already attacking as much as they can (why wouldn't they be - its more profit that way since its business after all) the only way to maintain profitability with lower per-attack yields would be to ask for more ransom per-attack which would likely drive the yields down even further. Reminds me of [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["qybUFnY7Y8w",1],["5Dw3aKbw5Wo",1]]},"AgkfIQ4IGaM":{"wy":"HN points to it as an intuitive way to see what hidden layers of a convnet learn, and as a reference for hierarchical feature learning.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend this video on the Deep Visualization Toolbox","i":11530743,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11530743,"author":"rkaplan","month":"2016-04","story":"What convolutional neural networks look at when they see nudity","stance":"rec","why":"Understand how convnets work through visualization.","quote":"Highly recommend this video on the Deep Visualization Toolbox","text":"Highly recommend this video on the Deep Visualization Toolbox to anyone interesting in understanding more about how convnets work through visualization: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44906719,"author":"jychang","month":"2025-08","story":"What's the strongest AI model you can train on a laptop in five minutes?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of a neuron lighting up on faces, showing capability needs model size.","quote":"it's clearly capability that a smaller model cannot demonstrate","text":"Why not? It takes models of a certain size to contain xyz neuron/feature. [VIDEO] That's not a mirage, it's clearly capability that a smaller model cannot demonstrate. A model with less parameters and less hidden layers cannot have a neuron that lights up when it detects a face."},{"id":24244708,"author":"gnramires","month":"2020-08","story":"GPT-3 has no idea what it’s talking about","stance":"reference","why":"Feature map visualization shows networks learn hierarchical concepts.","quote":"For example, using feature map and kernel visualization.","text":"And it also suffers from the tired assumption that GPT-3 (or any language models) should, or are designed to in any way, give reasonable answers[1]. All GPT-3 does is give likely continuations , given the training corpus. The prompts here are too short, and it could likely just be writing mediocre fiction continuations. Fiction tends to not be reasonable much of the time (to create story conflict). > \"To understand why, it helps to think about what systems like GPT-3 do. They don’t learn about the world—they learn about text and how people use words in relation to other words. What it does is something like a massive act of cutting and pasting, stitching variations on text that it has seen, rather than digging deeply for the concepts that underlie those texts.\" This is another pet peeve of mine. It has long been shown experimentally[1] that neural networks such as image recognition and text prediction networks such as GPT-3 do understand deep concepts that underlie texts (not perfectly yet, of course), from emergent abstractions and similar cognitive tools employed by human brains. [1] Gwern has also written extensively on failures of proper prompt programming: [LINK] [2] For example, using feature map and kernel visualization. In object classification or detection CNNs, specialized filters arise for detecting common observed object classes, like faces. Moreover, there is a hierarchical assembly of objects from elementary components (e.g. from lines, to limbs, to humans). Dee"},{"id":13251317,"author":"rasmi","month":"2016-12","story":"Understanding Neural Networks Through Deep Visualization","stance":"reference","why":"Shows neurons detecting edges, faces and text.","quote":"you can see neurons that detect edges, faces, and text.","text":"This toolbox allows you to visualize what neural networks \"see\" at each layer, and what image patterns activate what sorts of neurons (and how). For example, in this video [1] you can see neurons that detect edges, faces, and text. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"Hl1zEzVUV7w":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a primary source for Tesla's claims on battery materials, assembly design, 48V architecture and energy investment.","q":{"x":"I recommend watching the original presentation by Tesla's lead designer from Investor Day, it's about 12 mins and has great visuals","i":36116730,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36116730,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2023-05","story":"Tesla Rethinks the Assembly Line","stance":"rec","why":"Short presentation on Tesla's new assembly line design","quote":"I recommend watching the original presentation by Tesla's lead designer from Investor Day, it's about 12 mins and has great visuals","text":"I recommend watching the original presentation by Tesla's lead designer from Investor Day, it's about 12 mins and has great visuals: [VIDEO] One change that people don't appreciate much is that Tesla has gotten much better at making their cars. While other automakers now have models which spec-wise are competitive with the 3 and and Y, only Tesla and maybe BYD can make EVs profitably at scale. This is why they've been able to cut prices and still be decently profitable - a Model 3 RWD costs $40,240, which is about $33,500 in 2018 dollars (when the Model 3 started selling). I expect legacy car makers will follow Tesla's lead on this process (and switching to 48V) much as they are now following and introducing large castings in their cars."},{"id":43853521,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2025-05","story":"\"AI-first\" is the new Return To Office","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Tesla's care in systems integration","quote":"I am amazed at the level of care they put into their operations.","text":"I fell for this fallacy during the early days of the Tesla Skeptics wave. I thought no way this nonsense management style will keep them going once the competitors catch up. OEMs know how to make good reliable cars. Tesla is a shitshow. Turns out the incumbents repeated many of the EV specific mistakes Tesla did early on, and Tesla continued to innovate in what they thought matters (electric powertrain, software, infrastructure etc.) Even today if you watch some of Tesla's old battery day presentations, I am amazed at the level of care they put into their operations. The systems integration goes down to even the little things: [VIDEO] People really see this with how far ahead SpaceX and Starlink are. How does Musk keep getting the top people from the best schools to want to come to his companies when it is well known that prior groups get ground down to nothing and then tossed aside? I tried to figure this out by confronting Starlink employees directly at DEFCON conference a few years back. Essentially they are so driven they just want to work with A players. They dont tolerate B players or they will leave. Musk is sometimes wrong to the detriment of the team but sometimes he is really right and he pushes the team to perform their best and to think outside the box(or first principles as he would say)."},{"id":41518001,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2024-09","story":"FTC Pushed to Crack Down on Companies That Ruin Hardware via Software Updates","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as footnote on eFuse behavior in Teslas","quote":"Yes I know about eFuses, they were used on the Xbox 360 console as well.","text":"Yes I know about eFuses, they were used on the Xbox 360 console as well. As far as I know, they are similar types of eFuses on the Tesla that are capable of changing their state via firmware instead of one time blow (different application) but I could be wrong. Its just in this case the thing being controlled is not a checksum for what firmware can be installed, but a checksum to confirm if current should flow to a specific path. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38557990,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2023-12","story":"Tesla shares 48V architecture with other automakers to move the industry","stance":"reference","why":"Sections on electronic architecture and 48V savings","quote":"The savings in terms of weight and efficiency are actually significant.","text":"The savings in terms of weight and efficiency are actually significant. This was covered in Tesla's investor day presentation earlier in the year: The section on electronic architecture (~10min): [VIDEO] The sub section (~4min) on 48V in particular: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35462723,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-04","story":"Tesla Master Plan Part 3","stance":"reference","why":"Investment needed for sustainable energy versus fossil fuels","quote":"Per Tesla investor day,[0] this compares favorably to ~$14 trillion in investment that would be needed for fossil fuels over that time.","text":"Over 20 years. Per Tesla investor day,[0] this compares favorably to ~$14 trillion in investment that would be needed for fossil fuels over that time. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35474563,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-04","story":"Tesla Master Plan Part 3","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's message of optimism about sustainable energy","quote":"optimism that is based on actual physics and real calculations, not wishful thinking","text":"Or to put it in Musk's own words[0]: \"This is really the main message of today. And I really wanted today to be not just about Tesla investors who own stock, but really anyone who is an investor in Earth . \"What we're trying to convey is a message of hope and optimism. And optimism that is based on actual physics and real calculations, not wishful thinking. Earth can and will move to a sustainable energy economy, and will do so in your lifetime.\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35005595,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-03","story":"Tesla's Next-Gen Electric Motors Will Get Rid of Rare Earth Elements","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla investor day on reducing scarce battery metals","quote":"Per Investor Day[0] and other sources, they - developed low-cobalt and low-nickel cathodes, to reduce scarce battery metals","text":">just as newer battery chemistries moved away from cobalt Tesla seems to be the leader here. Per Investor Day[0] and other sources, they - developed low-cobalt and low-nickel cathodes, to reduce scarce battery metals - increased overall vehicle efficiency to allow iron cathodes which are earth-abundant - consolidated electronic modules to reduce microchips and wiring - are soon switching to 48V, to further reduce low-voltage copper wiring (copper being a 'medium-rare' metal) - switched from copper to aluminum high voltage conductors (per Munro Live), aluminum having better conductivity-to-weight - reduced vehicle structure with structural battery design and 'gigacasting' manufacturing Many of these seem to benefit from vertical integration. Are any other manufacturers pushing as hard here? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35059147,"author":"pornel","month":"2023-03","story":"Porsche, Ferrari E-Fuel Push at Heart of EU Engine Debate","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that lithium supply will follow demand","quote":"There is an abundance of lithium on Earth.","text":"Stellantis is just a legacy automaker with no EV expertise, so no wonder they struggle to make money on EVs. There is an abundance of lithium on Earth. Increased demand increases production. The price spikes are temporary due to a lag between increases in demand and the production that follows: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35147832,"author":"robomartin","month":"2023-03","story":"How Much Power Do Autonomous Vehicles Use? A Lot","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on overbuilding solar for continuous power","quote":"here's Elon Musk talking about something you claimed to be a lie","text":"How do you power a city at night if all you have is solar? Solar requires 100% reliable backup. Batteries? That's backup. Oh, wait. The batteries need backup because they cannot be charged by a solar array that is sized to only fit the load. You need to oversize it. By a lot. In fact, you need to at least double your array size to charge batteries to be used at night. In other words, backup for the backup. Wind? That's backup. Hydro? That's backup. More solar from thousands of miles away where there might still be light? That's backup. Nuclear? That's backup. Gas/Coal/Fossil? That's backup. Oh, wait, here's Elon Musk talking about something you claimed to be a lie, when I said you need to overbuild solar arrays by 7x or more for continuous power. Here he is, less than two weeks ago: [VIDEO] You need to 5x for continuous power, he says. Oops. It's also great that he mentions solar and wind can coexist. Some of backup can be co-located at some locations. Fantastic. Please stop resorting to insults. Please? Insulting people does not change the reality around you. It never will. Do the math and understand what you are talking about. Please. Nobody is saying solar is bad or impossible to use. This interpretation you seem to prefer is entirely fabricated. My claim that solar requires 100% backup is --quite literally-- as true as day and night and as necessary as what happens with solar between day and night. That you continue to attack and insult me over this is difficult to unders"}],"r":[["PqPGOhXoprU",1],["LbH1ZDImaI8",1]]},"Jg7mLSG-Yws":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid example of combustible dust hazards and of the CSB's investigative work.","q":{"x":"Sugar dust is violently explosive.","i":24163385,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":48254342,"author":"themafia","month":"2026-05","story":"CA declares state of emergency as fire crews race to contain toxic chemical leak","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the USCSB's investigative work on dust explosions","quote":"the USCSB is one of my favorite federal institutions","text":"Once I learned that sugar dust can literally _detonate_ I stopped being a Libertarian. Also the USCSB is one of my favorite federal institutions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24163385,"author":"akira2501","month":"2020-08","story":"Welders set off Beirut blast while securing explosives","stance":"reference","why":"Sugar dust explosion at Imperial Sugar","quote":"Sugar dust is violently explosive.","text":"> It really doesn't take much. I always remember the Imperial Sugar Factory. _Dust_ is enough. Sugar dust is violently explosive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18714537,"author":"retSava","month":"2018-12","story":"Was History Fair to the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory Owners?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of airborne particles as a fire hazard","quote":"Lots of highly flammable particles in the air is really a recipe for disaster","text":"Fires really scare me. They are one of the things that rapidly can get out of control, have devastating consequences, can happen in everyday conditions (as opposite to eg flying). Lots of highly flammable particles in the air is really a recipe for disaster, see eg [VIDEO] (sfw) Locked or blocked exit doors is evil, see eg the Station fire, or the fire in Gothenburg disco fire of 1998: [LINK] A year back, I was eating breakfast with my 4-yo daughter, and we came up with the question of \"what happens if we put hard bread in the toaster?\". Don't do it. The fire started quickly, and the flames were surprisingly large (under a cupboard too). Luckily, despite the small fire extinguisher not working at all, I managed to put the fire out without any damage to anything but the toaster. Edit: Station nightclub fire (warning: scary, pretty graphic), [LINK]"}],"r":[["vtOA1vuoDgQ",1]]},"LSxbc1IN9Gg":{"wy":"HN cites it as Linus's own explanation of why he invested in Framework and why outside investment suits a hardware company.","q":{"x":"Linus (Tech Tips) has a good video about why external investment is useful","i":30926411,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30926411,"author":"netsharc","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Why is raising money from VCs worn as a badge of honour?","stance":"rec","why":"Why external investment is useful for a hardware company.","quote":"Linus (Tech Tips) has a good video about why external investment is useful","text":"Linus (Tech Tips) has a good video about why external investment is useful: [VIDEO] , but he's talking about a hardware company, which is probably different to a software one. Skip to around 5:00 to get to topic."},{"id":30435844,"author":"GauntletWizard","month":"2022-02","story":"Bringing the Framework Laptop to more of the world","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Linus Sebastian invested in Framework.","quote":"","text":"Not that I agree with the characterization, but they might be referring to Linus Sebastian of Linux Tech Tips, who invested a bunch of money into the company: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29286845,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-11","story":"Modern mainboard for traditional T60 ThinkPad","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses money involved in hardware retail.","quote":"Linus (Tech Tips) talks about how much money would be involved","text":"Linus (Tech Tips) talks about how much money would be involved, and going full retail seems to be... very hard: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29015758,"author":"benbristow","month":"2021-10","story":"New MacBook Pro has first ‘DIY-friendly’ battery replacement design since 2012","stance":"reference","why":"Linus's investment in Framework.","quote":"","text":"It's gotten a bit more media attention recently as Linus (of Tech Tips/YouTube fame, not Linux/Git fame) has made a rather large investment in them and has been talking about them a lot on his platforms. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28608268,"author":"CubityFirst","month":"2021-09","story":"The Framework is the most exciting laptop I've used","stance":"reference","why":"Linus explains his philosophy on investing in Framework.","quote":"From his latest video where he disclosed / explained his philosphy in investing into Framework.","text":"From his latest video where he disclosed / explained his philosphy in investing into Framework. > [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["t1MqJPHxy6g",1],["Q2aEzeMDHMA",1]]},"TJYOkZz6Dck":{"wy":"Brought up as a beloved classic whenever forklifts or workplace safety come up in a thread.","q":{"x":"Staplerfahrer Klaus is a gory, dark-comedy PSA about the hazards of forklifts in warehouses","i":40368518,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40368518,"author":"ooterness","month":"2024-05","story":"How to get 7th graders to smoke","stance":"rec","why":"Gory dark-comedy PSA about warehouse forklift hazards","quote":"Staplerfahrer Klaus is a gory, dark-comedy PSA about the hazards of forklifts in warehouses","text":"For those unaware, Staplerfahrer Klaus is a gory, dark-comedy PSA about the hazards of forklifts in warehouses: [VIDEO] Most training modules are dry and clinical. This one is the opposite. Many people are killed, dismembered, and otherwise mutilated. I think the combination of good comedy and vividly illustrated consequences makes Klaus far more effective and memorable than the average corporate training module that OP is discussing."},{"id":46374114,"author":"kalaksi","month":"2025-12","story":"CEO killed at industrial site by worker operating forklift","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Forklift driver Klaus is a classic.","text":"I was just about to share it too. Forklift driver Klaus is a classic. Here's one with english subtitles: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44956294,"author":"LarMachinarum","month":"2025-08","story":"Forklifts require training","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"funny classic \"Forklift driver Klaus\" short vid","text":"Am I the only one who, after reading the title, had to think of the funny classic \"Forklift driver Klaus\" short vid (which, with its very own kind of humour, shows WHY forklifts require training)? * For those who wouldn't know it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24851069,"author":"incanus77","month":"2020-10","story":"Forklift truck drivers who never leave their desks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's a version with English subtitles","text":"First thing I thought of when I read 'forklift drivers' was Klaus. Here's a version with English subtitles: [VIDEO]"}]},"VkR2hnXR0SM":{"wy":"Shared as a fan-made teaser for Blindsight in threads about the novel or alien intelligence.","q":{"x":"Some fans of the book put together a short teaser trailer for it","i":27499723,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27499723,"author":"indigochill","month":"2021-06","story":"Neal Stephenson – Announcing Termination Shock","stance":"rec","why":"Fan-made teaser trailer for the novel Blindsight","quote":"Some fans of the book put together a short teaser trailer for it","text":"In addition to all the other recommendations, I recommend Blindsight. You can buy it where all good books are sold, or the author also gives it away on his website ( [LINK] ) but considers it good form to send a donation his way if you enjoy it. Some fans of the book put together a short teaser trailer for it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48707672,"author":"GolfPopper","month":"2026-06","story":"More evidence of life on Mars but still no life","stance":"reference","why":"Fan-made short film trailer for Blindsight","quote":"There's a fan-made short film/trailer for Blindsight","text":"There's a fan-made short film/trailer for Blindsight : [VIDEO] Also, \"just an AI probe\" and \"It would be amazing\" in the context of Blindsight ... <insert mad laughter>."},{"id":39133971,"author":"genman","month":"2024-01","story":"Researchers find signs of intelligence among jumping spiders","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is notably also adored by Watts","text":"[VIDEO] This is notably also adored by Watts [LINK] In case you want to fall down into the rabbit hole then [LINK] Now I just wish that some serious producer like Nolan would get a hint."}]},"VrKW58MS12g":{"wy":"HN mentions it in robocall and scam threads as an entertaining look at how phone scams work and how vigilantes fight back.","q":{"x":"I’d recommend two videos to start: Jim browning at and mark rober at .","i":26809846,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26809846,"author":"ipython","month":"2021-04","story":"FCC finally starts tackling America's robocall scourge","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how scams work and how vigilantes fight back.","quote":"I’d recommend two videos to start: Jim browning at and mark rober at .","text":"This is exactly the point. I happen to have a landline which somehow is associated with a demographic profile of a senior citizen. I’ve had this number for over a decade now. The number of scam calls targeting seniors is staggering. Of the 200-300 spam robocalls I receive every month, about 50-80 are blocked by my cell phone (the landline rings to my cell) and I also subscribe to nomorobo on the landline, which I assume blocks even more. Still, that’s some 200 calls that make it through even with two levels of blocking. The scams are terrifying- serious sounding people on the line saying your social security number will be cancelled and you will be arrested unless you speak immediately to an officer; Medicare advantage scams; IRS and treasury scammers... and since my personal info is published by the phone company, many times the scammers have all my personal information as “proof”! It’s a sickening information asymmetry that can totally fool an unsuspecting older person who doesn’t entirely understand what’s going on. If you’re curious about what these scams look like and how vigilantes actively work to combat them, I’d recommend two videos to start: Jim browning at [LINK] and mark rober at [VIDEO] ."},{"id":30198556,"author":"anonymouse008","month":"2022-02","story":"App store bill sails out of Senate Judiciary Committee","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This 'hack' is quite insane... Brillant and horrid","text":"> If you are arguing that some people will be tricked into installing malware from other sources then you are right that this change would allow that but it will still require user to go through few steps which should raise red flags for them. Some people just can't resist pushing that button without thinking and regardless of the consequences and in that case app store safety net is just temporary anyway, they are still exposed to the real world where that will harm them in a more serious way. I seriously could care less what we as HN want out of a phone in a way. We are head strong enough to do something different for ourselves -- literally look at PinePhone, etc. What I'm concerned about is the trickery that is sinister beyond belief, getting access that they never earned and do not deserve. Here's an example: [VIDEO] This 'hack' is quite insane... Brillant and horrid... and I foresee it happening left and right."},{"id":27018615,"author":"yladiz","month":"2021-05","story":"Request for comments regarding topics to be discussed at Dark Patterns workshop","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"(it’s the Mark Rober phone scammer video)","text":"There are also scams costing people money without using iOS, for example where the person is tricked into thinking they have a debt and sending thousands of dollars in cash to a random address[1]. What’s your point? 1: [VIDEO] (it’s the Mark Rober phone scammer video)"},{"id":26661317,"author":"berdario","month":"2021-04","story":"LineageOS 18.1","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"They'll rather exploit analog ways to get money out of you, recent example","text":"1- You don't, but it's not a real problem since non-cryptocurrency payments are reversible and trackable, so scammers won't use them. They'll rather exploit analog ways to get money out of you, recent example: [VIDEO] 2- You don't, but sandboxing should be enough to isolate untrustworthy apps from OS-level APIs that could do what you're afraid of."}],"r":[["rE3j_RHkqJc",1]]},"XyJh3qKjSMk":{"wy":"HN mostly quotes it as a joke about the nuclear missile Harrods would sell, and one commenter praises the show as political education.","q":{"x":"one of the UK's best contributions to political education and entertainment","i":45875305,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45875305,"author":"rswail","month":"2025-11","story":"How the UK lost its shipbuilding industry","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"one of the UK's best contributions to political education and entertainment","text":"Voted up just for the reference to Yes Prime Minister, one of the UK's best contributions to political education and entertainment. On the other hand, there's Sir Humphrey's opinion on Trident. \"It's the nuclear deterrent Harrods would sell.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22148929,"author":"chadcmulligan","month":"2020-01","story":"The Era of the Trident Engine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the nuclear Missile Harrods would sell","text":"Thought the same - always think of this when I hear Trident - the nuclear Missile Harrods would sell [VIDEO]"},{"id":7167548,"author":"masterstatesman","month":"2014-02","story":"Letters of Last Resort","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It is the nuclear missile Harrods would sell you.","text":"I love this part as well, when Sir Humphrey is arguing for Trident: [VIDEO] \"In the world of nuclear missiles, it is the Savile Row suit, the Rolls Royce Corniche, the Chateau Lafite 1945. It is the nuclear missile Harrods would sell you.\""}]},"Y_9vd4HWlVA":{"wy":"HN points to it as the clearest and most striking explanation of femto-photography, with animations that make it easy to follow whenever related imaging stories appear.","q":{"x":"Seriously, watch it. Very cool.","i":4401666,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4401666,"author":"HorizonXP","month":"2012-08","story":"RIP Open Source MySQL","stance":"rec","why":"TED demo of trillion-fps camera showing light moving, even appearing to flow backwards.","quote":"Seriously, watch it. Very cool.","text":"Indeed! You should see time flowing backwards in this YouTube video at this timecode: [VIDEO] Seriously, watch it. Very cool."},{"id":20814790,"author":"amingilani","month":"2019-08","story":"Show HN: Wave-based non-line-of-sight computational imaging in Julia","stance":"reference","why":"Talk with an animated explanation of femto-photography and seeing around corners.","quote":"which had an animation showing how it was done for an ELI5","text":"There was a similar technique discussed in a Ted Talk using femto-photography, which had an animation showing how it was done for an ELI5[0]. The talk contains references future technology to see around corners. This article may have been inspired by the talk, but it certainly makes it much more available for the rest of us. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18204814,"author":"irisli","month":"2018-10","story":"Camera freezes time at 10 trillion frames per second","stance":"reference","why":"TED talk explaining in detail how the trillion-fps camera works.","quote":"has a TED talk that explains it in detail","text":"The team behind the video of the coke bottle has a TED talk that explains it in detail: [VIDEO] Edit: oops I misread your comment"},{"id":8709394,"author":"_nedR","month":"2014-12","story":"Femto-Photography: Visualizing Photons in Motion at a Trillion Frames per Second","stance":"reference","why":"Ramesh Raskar's TED talk on the femto-photography project.","quote":"did a TED talk on it 2 years ago","text":"Not sure why exactly this is news. Ramesh Raskar, the head of the MIT team doing the project did a TED talk on it 2 years ago : [VIDEO]"}]},"YaUlqXRPMmY":{"wy":"HN cites it when discussing how math should be learned and taught, and why physics isn't built from axioms.","q":{"x":"Feynman was an excellent communicator","i":10188585,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10163528,"author":"asgard1024","month":"2015-09","story":"Bruce Eckel announces he's going to write Atomic Python","stance":"rec","why":"Greek vs Babylonian mathematics mapped onto language styles","quote":"Here's a helpful video from Feynman that kinda explains it","text":"> What is simple and clear about Python? .. it's really a pretty complex language with a deep ecosystem Here's a helpful video from Feynman that kinda explains it: [VIDEO] Python language (ditto Perl, Ruby..) uses Babylonian approach, while the languages with more fundamental building blocks (like say Scheme, Haskell..) are more like Greek approach. So Python it is not simpler as a language per se, but it is simpler to mold onto the problem. At least that's my explanation why people (myself included) like to use it, despite well understanding there are more elegant (mathematically) languages out there."},{"id":10188585,"author":"nerd_stuff","month":"2015-09","story":"Feynman the Babylonian (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"The original Feynman lecture segment itself","quote":"Feynman was an excellent communicator","text":"Here's the part of the lecture mentioned in the article: [VIDEO] Feynman was an excellent communicator, it's rare that having an intermediary \"explain\" what he was saying adds more than it takes away."},{"id":37334660,"author":"throwaway71271","month":"2023-08","story":"Tell HN: UC Berkeley's CS61A/B/C are the best courses to learn CS and coding","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy to disagreement about order of teaching","quote":"reminds me a bit of this Feynman video about 'Greek' versus 'Babylonian' mathematics","text":"By linear I did not mean the order in which you study, but rather how your understanding builds up, as in, how much time you put in, and how much you grow. For example if your native language is subject verb object (cows eat grass) it is quite linear to learn subject object verb (cows grass eat) languages (e.g. Japanese), you put in time, and you make progress. There are other subjects where when you get stuck you cant move on, and the pedagogy and androgogy systems we have came up with for math/physics and etc are getting better and better to understand what people don't understand and how to move them further. Which on its own is quite problematic when you have a class of 30 kids and you are moving with the 'average' kid which does not exist. In the same time, programming is fairly new, and teaching it is still evolving, educators still disagree on what is important and in which order (reminds me a bit of this Feynman video about 'Greek' versus 'Babylonian' mathematics [VIDEO] ) There was a great example someone used, the amount of people who get confused by the equal sign, and some actually never go through understanding references and values for many years: x=5 y=x x=6 print(y) and a=[] b=a a.push(1) print(b) Now I am teaching my daughter and I spent about 3 days per week just on pointers and strings (We even made a card game we play from time to time [LINK] ), and I can see when she is stuck and what exactly she is stuck on, but how can you do that with 30 kids, when the"},{"id":16562517,"author":"bryanph_","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: How to self-learn math?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on why math has no bottom-up","quote":"Here you have Feynman talking on this","text":"There is no such thing as \"bottoms up\" in mathematics, I think a relatively broad examination of mathematics might be useful so that you can discover what is relevant to the subjects you are actually interested in. I learned this the hard way. Here you have Feynman talking on this: [VIDEO] Finding a real world use case for the math you learn will be crucial if you self-study to keep yourself motivated to keep going."},{"id":10165507,"author":"nnq","month":"2015-09","story":"Magnetic wormhole created for first time","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on physics not being built on axioms","quote":"physics isn't that much built on axioms, in general","text":"> causality is still a strong axiom The \"causality axiom\" is a nice (or trivial) statement in philosophy. But as far as we know, there's no such axiom in any mathematical theory proved to match physical reality, and nothing so clear cut in modern theoretical physics at all afaik... Also, physics isn't that much built on axioms, in general: [VIDEO] (Summarizing Feynman: you still get from time to time cases where you can prove something from \"axioms\", but then you also find it to still hold in contexts where the axioms don't apply, or even to not work in others where they do apply... and the latter is the case where you have to remake your theory to match reality ) But my main point is that considering something impossible simply because our puny ape brains can't fathom them is preposterous!"},{"id":9860249,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2015-07","story":"Will Our Understanding of Math Deteriorate Over Time?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"“In mathematics and theoretical computer science, we read research papers primarily to find research questions to work on, or find techniques we can use to prove new theorems.” This is why figuring out an elegant, concise, and powerful set of mathematical models which apply to multiple domains, and then devoting effort to simplifying, organizing, and explaining those ideas in an accessible way is so important. Incentives for researchers are mostly to push and prod at the boundaries of a field, but in my opinion mathematical ideas are only of marginal value in themselves; more important is the way they help us understand and interact with the physical universe, and for that building communities, developing effective languages and notations, codifying our understanding, and making it accessible both to newcomers and to outsiders is the most important task for a field, and perhaps for our society generally. Just like with software projects or companies, the most “success” comes from helping a range of other people solve their problems and extend their abilities, not from making technically beautiful art projects for their own sake (not that there’s anything inherently wrong with those). Perhaps more generally, while theorem proving has overwhelmingly dominated pure mathematics and related fields for the past 80–100 years, and has been an important tool since Euclid, theorem proving is only one way of approaching the world, and in my opinion is a mere tool, not an end in itself. "}]},"aw6RsUhw1Q8":{"wy":"HN refers to it mainly for context on coal executive Robert Murray and the lawsuit that followed the segment.","m":[{"id":24656899,"author":"notpiika","month":"2020-10","story":"Bob Murray, Who Fought Regulations as a Coal Operator, Files for Lung Benefits","stance":"rec","why":"Great watches on Murray.","quote":"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight episodes on Murray are great watches.","text":"If you haven't already seen them, John Oliver's Last Week Tonight episodes on Murray are great watches. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":19908055,"author":"twright","month":"2019-05","story":"Third-Biggest U.S. Coal Company Files for Bankruptcy","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on aggressive coal executives refusing fair worker compensation.","quote":"goes through some rather aggressive coal executives who don't want to fairly compensate workers.","text":"Historically, coal is an industry filled with -to put it lightly- labor disputes due to exploitative conditions [1][2]. Today, the industry in decline by any definition and as companies try to awl out some sort of profit they cut corners which create unsafe conditions. When accidents happen, the company will skirt responsibility to the very end (someone else has a nice link here [3]). The \"Last Week Tonight\" on coal from a few years ago (start at 8:39[4]) goes through some rather aggressive coal executives who don't want to fairly compensate workers. Here are a few recent coal disasters you can read about: [5,6] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO] [5]: [LINK] [6]: [LINK]"},{"id":14671487,"author":"joshuamorton","month":"2017-06","story":"EPA: Pruitt will launch program to 'critique' climate science","stance":"reference","why":"Segment that prompted the Robert Murray lawsuit threat.","quote":"the same Robert Murray that threatened to (and then did) sue John Oliver after his segment on coal this month","text":"As an interesting note, the Murray referenced in the article is the same Robert Murray that threatened to (and then did) sue John Oliver after his segment on coal this month [0][1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UN8bJb8biZU",1]]},"cCeeTfsm8bk":{"wy":"Brought up in threads about isolation, dystopia and busyness as a moving, relevant short film.","q":{"x":"It's saddening and quite moving.","i":10140355,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10140355,"author":"thewarrior","month":"2015-08","story":"Minecraft creator Notch shares the darker side of life after a big exit","stance":"rec","why":"Oscar-nominated short film paralleling feelings of isolation after a big exit.","quote":"It's saddening and quite moving.","text":"By sheer coincidence , I just finished a watching an Oscar nominated short film called MORE which exactly parallels Notch's situation. It's saddening and quite moving. [VIDEO] I think both Notch's feelings of isolation and ennui and the resentment of those around him are both valid. One doesn't preclude the other , in fact they feed into each other."},{"id":16781452,"author":"newnewpdro","month":"2018-04","story":"Why is everyone so busy? (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I remembered this short video from the 90s, semi-related.","text":"Everywhere Americans look they're pressured to consume more, achieve more, live beyond their means, go into debt (student loans, car loans, home mortgages), and have a family on top of it all while saving for some fabled retirement and college tuition for the kids. If you unplug and simplify your life, don't bother with pursuing a family, you'll see how disincentivized to be busy you are and how absurdly everyone else appears to be living. But if you pursue all the things your competitors are pursuing, you'll find yourself having to acquire similar levels of resources. There's not enough to go around for _everyone_ to have that amount of excess, hence it must be fought over. Basically what you have is a non-violent (most of the time) resource competition determining who gets how much of what in what order, where the participants aren't even pursuing a specific quantity - they're just after as much as possible while they're still able, MORE. edit: After writing the above, I remembered this short video from the 90s, semi-related. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15972067,"author":"aorav","month":"2017-12","story":"Magic Leap One","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"reminds me of the quite dystopian short Movie 'More'","text":"The entire presentation, and the look of the glasses reminds me of the quite dystopian short Movie 'More': [VIDEO]"}]},"fPF4fBGNK0U":{"wy":"HN uses it as shorthand for how compounding engineering improvements, crumple zones especially, made things safer, applied to analogies well beyond cars.","q":{"x":"Have a look at the excellent 2009 vs 1959 car crash test video","i":7395911,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7395911,"author":"pugz","month":"2014-03","story":"Why Cars Will Kill 30,000 Americans This Year","stance":"rec","why":"Crash test showing how modern cars protect occupants far better than 1950s cars","quote":"Have a look at the excellent 2009 vs 1959 car crash test video","text":"Modern car design saves lives. Cars in the 1950's had stiff, thick plates and it didn't do them much good. Have a look at the excellent 2009 vs 1959 car crash test video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36333054,"author":"KennyBlanken","month":"2023-06","story":"Trapped Under Trucks","stance":"reference","why":"Shows why old cars needed roll cages","quote":"Roll cages were a Thing because, well, this","text":"Most modern passenger car passenger cells don't need roll cages because their passenger safety cells are properly engineered, and because crash safety is much more comprehensive, with wheels and suspension components designed to intentionally fail and crumple in a crash so they aren't pushed into the passenger cell. A ton of cars pass Euro-NCAP and IIHS crash and rollover tests with flying colors. Roll cages were a Thing because, well, this: [VIDEO] Obviously not every car is up to snuff, especially at the bottom end of the market. You couldn't pay me to drive a current production GM, Ford, Suzuki, Mitsubishi, etc compact sedan. 5-point harnesses aren't really that necessary at 0-60mph speeds in a vehicle with modern airbags; the fifth strap is mostly for anti-submarining but a number of manufacturers have produced seats that are anti-submarining and anti-submarining dashboard design (both material and shape) is a thing as well. All of it's possible, it's just a lot of car companies are cheap and consumers don't really shop based on stuff like \"is there an anti-submarining design to the dash.\""},{"id":35828901,"author":"jackhack","month":"2023-05","story":"Squad Solar City Car","stance":"reference","why":"Head-to-head crash showing crumple zones beat rigid frames","quote":"Crumple zones, energy absorbing materials, deformation of structures = survivability.","text":"Not just modified vehicles or hummers, but a road filled with SUVs, minivans, delivery trucks, crossovers. Even a \"standard\" Ford F150 is goliath, and an indestructable frame won't save you. To the contrary, a super-rigid frame translates into occupant injury. Crumple zones, energy absorbing materials, deformation of structures = survivability. A 20 mph head-on collision in a 1950s auto with a full frame design is often far more serious than a 40 mph collision in a modern auto. Head-to-head comparison: [VIDEO] (likely death vs possible foot injury) Now contemplate a 6' long vehicle with 1' or less of \"crumple zone\"."},{"id":30157508,"author":"KennyBlanken","month":"2022-02","story":"New engine could save internal combustion from the scrap heap","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the survival difference in a collision between old and new cars","quote":"passenger safety didn't change much for American cars between the 50's and early 80's.","text":"You being seriously injured or dying in an offset ~30mph collision, versus getting out of the car dazed and confused is the difference. And yes, passenger safety didn't change much for American cars between the 50's and early 80's. [VIDEO] You causing more pollution (particulate, NOx, etc) than a hundred luxury SUVs or more, is the difference. Driving down the highway at 75mph able to hear the back seat passenger whisper versus having to talk loudly so the person in the front seat next to you can understand you. A climate control system that keeps the car comfortable whether it is -20 degrees or 100 degrees. Handling, brakes, and acceleration that is astronomically better than whatever your Chevette has. There's no such thing as a \"200mpg engine\" - most modern car engines are very efficient, with the Prius's Atkinson cycle engine being the most efficient among worldwide-available, mass-production, affordable cars. It's a function of aerodynamics, parasitic electrical and mechanical losses, and weight. > It's not all one thing or the other, and it never should be, but we've been milking the old internal combustion cash cow for so long we've virtually guaranteed her death, even though evolution of the internal combustion engine would be a net benefit. Making an engine that would last 750,000 miles is certainly possible - just look at engines in big rigs - but people don't care about that in a personal vehicle, and the capex and opex for such an engine is incredible compared to "},{"id":29188777,"author":"yissp","month":"2021-11","story":"Unsafe at Any Speed","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates car safety improvements between 1959 and 2009","quote":"a good illustration of the improvements that have been made in car safety","text":"This video of a crash test between a 2009 Chevy Malibu and 1959 Bel Air is a good illustration of the improvements that have been made in car safety [VIDEO]"},{"id":23968036,"author":"oblio","month":"2020-07","story":"How popular media portrays the employability of older software developers","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder of how far safety has improved","quote":"Whenever you forget how much we've moved forward, despite occasional setbacks, look at this","text":"I think it easy to lose sight of the big picture. Things are constantly improving, even if the road is winding. People just forget some of the horrors of the past. We used to have VB/VBA, now we have Javascript. We used to have Ada, now we have Rust. C++ has evolved. Even C has evolved, in a kind of meta way: C itself hasn't evolved much, but the ecosystem around it has evolved a lot. How common was something like Valgrind back in 1990? Whenever you forget how much we've moved forward, despite occasional setbacks, look at this: [VIDEO] :-)"},{"id":20643103,"author":"oblio","month":"2019-08","story":"Why is modern web development so complicated?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of compounding improvements over time","quote":"Over time those improvements compound and you end up with this","text":"And that's actually rational behavior. It's also a very simple and very materialistic explanation of why humankind evolves, bit by bit. It works the same way even for discovering new medicine or any kind of technology. The new thing must be at least a bit better than the old thing. Over time those improvements compound and you end up with this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15417269,"author":"oblio","month":"2017-10","story":"Uncle Bob and Silver Bullets","stance":"reference","why":"Crash test showing designers do not rely on perfect drivers","quote":"Somebody should show Uncle Bob this","text":"Somebody should show Uncle Bob this: [VIDEO] My point being: yes, if we'd be perfect drivers at all times and in all weather conditions there would be no more accidents. Luckily for us auto designers haven't adopted this perspective :)"},{"id":14467055,"author":"saulrh","month":"2017-06","story":"Coal plants emit more radioactive waste than nuclear plants (2007)","stance":"reference","why":"Crash test as analogy for modern designs being safer than old ones","quote":"no modern car could be as unsafe as as a car from the 1960s","text":"Estimates of excess deaths due to nuclear, counting chernobyl , are the lowest of any energy technology. This isn't even counting the part where no new nuclear reactor could possibly be as unsafe as Chernobyl or Fukushima in the same way that no modern car could be as unsafe as as a car from the 1960s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12869781,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2016-11","story":"GoPro shows its vulnerability after horrific Q3 earnings","stance":"reference","why":"Crash test of an old car versus a modern one","quote":"Not quite that exact combo, but you get the idea","text":"A 1957 Ford pickup is going to be an absolute deathtrap in an accident. I'm not sure that's a great example. Not quite that exact combo, but you get the idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7396030,"author":"tokenadult","month":"2014-03","story":"Why Cars Will Kill 30,000 Americans This Year","stance":"reference","why":"Crash test comparing 2009 and 1959 cars","quote":"","text":"The death rate per miles driven in the United States has fallen dramatically in my lifetime, and I can remember when the annual number of deaths was much higher.[1] That said, the article makes a very interesting claim about attitudes that we should all follow to the end of the article for further discussion here: \"There’s an open secret in America: If you want to kill someone, do it with a car. As long as you’re sober, chances are you’ll never be charged with any crime, much less manslaughter.\" My wife bike-commutes year-round (yes, even in Minnesota), and as I mention this among Facebook friends, other friends who are also bike commuters point out that car drivers can basically kill bicyclists in the United States with no legal penalty at all. That's not a good social environment for getting more people out for exercise and energy conservation by substituting bicycling for driving cars. The history reported in this article is very interesting. There are a lot of contemporary photographs of changing American cities. The quotations from experts provide perspective on the visuals: \"'If a kid is hit in a street in 2014, I think our first reaction would be to ask, \"What parent is so neglectful that they let their child play in the street?,\"' says Norton. \"'In 1914, it was pretty much the opposite. It was more like, \"What evil bastard would drive their speeding car where a kid might be playing?\" That tells us how much our outlook on the public street has changed.\" Indeed. Are we "}]},"pRlh8LX4kQI":{"wy":"Referenced in security discussions as an example of defense-in-depth sandboxing for a desktop Linux environment.","q":{"x":"Check out the talk \"Linux for Chromebooks: Secure Development\"","i":22715575,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22715575,"author":"est31","month":"2020-03","story":"Fixing the Desktop Linux Security Model","stance":"rec","why":"Explains secure Linux sandboxes on Chrome OS.","quote":"Check out the talk \"Linux for Chromebooks: Secure Development\"","text":"Chrome OS is a very secure Linux distro. While the base system is very restricted, they also support Linux sandboxes, making it secure for you to use the computer both for online banking and for downloading random code from the internet. Check out the talk \"Linux for Chromebooks: Secure Development\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":41800976,"author":"mdhb","month":"2024-10","story":"WASM Is the New CGI","stance":"reference","why":"Shows security care in Chrome OS developer environment.","quote":"","text":"Even when you enable “developer mode” which is essentially Debian in a VM the level of care that went into making sure that no matter what happens there you will never suffer a full system compromise is truly impressive. To give you a sense of where they were half a decade ago you can already see that it’s as I described miles in front of anything that exists even today in this video: [VIDEO] When we get to talking about when they went for a total ground up first principles approach with Fuchsia as a next generation operating system that is something else entirely on a different level again. I genuinely didn’t have a hint of irony in my original comment. They are actually that much better when it comes to security."},{"id":36981873,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-08","story":"ChromeOS is splitting the browser from the OS, getting more Linux-y","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the Linux VM design similar to WSL.","quote":"","text":"That is a VM, similar in concept to WSL on Windows, with the difference that the host is also Linux based. \"Linux for Chromebooks: Secure Development\" [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23259676,"author":"est31","month":"2020-05","story":"Stealing secrets from developers using WebSockets","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how Chrome OS sandboxes the localhost server.","quote":"","text":"You're likely not even safe from this if you are using Chrome OS. It does sandbox the localhost web server [1] [2], but it does not restrict access to it from the host. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":20870181,"author":"est31","month":"2019-09","story":"Parents Thought They Wanted Tech in Every Classroom, Now They’re Not So Sure","stance":"reference","why":"Shows desktop tools sandboxed without developer mode.","quote":"","text":"You can now have sandboxes where you can install desktop tools without having to enable stuff like developer mode: [VIDEO] Sandboxing this stuff away is in general a good idea for protecting \"normal\" users. People often want to install pirated games or whatever and that's abused as a vector to get malware onto those devices. If that's not enough you can install entirely different operating systems and even bootloaders (if you want to)."},{"id":20901188,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2019-09","story":"A Message about iOS Security","stance":"reference","why":"Shows defense-in-depth isolation used on Chromebooks.","quote":"Look at the defense-in-depth used on Chromebooks to isolate execution","text":"Back when Chrome was based on WebKit, IIRC Chrome team found and fixed oodles and oodles of bugs in WebKit, because WebKit wasn't even using fuzzing, or not enough fuzzing. Even as late as 2017, fuzzing was still finding significantly more security issues in Safari than other browsers: [LINK] Also, when discussing iCloud, you need to distinguish between the backend service, and the frontend service. There have been significant CVEs found in the front-end client. Apple doesn't run many front end Web services, so there's less to exploit. They also don't allow you to host executable code like AWS, Azure, and GCP, so the attack surface is much more confined. That Google has exposed their infrastructure to the unforgiving nature of the Web for 2 decades, with exploits few and far between, is a testament to the quality of the security engineers. The most secure device on the planet, isn't iOS, it's Chromebooks. Look at the defense-in-depth used on Chromebooks to isolate execution: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20795268,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2019-08","story":"Banned C standard library functions in Git source code","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how containers are sandboxed on Chromebooks.","quote":"","text":"Well, you can see how they are locking it down with containers sandboxing from an IO talk, by making use of gVisor and a Rust based containers. \"Linux for Chromebooks: Secure Development\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":20809428,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2019-08","story":"What happens when you launch Google Chrome for the first time?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains architecture of Crostini secure sandbox.","quote":"Linux for Chromebooks (Crostini) gives you a secure sandbox for development.","text":"Regarding ChromeOS: \"Linux for Chromebooks: Secure Development\" > Learn how Linux for Chromebooks (Crostini) gives you a secure sandbox for development. Through a variety of demos, this talk will explain the architecture underlying Linux for Chromebooks and the design decisions that keep it easy to use. [VIDEO] And Android: \"Adopting the Arm Memory Tagging Extension in Android\" [LINK] \" [LINK] [LINK] \"Improving Stability with Private C/C++ Symbol Restrictions in Android N\" [LINK] \"Android NDK Native APIs\" [LINK]"},{"id":19870967,"author":"jt2190","month":"2019-05","story":"All Chromebooks will also be Linux laptops going forward","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on Crostini architecture; commenter summarizes technical details.","quote":"Video of the talk: Linux for Chromebooks, (A.K.A Crostini): Secure Development","text":"Video of the talk: Linux for Chromebooks, (A.K.A Crostini): Secure Development presented by Sudha Broslawsky, Tom Buckley, and Dylan Reid at Google I/O 2019 [VIDEO] Some technical details: * Container: Crostini (default is Debian 9) * Guest: Termina VM: LXD [1], Linux Kernel * Host: CrOS: crosvm, Linux Kernel Drivers are run in \"minijails\" to minimize exposure. Files are shared from container to OS via 9P [2]. Google is calling theirs \"9S\" Edit: I think this in-depth document may be up to date: \"Running Custom Containers Under Chrome OS\" [LINK] [1] from Canonical, see [LINK] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["gbYXBJOFgeI",1],["bnKIVX968PQ",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["8xDK2LgSeyk",1],["ZCmnQR3_qWg",1]]},"vKmQW_Nkfk8":{"wy":"Cited for its critique of talent wasted on social media and for the data-mining-for-biology argument, plus the around-14-minute quitting moment.","q":{"x":"this is an excellent talk about preparing to solve hard problems in bio.","i":3112640,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":3112640,"author":"shoeless","month":"2011-10","story":"BioCurious, a hackerspace for bio now open to public","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk on preparing to solve hard problems in biology.","quote":"this is an excellent talk about preparing to solve hard problems in bio.","text":"I recommend watching Steve Yegge's excellent presentation at O'Reilly's OSCON Data. He points out a number of books in the video, but in general this is an excellent talk about preparing to solve hard problems in bio. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10947374,"author":"betolink","month":"2016-01","story":"The alarming threat of Zika virus","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge on working on things that matter, like molecular biology.","quote":"a talk given at Oscon Data by Steve Yegge about working on the things that matter[0]","text":"This is getting serious here in southern Mexico, we just had a chikungunya outbreak a year ago and now this. These situations remind me of a talk given at Oscon Data by Steve Yegge about working on the things that matter[0]. Virus are complex and modelling molecular biology requires CS expertise, it's kind of sad that we're normally discussing the new JS frameworks instead of working on the hard(and important)problems. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7440282,"author":"jrs235","month":"2014-03","story":"Why I Never Hire Brilliant Men (1924)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like cat picture projects. Interesting how people think the OP story is real and many people mistakeningly think Steve Yegge quit Google (he just quit the project). [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6616386,"author":"jared314","month":"2013-10","story":"Where Have All the Geniuses Gone? ","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge complains about talent focused on social media over hard problems.","quote":"In which he complained about Google, and all the hard working people there, focusing on social media instead of hard problems.","text":"> But instead they feel motivated to work for a big company on long-term useless problems. I remember this opinion also surfacing with the Steve Yegge OSCON 2011 presentation[1]. In which he complained about Google, and all the hard working people there, focusing on social media instead of hard problems. There was a good discussion as to why these problems are not in the forefront. [1] [VIDEO] Subsequent discussion: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":6387901,"author":"zipfle","month":"2013-09","story":"How a Relationship Dies on Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge laments smart people working on cat pictures.","quote":"this talk by Steve Yegge[1] about how stupid it is that so many smart people spend their time working on cat pictures","text":"I closed my fb account two or three years ago, at a point when I was really virulently anti-fb. I still haven't re-opened it, but I've mellowed a bit--not that I approve of it, really--it's a disgusting panopticon at best--but it's at least sufficiently obvious that it's hard for me to feel like anyone hangs out there without understanding what the basic dynamic is. Now, articles like this don't make me feel bad for the kids who 'have to' use facebook (not that they don't; it sucks being ostracized, we all know). Really, they make me remember this talk by Steve Yegge[1] about how stupid it is that so many smart people spend their time working on cat pictures. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6102736,"author":"jsolson","month":"2013-07","story":"Ask HN: What happened to Steve Yegge after 'quitting'","stance":"reference","why":"The OSCON talk behind the quitting rumor.","quote":"check out from ~minute 13 onward","text":"That may have been some people's expectation, but as his public G+ page says[0], he still works at Google. Sergey called his piece a 'night-time aid'[1]. Also Steve's take on it [2]. That said, the OP was talking about his OSCON talk (check out from ~minute 13 onward): [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":4270482,"author":"eshvk","month":"2012-07","story":"The Instagram Effect: Causing Silicon Valley to fund the wrong companies?","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge says data mining should be used more on biology problems.","quote":"See Steve Yegge's talk on how The Data Mining needs to be used more to solve problems in biology","text":"I think there is this (from my limited experience rather naive) belief that if a bunch of smart computer people get together, they can solve problems that have flummoxed biologists for long. This is something pervasive which afflicts even the smartest ones (See Steve Yegge's talk [VIDEO] on how The Data Mining needs to be used more to solve problems in biology). Having spent some time in grad school foolishly believing that all biology needed was a sprinkle of math and a generous dollop of python skills, I think the problem is that in Computer Science we have problems that are sometimes insanely hard but due to the several layers of abstraction, are inherently rather deterministic and much less noisy than real-world systems. I think the best comprehensible example comes from the physical layer of most electronics where several of the modeling equations are naive approximations designed to work under specific conditions. The complexity becomes even more overwhelming when you look at biological systems: The nonlinearity especially in say genetic hacking becomes apparent when you realize that each tiny modification has so many unintended effects (making modeling in a computer environment especially difficult) which is why biologist go through the grind of running all those \"boring\" real world experiments in labs. I think there are quite a few research labs that are using data mining and machine learning techniques to try to solve problems but we are at most quality places well b"},{"id":3987892,"author":"cletus","month":"2012-05","story":"All the oxygen trapped in a bubble","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've called the kneejerk \"bubble\" reactions \"boring\" [1] and I stand by that. That doesn't mean I disagree (or agree for that matter). It just means that banal perjoratives with nothing to back them up are boring . To call this a bubble, one must first describe what one means by a bubble. A bubble in my mind is a period of rapid growth in valuations followed by a massive devaluation on such a scale that it hinders investment and innovation. The subprime collapse was a bubble. Many people were left underwater on their mortgages. Building in many places stopped for years. This has a knock-on effect on jobs, related industries and so on. That's a bubble. The dotcom collapse was also a bubble. But there are several differences this time around: 1. Startup costs now, for most Internet startups, are essentially zero. 10+ years ago you had to spend $5-10M to do anything (once you paid for Sun servers, Oracle licenses and so on); 2. Lax listing requirements, particularly on the NASDAQ, helped perpetuate fraud; 3. There was no experience to draw on (from living memory at least). Now we hopefully know a little better; 4. Sarbanes-Oxley, clusterfuck that it is, has at least kept the number of Internet companies IPOing relatively low. This has some negative consequences too but it means that retail investors and mutual funds have largely been excluded from the startup scene, which honestly is a Good Thing [tm]; and 5. Now, as opposed to then, there are real businesses operating in the te"},{"id":3876897,"author":"cletus","month":"2012-04","story":"Instagram","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge argues brilliant minds waste compute on cat pictures.","quote":"I'm also reminded of Steve Yegge's OSCON talk [2] from some months back.","text":"Congratulations on the investment. The return is nothing short of spectacular. It's a truly excellent example of execution and timing . But the point that keeps resurfacing in my mind is SpaceX. SpaceX, from nothing, created a low-earth orbit delivery system that is revolutionizing satellite launches and (soon) the cost of getting men into space... for less than the price that a bunch of people can send photos to each other with cheesy filters [1]. It would be difficult to overstate the impact Elon Musk has had, is having and will have on humanity (and no this isn't hyperbole) through SpaceX (and maybe even Tesla). And it didn't even require, relate to or is connected with some bullshit social network. I'm also reminded of Steve Yegge's OSCON talk [2] from some months back. The computer power we have available now is stunning. used for the right purposes it could fundamentally change humanity for the good, whether that be in bioinformatics or whatever, is hard to overstate. Yet we're using all this power and the brightest minds on the planet... to send cat pictures. It's actually reached the point that when I get unsolicited recruitment email or read about some new startup on HN that I tune out as soon as I see the word \"social\". There's something astoundingly depressing about all this. EDIt: I should add that my issue isn't that the founders and investors sought wealth. I don't begrudge them that at all. Not by any means am I anti-capitalist. Bill Gates, as one example, is d"},{"id":3565996,"author":"readme","month":"2012-02","story":"Machine Learning for Hackers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Steve Yegge reference","text":"I think these books are not really meant to be a PhD education but more of a tutorial introduction to the field. What O'Reilly is doing right now is really important. As Steve Yegge said, they are \"trying to provoke a culture change.\" Would-be brogrammers will find these books, read them, and graduate from cat-picture projects into more sophisticated applications that can solve real computer science problems. It matters less whether the readers of these books actually get a comprehensive understanding of the theoretical knowledge behind them, then it does that they enrich readers and spread the desire to learn and attain true understanding of the field. Steve Yegge reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3104386,"author":"espeed","month":"2011-10","story":"Steve's Google Platform rant","stance":"reference","why":"Yegge's talk jabs at cat-picture projects and Facebook.","quote":"I loved what he said, and at first glance this seemed like a jab at the newly released Google+.","text":"Is Steve Yegge Google's new secret recruiter agent? :) A few weeks ago he publicly quit his \"cat pictures\" project ( [VIDEO] ) to pursue more noble a quest in data mining. I loved what he said, and at first glance this seemed like a jab at the newly released Google+. But it's actually a bigger knock on Facebook since the \"cat pictures\" app is Facebook's primary gig, and so far it's only a side gig at Google. I wonder how many FB peeps started to wonder if there really is any meaning in cat pictures. Now it's Amazon -- \"Amazon does everything wrong, and Google does everything right\", except for 3 things, one being \"platforms.\" But his Amazon jabs are not as subtle as the cat pictures one -- \"Their pay and benefits suck, although much less so lately due to local competition from Google and Facebook. But they don't have any of our perks or extras.\" Maybe Steve is Google's new unofficial recruiting agent. He makes reference to it here, \"I actually did a spreadsheet at one point but Legal wouldn't let me show it to anyone, even though recruiting loved it.\" When you think about it, he's the perfect person to have run a psyop designed to get the Facebookers and Amazonians to lay down their cat pictures and join the Googlers building the next generation platform, while partaking in all of their perks. Google can just play it off as, \"oh, that's just crazy uncle Steve on one of his rants again\". I don't know what it is, but I think it's great on multiple levels :)"},{"id":2811919,"author":"conorh","month":"2011-07","story":"Steve Yegge quits Google in the middle of his speech [OSCON Data 2011]","stance":"reference","why":"Pinpoints the moment in the talk where Yegge appears to quit.","quote":"At around 14:10 in the speech","text":"At around 14:10 in the speech - [VIDEO] Also I'm not sure if he quit Google, or if he just quit the project he was working on."}]},"0XHC5Kxxv_w":{"wy":"Mostly used as evidence in arguments about early mask messaging, often by critics of public health officials.","m":[{"id":25480888,"author":"fsagx","month":"2020-12","story":"New York Times retracts core of podcast series ‘Caliphate’","stance":"reference","why":"Fauci interview explaining early mask guidance and supply concerns.","quote":"I think this may be the interview where people in the","text":"I think this may be the interview where people in the \"he lied\" camp may be referencing: [VIDEO] “…when people were saying you don’t really need to wear a mask well the reason for that is that we were concerned the public health community and many people were saying this were concerned that it was at a time when personal protective equipment including the N95 masks and the surgical masks were in very short supply and we wanted to make sure that the people namely the health care workers who were brave enough to put themselves in a harm ways to take care of people who you know are infected with the corona virus and the danger of them getting infected we did not want them to be without the equipment that they needed so there was not enthusiasm about going out and everybody buying a mask or getting a mask we were afraid that that would deter away and the people who really needed it”"},{"id":28418421,"author":"choward","month":"2021-09","story":"Doctors and pharmacists ramp up calls for Americans to stop using ivermectin","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is why people don't trust this guy.","text":"> \"Right now, in the United States, there's no reason to be walking around with a mask.\" And when he did decide we should be walking around with masks he didn't cite any evidence. He said it was because surgical and N95s masks were in short supply. [VIDEO] When I was trying to find this video, I came across another video where he speaks in absolute terms that all we need to do is wears masks, social distance and practice good hygiene we would turn around the pandemic. I understand that things change over time, but whenever he speaks, he speaks as if what he is saying is fact. Now we even have vaccines and we clearly haven't turned it around. This is why people don't trust this guy. [LINK] I honestly don't see how you can watch all these videos and still trust this guy. I get that things change, but that's why you don't speak in absolute terms. Words matter."},{"id":24064322,"author":"mistermann","month":"2020-08","story":"Science and scientific expertise are more important than ever","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he knowingly misled (as one player in a coordinated effort) the public about the usefulness of the general public wearing masks","text":"> he knows, but can't communicate what he knows One example of this was when he knowingly misled (as one player in a coordinated effort) the public about the usefulness of the general public wearing masks at the initial outbreak of the pandemic. Now that the truth came out, Trump supporters have indeed latched onto this as proof that what the medical establishment says is not always true. Whether the benefit was worth the cost is another one of those things that is unknown. [VIDEO] (1:30 onward) [LINK] (Note the rhetorical \"fact checking\" warning about the video. Whether you like it or not, people will react to this behavior in ways that are non-beneficial to overall society. I wonder if that is taken into consideration when strategic decisions are made behind closed doors on what representation of reality is projected into people's minds. Another one of those things that will remain unknown I imagine."},{"id":23668550,"author":"ed-209","month":"2020-06","story":"The CDC lost control of the coronavirus pandemic, then the agency disappeared","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"mask wearing was discouraged in order to secure availability for health workers","text":"\"Believing people without symptoms didn't spread the virus, the agency limited testing, discouraged masking...\" This is false by Fauci's own account (see below interview) in which he states mask wearing was discouraged in order to secure availability for health workers. This is no small distinction as it raises questions about the importance of public trust/credibility during a health crisis. [VIDEO]"}]},"6yN2H3--1aw":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke about nerds faking normalcy or sports small talk.","m":[{"id":39273540,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2024-02","story":"Why buying tickets to a game has become so unaffordable","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Did you see that ludicrous display last night?","text":"> Plus it's easy small talk fodder. \"Did you see that ludicrous display last night?\" * [VIDEO]"},{"id":29757126,"author":"Lio","month":"2022-01","story":"Intellectual Loneliness","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"E.g. \"Did you see that ludicrous display last night\"[1]","text":"I'm always amused when I hear this point of view. It's a bit like telling someone that likes Jazz that they should just like auto-tune pop music because it's what everyone else likes. I'm self-aware enough to have considered that I might just be bad at communicating but still don't seem to be able to do much about it. > My experience is the opposite. I guess most people would like to be seen as an \"everyman\" that can talk to anyone but we're just not all wired that way. There's plenty of popular culture tropes that deal with that. E.g. \"Did you see that ludicrous display last night\"[1] It gets easier with age but being boring , if you want to call it that, is OK too. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24717111,"author":"gridlockd","month":"2020-10","story":"Why Nerds Are Unpopular (2003)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Unlike in this satirical clip, I don't think it's possible to fake being \"a normal guy\".","text":"> Your comment about awkwardness/inability to hold a conversation doesn't strike true either. Rather, I felt like no one else could hold a conversation. If you believe the problem is with \"everyone else\", the problem really is with you . Nerds generally struggle with holding conversations without constantly sending \"out-group\" signals while failing to send \"in-group\" signals. [VIDEO] Unlike in this satirical clip, I don't think it's possible to fake being \"a normal guy\". However, in my experience, it's possible to build rapport by being respectful and signaling that you get where the other person is coming from. Avoid being intellectually dominating. That way, you'll be perceived as \"that okay smart guy\" instead of \"that fucking nerd\". Even if you don't care about being popular, learning how to read people is an extremely important skill, especially if you're somewhere on the autism spectrum. Just approach it as another nerd subject (human psychology)."}]},"9mzPj_IaMzY":{"wy":"Cited in policing debates as an example of de-escalation and subduing an armed suspect without guns.","q":{"x":"Here's how the UK engage someone wielding a machete, without guns or any other weapons beside extendable batons.","i":23377076,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35070003,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2023-03","story":"Gang Members Hold Positions at ‘Highest Levels’ of LA Sheriff’s Department","stance":"reference","why":"Police mobbing a suspect with superior numbers, no harm done","quote":"mobbing with massively superior numbers, resulting in no harm to anyone","text":"> if you want to enforce the law for dangerous and violent people, doesn’t it seem reasonable you’ll need at least equally if not more dangerous and violent people to do so? No. There are other ways. For example, mobbing with massively superior numbers, resulting in no harm to anyone, and a suspect getting the hospital care they needed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32025788,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-07","story":"Former PM Abe Shinzo dies after being shot","stance":"reference","why":"UK police handling of a machete-wielding individual","quote":"How the UK police dealt with a machete-wielding individual","text":"In most other countries, training encourages the use of lethal force as a last resort. In Germany (pop. 84M), in 2015, a total of 101 rounds were discharged by police for the entire year: * [LINK] How the UK police dealt with a machete-wielding individual: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":29606028,"author":"mrsuprawsm","month":"2021-12","story":"Man dies after he is shot by the police with a stun gun and catches fire","stance":"reference","why":"UK police tactics for subduing an armed man","quote":"It takes a bit of effort and coordination but is obviously massively preferable","text":"There’s a fair number of videos from eg the UK where they do exactly this. The usual playbook seems to be: get a few people distracting the guy from the front, and another few rush them from the back whilst distracted and hit them with batons or just jump on them. Or just distract them for a long while and wait for more backup and people with shields. [VIDEO] It takes a bit of effort and coordination but is obviously massively preferable to being executed on the spot by the state."},{"id":27240611,"author":"BoorishBears","month":"2021-05","story":"Leaked emails show crime app Citizen is testing on-demand security force","stance":"reference","why":"Example of non-lethal police response to a knife attacker","quote":"Like the stuff in those videos is not easy","text":"This is missing the forest for the trees so badly it hurts. Why do you think I said I'd be forced to shoot? Because even without training common sense tells you \"not allowing the attacker to close enough distance with you to use the knife\" is a pretty good course of action By your logic the moment a person calls 911, the people who are supposed to help, the person has been sentenced to death. Think about that for a second. 1. The cops will arrive 2. \"avoid knife getting close to me\" 3. Less than lethal is not reliable, hell even lethal force isn't instant, I feared for my life, they're shot very dead. - It's not an easy problem, but how is that ok? There's not many ways to fix it other than trying something other than lethal force. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Like the stuff in those videos is not easy, I'm not trying to pretend I could do it, or most people could. It requires cops being able to put the most efficient response for self-preservation behind trying to save someone, which again, I'm not saying we require of anyone... But let's call a spade a spade at that point. That's not the concept of policing I see paraded. That's not the \"thin blue line\", it sounds more like a cell of civilians that are deployed to bad situations where they then \"apply self-defense\"..."},{"id":25673230,"author":"cygx","month":"2021-01","story":"Infosec implications of the US Capitol physical breach","stance":"reference","why":"Example that armed suspects can be arrested without shooting","quote":"it can be done (eg made the news a while ago)","text":"Circumstances matter, but it can be done (eg [VIDEO] made the news a while ago). Here in Germany, people wielding knives get killed from time to time as well. Pepper spray tends to get used first, and I do not remember any case where a dozen rounds were fired[1] (German police fire on the order of 50 rounds total per year, cf [LINK] ). [1] edit: apparently, this does happen, cf [LINK]"},{"id":23377076,"author":"chrisseaton","month":"2020-06","story":"Mapping Police Violence","stance":"reference","why":"UK police disarm machete suspect using shields and numbers","quote":"Here's how the UK engage someone wielding a machete, without guns or any other weapons beside extendable batons.","text":"> If you were a police officer, would you, unarmed, engage another person wielding a hatchet? I'd say that's unlikely. Would you even take the job if you were unarmed? Great example! Here's how the UK engage someone wielding a machete, without guns or any other weapons beside extendable batons. [VIDEO] They try to deescalate, they bring in reinforcements, they surround, then they disarm him using shields, not weapons, and he survives to stand a fair trial. No guns needed. And if they thought they couldn't arrest him using shields they could call in a specialist firearms unit, who are specifically trained, authorised, and accountable."},{"id":21682157,"author":"alistairSH","month":"2019-12","story":"ACLU to police using robots: Tell us more","stance":"reference","why":"UK police apprehend machete suspect without guns","quote":"this is the police in the UK apprehending a suspect with a machete.","text":"I was going to bring that up. Totally agree - further normalizing the use of violence by the police should be avoided. While not a sniper, this is the police in the UK apprehending a suspect with a machete. I've never heard of similar tactics being used in the US. And conversations with police have led me to believe they would actively avoid such tactics and instead resort to violence. [VIDEO]"}]},"ALZZx1xmAzg":{"wy":"Commenters drop it as an obligatory joke in threads about piracy, DRM and copyright enforcement.","q":{"x":"here is a funny parody of these warnings","i":3476916,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":44002757,"author":"dspillett","month":"2025-05","story":"Internet Artifacts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"probably being the most famous","text":"If you were around and paying for media at the time, good have seen it before films at the cinema and as a pre-menu sting (sometimes unskippable) on some DVDs. One of those examples where paying customers were the only ones irritated by such a measure. It certainly had wide audience awareness in its intended form, which is why the many comedy interpretations ( [VIDEO] probably being the most famous) came into being and worked so well."},{"id":42540613,"author":"sstephant","month":"2024-12","story":"Piracy in the UK: the failed war on illegal content","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I never saw those piracy messages in my country but I still remember 'the It crowd''s version of it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35726141,"author":"prepend","month":"2023-04","story":"Netflix Loses 1M Users in Spain over Password Policing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I think following the law is ethical, and copyright is not only the law, it follows directly from the Constitution. Copyright was 14 years at the time of the Constitution. If it was still that, I’d have no problem waiting it out and only watching material 14 years old. Following laws isn’t always ethical. The constitution also allowed slavery. Do you think owning slaves was ethical? There’s lots of instances where laws are out of sync with ethics and morality, and, hopefully change over time. In the past 20 years gay marriage became legal and I would argue it’s always ethical. Same for recreational drug use. And therapeutic mdma and ketamine. The list goes on and on. Especially when the copyright laws aren’t the result of some specific debate and determination but by very targeted lobbying by Disney and others to extend copyright from 14 years to its current length of life+70 years or 95 years for corporate works. Upending copyright would have nothing to do with trademarks (perpetual as long as in use) or patents (usually 20 years) and can be reformed completely independently. Although those other IP topics could also be improved. I’m not making a judgement on your beliefs or how you formed them. I don’t know you. But there are PR campaigns to promote copyright targeted toward school kids (remember “don’t copy that floppy” [0] and “you wouldn’t steal a car” [1]) and I think they are in alignment with “copying a friend’s video is like stealing bread” so you just seem to be u"},{"id":21311181,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2019-10","story":"Disney+ streaming uses draconian DRM","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory IT Crowd reference","text":"> It reminds me of those \"you wouldn't kill\" segments at the start of DVD's that you couldn't skip ... Obligatory IT Crowd reference: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":16680808,"author":"starshadowx2","month":"2018-03","story":"Kim Dotcom wins battle in ongoing fight against U.S. extradition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"You wouldn't shoot a policeman, and then steal his helmet. You wouldn't go to the toilet in his helmet and then send it to the policeman's grieving widow, and then steal it again.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":6232914,"author":"Luc","month":"2013-08","story":"The Coming War on General Computation (2011)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> just like in that \"parody\" video where the SWAT team shoots the kid who was downloading songs in the head at the end IT Crowd: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4828293,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-11","story":"Raided 9-Year-Old Pirate Bay Girl Came To Save Us All","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This \"piracy war\" is starting to look more and more like the drug war. I could see how in US especially, if marijuana is going to be legalized, all those agencies which would now be left out of work, could refocus on raiding \"pirates\", barging in and shooting people's dogs, and whatnot. Then this parody might become a reality (the part with the girl at the end): [VIDEO]"},{"id":3476916,"author":"nkassis","month":"2012-01","story":"SOPA lives—and MPAA calls protests an \"abuse of power\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here is a funny parody of these warnings","text":"Yeah and what does Dodd and the MPAA call those stupid warnings and crap they add at the beginning of every movie telling us we are thief etc. ? If you don't know what I mean, here is a funny parody of these warnings: [VIDEO] The Hypocrisy is at an all new level."}]},"AmUC4m6w1wo":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete early example that AI-faked video was already convincing, in discussions of misinformation and trust in media.","q":{"x":"It's becoming easier and easier to fake image, video, and audio in a way never seen before","i":25385099,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25385099,"author":"MonAlternatiu","month":"2020-12","story":"We can have democracy or we can have Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that faking video is getting easier.","quote":"It's becoming easier and easier to fake image, video, and audio in a way never seen before","text":"Many reasons. - For one when a news article is incorrect it is very easy to hold accountable the source of that information. How many people keep track of who said what on the internet? - Fake Information takes more time to respond to than true information. False news travels 6 times faster (..) than truthful news [0]. - Media has a cycle of information that is way slower and more curated (even if manipulative) which allows true information to be heard louder. - It's becoming easier and easier to fake image, video, and audio in a way never seen before [1]. - It's way easier for platforms like FB and Twitter and YT to make you spiral down a rabbit hole of similar content. Sure, this did happen in the past, but the current level of detailed recommendations makes it really easy for people to lose context inside their bubble. - People are not machines, if they hear a message many times by different sources that trigger the right emotional responses they will believe it to be true. Now group every point and compound it by a lot of consumption and ingest it into monkey-like brains :) [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17532224,"author":"y4mi","month":"2018-07","story":"Labour HQ used Facebook ads to deceive Jeremy Corbyn during election campaign","stance":"reference","why":"Example of convincing fake video footage.","quote":"It's frighteningly convincing.","text":"It's called agree of misinformation because it's become increasingly easy to produce convincing fake footage. It's entirely possible to fake a real-time video interview for instance using voice synthesis and mapping the face of your target upon your face. It's frighteningly convincing. I.e. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17358536,"author":"Nadya","month":"2018-06","story":"Keybase Exploding Messages","stance":"reference","why":"Example of AI-faked video of Obama.","quote":"All of these can already be believably faked","text":"Photo, audio, and video evidence should already be dismissed until one is able to verify the integrity and source. All of these can already be believably faked - it's just a matter of educating people that a layperson can easily create fake things by using tools developed by research teams. Fake text is the easiest to fake if you can identify the font used - any image editor will work. HN uses 9pt Verdana, even without using dev tools I could fake your post to say anything I wanted it to say since it would just be 9pt Verdana on a solid background set to text wrap every 1050px. See: [LINK] & [VIDEO]"}]},"BHup81lEjqo":{"wy":"Cited as an example of artists hiding visuals in audio spectrograms.","q":{"x":"exploited by artists like Venetian Snares [1] and Aphex Twin [2]","i":32956397,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":46470295,"author":"duskwuff","month":"2026-01","story":"Daft Punk Easter Egg in the BPM Tempo of Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger?","stance":"reference","why":"Better visualization of the track.","quote":"There's a better visualization of the track here","text":"Into the final track of an album titled \"Songs About My Cats\", titled \"Look\". :3 There's a better visualization of the track here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32956397,"author":"stuxnet79","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What's the point of audio waveform visualization?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of artists encoding visuals in audio spectrograms.","quote":"exploited by artists like Venetian Snares [1] and Aphex Twin [2]","text":"> It can be art A fact that's been exploited by artists like Venetian Snares [1] and Aphex Twin [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] Say what you will about \"IDM\" but the whole genre / movement enabled quite a bit of creative expression and range. I'd be hard pressed to see tracks like these being included in albums in any other context."},{"id":40429734,"author":"Y_Y","month":"2024-05","story":"Images that Sound: Generating spectrograms that are also images","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is the title supposed to be a joke? \"Images that sound\" really doesn't _sound_ right to me. Also I believe the missed out on citing the pioneering work of Snares et al. 2001 [0] [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"DgrO_rAaiq0":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in polygraph and lie-detector threads.","m":[{"id":47116348,"author":"Anthony-G","month":"2026-02","story":"Personal Statement of a CIA Analyst","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This comment reminds me of one of my favourite scenes from The Wire","text":"This comment reminds me of one of my favourite scenes from The Wire: [VIDEO] “Americans are stupid people, by and large. We pretty much believe whatever we’re told”. “The bigger the lie, the more they believe”."},{"id":45941886,"author":"defrost","month":"2025-11","story":"FBI Director Waived Polygraph Security Screening for Three Senior Staff","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Classic moment where a copy machine is used for a lie detector.","text":"Police interviews on scripted TV copoganda dramas, or fly on the wall 'reality' police TV shows? Either way a great many police are believers in the power of theatre. As they should be, staging power imbalnce, inserting faux sympathy, etc. are all powerful tools with strong potential for misuse which has prompted regulation in a number of countries demanding full recording of interactions with suspects. Speaking of police interviews on TV shows: Opening scene to Season 5 of The Wire. Classic moment where a copy machine is used for a lie detector. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19954254,"author":"bcaa7f3a8bbc","month":"2019-05","story":"U.S. Judge Orders “Immediate Removal” of Website Critical of Voice Lie Detector","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of this hilarious video clip","text":"> However, the power in the lie detector comes from its name - \"The Lie Detector\" - and the common belief that it actually works. Ha. It reminds me of this hilarious video clip ( [VIDEO] ) from The Wire , S5E01, which Detective William \"Bunk\" Moreland interrogated DeShawn and made him believe the office copy machine was a lie detector. This plot itself was based on an false urban legend. > [...] they are also not admissible in court, as the law understands that they don't actually work. But in an interrogation process [...] it is a good arrow in the quiver. I think the first things come to my mind is: what types of psychological tricks are and aren't allowed in an interrogation process? Is it permissible to use a nonworking \"lie detector\" as an interrogation technique? What are the potential abuses of this trick, etc? These are some interesting things that can be argued and debated. I don't have background knowledge any of these subjects I mentioned so I won't start a debate, other interesting may want to do so. Anyway, interesting perspective. I appreciate your comment, which moved the question from the first layer: \"does a polygraph work and whether we should use it\", to the next layer: \"a polygraph doesn't work, but should we use it anyway?\""},{"id":18434500,"author":"3JPLW","month":"2018-11","story":"The Lie Behind the Lie Detector","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Example in fiction","text":"It's a useful interrogation technique, but only if the public believes it works. Example in fiction: [VIDEO]"}]},"EPk8MVEmiTI":{"wy":"HN cites it as the classic early example of a computer producing music through electromagnetic emissions, in threads on System Bus Radio and similar hacks.","q":{"x":"Live demo at the Computer History Museum, of an IBM 1401 playing music via AM radio bus emissions.","i":16172194,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":31730535,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2022-06","story":"The Floppotron 3.0","stance":"reference","why":"Example of unconventional hardware music","quote":"","text":"Awesome, the 2.0 decommissioning last week had scared. There's a spectacular variety of ways that music has been made in unconventional electromechanical ways--on the head motors of floppy drives 3.5\", 5.25\", and 8\", on hard drive voice coils, scanner carriage motors, steppers wherever the're found in CNC devices, dot-matrix printheads, pulsed laser cutters, tesla coils, all the way back to radio interference generated by the IBM 1401 ( [VIDEO] ). The 1401 video I actually saw in an older related HN submission; lots of comments linking out to different examples in these threads. Here are a couple, someone might be able to aggregate a bunch more: \"Eye of the Tiger\" played on a dot-matrix printer: [LINK] (2015, 62 comments) \"Imperial March\" on a single floppy drive: [LINK] (2011, 27 comments) Both the news site and original video of the second submission are lost to time, but luckily our saviour (of web content) Brewster Kahle has graced us with a copy in the Internet Archive.[1] The Wayback Machine also remembers a time when YouTube recommendations bore greater relevance--those on the archived video page from 2011[2] are entirely of videos of computer hardware music. Some might even still be up today. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] \"Imperial March\" was also what was played on the first incarnation of the Floppotron, with an impressively full sound from only two floppy drives ( [LINK] ). And perhaps related are those videos of the pleasingly periodic percussion of uncontrolled devices l"},{"id":25453137,"author":"kens","month":"2020-12","story":"Transmits AM radio on computers without radio transmitting hardware","stance":"reference","why":"IBM 1401 playing music on an AM radio","quote":"Here's a video of the IBM 1401 punch card computer (1959) at the Computer History Museum playing music on an AM radio","text":"This technique goes back to the early days of computers. Here's a video of the IBM 1401 punch card computer (1959) at the Computer History Museum playing music on an AM radio: [VIDEO] The same computer could also play music using the line printer: [LINK] I discuss how the line printer music worked here: [LINK]"},{"id":16172194,"author":"fernly","month":"2018-01","story":"System Bus Radio: Transmits AM radio on computers without transmitting hardware","stance":"reference","why":"Live demo of IBM 1401 playing AM radio music","quote":"Live demo at the Computer History Museum, of an IBM 1401 playing music via AM radio bus emissions.","text":"Live demo at the Computer History Museum, of an IBM 1401 playing music via AM radio bus emissions. [VIDEO] You can see the 1401 demo'd on Wednesdays and Saturdays -- although they don't do this in the regular demo, just read cards and print on the line printer."},{"id":11205644,"author":"kens","month":"2016-03","story":"System Bus Radio","stance":"reference","why":"IBM 1401 producing music via radio interference","quote":"The IBM 1401 (1960's era) could do this","text":"Generating music via radio interference goes way, way back. The IBM 1401 (1960's era) could do this: [VIDEO] It could also play music on the line printer by banging out the right sequence of characters. [LINK] Strangely, the 1401 radio music was later set to orchestra and released as a CD by an Icelandic composer: [LINK]"},{"id":9287018,"author":"kens","month":"2015-03","story":"Dot matrix printer playing “Eye of the Tiger” [video]","stance":"reference","why":"IBM 1401 playing tunes via radio interference","quote":"here's the 1401 playing tunes on a radio via RF interference from the core memory","text":"That's pretty impressive. Printer music goes way back, by the way. Here's a (low-quality) recording of a 1960s-era IBM 1401 mainframe playing the Blue Danube Waltz on a 1403 line printer: [LINK] And here's the 1401 playing tunes on a radio via RF interference from the core memory: [VIDEO] Unfortunately the 1401 at the Computer History Museum isn't currently authorized for line printer music because the stress might destroy the print chain and they don't have a replacement."}]},"HMGIbOGu8q0":{"wy":"Used as a punchline in threads about snake oil, bogus tests and startup fraud, rather than recommended as such.","m":[{"id":47831348,"author":"Terr_","month":"2026-04","story":"The insider trading suspicions looming over Trump's presidency","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The snake-oil suspicions looming over colored quartz and homeopathic water.","text":"In the sense of extreme understatement , yes, which is amusing since the \"heavy lifting\" is the other way around. Much like \"The snake-oil suspicions looming over colored quartz and homeopathic water.\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11524283,"author":"wstrange","month":"2016-04","story":"Theranos Is Subject of Criminal Probe by U.S","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Clearly they should have diluted the samples further.","text":"> blood constituents are not evenly distributed at small volumes Clearly they should have diluted the samples further. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9119141,"author":"jamiesoncj","month":"2015-02","story":"Building Microservices the Lean Way, part 2","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"your comment did make me re-watch this","text":"Oh right. I didn't get it. I thought tech homeopathy was some new framework / library / approach I hadn't heard about. Maybe homeopathy.js or similar? On the plus side, your comment did make me re-watch this: [VIDEO]"}]},"M9xMuPWAZW8":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical example of hiding images in audio spectrograms.","q":{"x":"here's an example of musician Aphex Twin hiding his face in a spectrogram of one of his songs","i":23523777,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23523777,"author":"alchemyromcom","month":"2020-06","story":"With Musical Cryptography, Composers Can Hide Messages in Their Melodies","stance":"reference","why":"Aphex Twin hid his face in a song's spectrogram.","quote":"here's an example of musician Aphex Twin hiding his face in a spectrogram of one of his songs","text":"This might be a widely known secret by now, but here's an example of musician Aphex Twin hiding his face in a spectrogram of one of his songs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16559305,"author":"jcims","month":"2018-03","story":"AI Has a Hallucination Problem That's Proving Tough to Fix","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I often wonder how much the multi-sensory aspect plays into it. When I see an image, I don't really process it as an image, I map that visual cue into the full gamut of sensory memories (?) of that object. I could write a page worth of these descriptive meatspace 'vectors' that are invoked when i see a banana and color my interpretation of its context. If my understanding is remotely correct, an RNN's view of a banana is basically like the face in Aphex Twin's Equation - [VIDEO] (headphone users beware). No qualitative or quantitative information about the object, just a certain tone of integer triplets in a cacophony of noise. It seems like a many-dimensional view of the world around us is going to be necessary for systems to more effectively intuit about interacting with it. It could be something we synthetically inject or we may need to give our models new senses they can use to extract their own meaning."},{"id":9686998,"author":"cnvogel","month":"2015-06","story":"Show HN: Small C Program to Convert Photos to Audio","stance":"reference","why":"Spectrogram image hidden in Aphex Twin track.","quote":"Aphex Twin had hidden spectrogram images in some of his tracks in the past","text":"Aphex Twin had hidden spectrogram images in some of his tracks in the past, unfortunately I think they are not well preserved by multiple steps of compression/reencoding to youtube videos. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"TF8THY5spmo":{"wy":"HN links it as direct observational evidence for a massive invisible object at the galactic center.","q":{"x":"here is a time lapse video of stars orbiting Sag A","i":19149203,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":42191692,"author":"westurner","month":"2024-11","story":"Show HN: Physically accurate black hole simulation using your iPhone camera","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an actual observation of black hole surroundings.","quote":"","text":"\"static\" is \"Shwarzchild without rotation\"? Do black holes have hair? Where is the Hawking radiation in these models? Does it diffuse through the boundary and the outer system? What about black hole jets? What about vortices? With Gross-Pitaevskii and SQR Superfluid Quantum Relativity [LINK] Ctrl-F Fedi , Bernoulli, Gross-Pitaevskii: > \"Gravity as a fluid dynamic phenomenon in a superfluid quantum space. Fluid quantum gravity and relativity.\" (2015) [LINK] : > FWIU: also rejects a hard singularity boundary, describes curl and vorticity in fluids (with Gross-Pitaevskii,), and rejects antimatter. Actual observations of black holes; \"This image shows the observed image of M87's black hole (left) the simulation obtained with a General Relativistic Magnetohydrodynamics model, blurred to the resolution of the Event Horizon Telescope [...]\" [LINK] \"Stars orbiting the black hole at the heart of the Milky Way\" ESO. [VIDEO] \"Motion of stars around Sagittarius A*\" Keck/UCLA. [LINK] /? M87a time lapse /? Sagittarius A time lapse /? black hole vortex dynamics"},{"id":19149203,"author":"cilefen","month":"2019-02","story":"How Supermassive Black Holes Were Discovered","stance":"reference","why":"Time lapse of stars orbiting Sagittarius A*.","quote":"here is a time lapse video of stars orbiting Sag A","text":"No actual photos yet exist of the environment immediately surrounding a black hole. But here is a time lapse video of stars orbiting Sag A: [VIDEO] . The Event Horizon Telescope project is trying to image some black hole surroundings [LINK]"},{"id":18128112,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2018-10","story":"Black holes ruled out as universe’s missing dark matter","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of stars orbiting an invisible massive object at the galactic center.","quote":"stars orbiting something that's a) very massive, and b) invisible","text":"Then there's this video ESO's Very Large Telescope shot[0], of stars orbiting something that's a) very massive, and b) invisible. Does that constitute a direct observation of a black hole? -- [0] - [VIDEO]"}]},"TbHBHhZOglw":{"wy":"HN uses it as an example of Google's search answers confidently giving the wrong date for the invention.","q":{"x":"it'll pop up with a giant, confident answer of 1984, despite the fact that if you do the research yourself you can find the first patent for it was filed in 1954.","i":33008671,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33008671,"author":"hbn","month":"2022-09","story":"Google Broke Image Search for Creative Commons","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates Google confidently giving a wrong invention date for the touch lamp.","quote":"it'll pop up with a giant, confident answer of 1984, despite the fact that if you do the research yourself you can find the first patent for it was filed in 1954.","text":"There's also something to be said about how Google should be returning information, not answers. Skip to the 1:00 mark in this Technology Connections video where he demonstrates how if you ask Google when the touch lamp was invented it'll pop up with a giant, confident answer of 1984, despite the fact that if you do the research yourself you can find the first patent for it was filed in 1954. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27623575,"author":"slipframe","month":"2021-06","story":"Google turned me into a serial killer","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Google's knowledge graph taking internet claims at face value.","quote":"the problem with Google's knowledge graph is that it takes everything it reads on the internet at face value.","text":"Technology Connections had a great observation about this in his video about touch lamps: the problem with Google's knowledge graph is that it takes everything it reads on the internet at face value. [VIDEO] (0:58 through 4:20)"},{"id":29554601,"author":"BrazzVuvuzela","month":"2021-12","story":"Wolfram Cloud","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"many of the answers google offers are not calculated at all, but instead are regurgitated nonsense","text":"Taking google's answer for things they're calculating with math is probably safe, but if you get into the habit of reading google's answers to queries it will have you believing a lot of nonsense because many of the answers google offers are not calculated at all, but instead are regurgitated nonsense that google read on the internet and took at face value (example from Technology Connections: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":24528264,"author":"ziml77","month":"2020-09","story":"Coffee Rust","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"One example of an answer that looks simple and definitive but was actually way off","text":"Please don't blindly trust those answer boxes. One example of an answer that looks simple and definitive but was actually way off: [VIDEO]"}]},"U7s5pT3Rris":{"wy":"HN brings it up in Iran-related threads as a critical or darkly joking reminder of casual hawkish rhetoric.","m":[{"id":41312805,"author":"text0404","month":"2024-08","story":"Ares Industries – Building low-cost cruise missiles","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"here's a fun one","text":"here's some intro material: [LINK] [LINK] here's a fun one: [VIDEO] - \"McCain Sings \"Bomb bomb bomb, bomb bomb Iran\" also check out the proxy wars being fought in Syria, Lebanon, and Yemen between the US and Iran in lieu of all-out ground war. i was going to start adding links from Trump's presidency but there's just so many..."},{"id":34959046,"author":"pazimzadeh","month":"2023-02","story":"Israel's Dangerous Shadow War with Iran","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Unlike John McCain singing","text":"The translation for that is questionable [LINK] Unlike John McCain singing \"bomb bomb Iran\" during his 2008 campaign [VIDEO] Iran has also never shot down a US passenger flight [LINK]"},{"id":27344530,"author":"belatw","month":"2021-05","story":"After years of conflict and instability, Iraq is opening up to tourism","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"presidential candidates gleefully parodying beach boy songs about bombing Iran.","text":"> What about the Iranian approach to the U.S.? What if Iran stopped development of nuclear weapons and attacking U.S. forces in Iraq and elsewhere, or sponsoring organizations that are destabilizing the Middle East? It's not like they're not also agitating the situation. It's in their best interest because that way they can maintain power. It takes threee to tango in this situation Provocations and aggressions that maybe the US should perhaps stop doing: - presidential candidates gleefully parodying beach boy songs about bombing Iran. [1] - spending many 10s of billions of America’s hard earned tax dollars surroinsing Iran with several dozen military bases. [2] - Assassinating or supporting Israel in assassinating Iranian scientists abroad.[3] - or assassinating Iranian military leaders [4] - Organizing violent military coups to replace democratically elected governments with Radical Islamist puppet dictatorships [5], [6] - Gatekeeping nuclear power by releasing destructive viruses into the world to destroy Iranian Nuclear research labs [7] 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] 5. [LINK] 6. [LINK] 7. [LINK]"},{"id":24746726,"author":"fit2rule","month":"2020-10","story":"US Seizes Domains Used by Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I wonder if McCain meant his death chant literally","text":"Oh, its a slogan .. I thought we were talking about official government policy. I wonder if McCain meant his death chant literally: [VIDEO] Its probably a good thing he didn't get a chance to set official US policy on Iran. EDIT: /s"}]},"YwZ0ZUy7P3E":{"wy":"HN uses it as shorthand in birth-rate and genetics threads, usually half-jokingly and sometimes with pushback that it oversimplifies.","m":[{"id":41882923,"author":"WorldPeas","month":"2024-10","story":"US startup charging couples to 'screen embryos for IQ'","stance":"reference","why":"Idiocracy intro cited as parallel to the story.","quote":"Reminds me of the idiocracy intro.","text":"Reminds me of the idiocracy intro. All people think about is getting more, until they can no longer have it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19043286,"author":"supermatt","month":"2019-01","story":"Harried South Koreans pamper pets instead of having kids","stance":"reference","why":"Idiocracy opening scene cited as satire of declining birth rates.","quote":"Sounds like a sure-fire way to accelerate idiocracy","text":"Sounds like a sure-fire way to accelerate idiocracy... [VIDEO] \"Evolution does not necessarily reward intelligence. With no natural predators to thin the herd, it began to simply reward those who reproduced the most - and left the intelligent to become an endangered species\" Edit: For those that seem to think I'm calling people who have children stupid - idiocracy is a satirical movie. I would suggest you actually watch the link i posted (i.e. at least \"read\" the comment before you reply to it...)"},{"id":17302736,"author":"millzlane","month":"2018-06","story":"Birth Rates Dropped Most in U.S. Counties Where Home Prices Grew Most","stance":"reference","why":"Idiocracy opening scene cited about birth rates and home prices.","quote":"Reminds me of the opening scene of the movie Idiocracy.","text":"Reminds me of the opening scene of the movie Idiocracy. It makes sense that those that can afford the greater priced homes are thoughtful enough not to have lots of children. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16810006,"author":"DanielleMolloy","month":"2018-04","story":"'I’m not having children because I want to save the planet'","stance":"reference","why":"Idiocracy opening scene cited about intelligent people not having children.","quote":"I have to think about the opening scene of Idiocracy","text":"Every time I hear a rational, intelligent person expounding why they decided not to procreate, I have to think about the opening scene of Idiocracy: [VIDEO] Don't get me wrong, everybody is free to make their own decisions (and I would be the last demanding from anybody to go through parenthood), but sometimes it seems as if intelligence is not an evolutionary advantage. There are whole subcultures of people who spend their life breeding, while especially the DNA lines of intelligent, working women just seem to end there. And it does not help that there are all these seemingly rational argumentation lines against children, e.g. about environmental impact. If you can pass on your education (and maybe some intelligence-related DNA), it may help society more overall in the end. Think of a big birthday party of an educated grandparent you may have visited - this procreation thing is an exponential function."},{"id":15214337,"author":"wernercd","month":"2017-09","story":"Massive genetic study shows how humans are evolving","stance":"reference","why":"Idiocracy opening scene as illustration of reproduction versus intelligence.","quote":"For reference, see Idiocracy opening sequence","text":"For reference, see Idiocracy opening sequence... [VIDEO] Smart people delay, wait and then end up not... \"dumb\" people just hook up and go to town... I think the fact that Evolution is still spreading \"successful\" genes and \"unsuccessful\" genes become less prevalent is obvious. What isn't obvious, is as Idiocracy so succinctly puts it, is that gene that are \"successful\" doesn't necessarily translate to intelligence, good decisions, stable lifestyle, etc."},{"id":23323863,"author":"simias","month":"2020-05","story":"Trump threatens to 'close' down social media platforms","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"if you take it seriously to talk about politics it has very sinister undertones","text":"I understand what you mean but I'm always frustrated when I see Idiocracy brought up in these discussions. Idiocracy is fine if you view it as a light satirical comedy but if you take it seriously to talk about politics it has very sinister undertones. For one thing it's extremely classist, throughout the movie popular culture is seen as fodder for dumb people while high culture if for clever people. Beyond that it also says that, effectively, dumb people and poor people are the same thing (as exemplified by the \"white trash\" segment at the start of the movie) and that dumb, poor people are bound to breed dump, poor people (and apparently they do that a lot) while clever people would breed other clever people (but they don't do it because... reasons). So social determinism taken to the limit. I mean just look at this intro: [VIDEO] What are examples of clever people? Darwin, Beethoven and Da Vince. Examples of \"degeneracy\"? A girl in skimpy clothes, wrestling and a... woman with boxing gloves? Because clearly \"panem et circenses\" is a novel concept. Then we go to say \"with no natural predators to thin the herd, we began to simply began to reward those who reproduced the most and left the intelligent to become an endangered species\". So we're now talking full-on eugenics. Also Beethoven was well known for fending packs of wolves in his youth, proving his evolutionary superiority. And I'm this point I'm literally one minute into the movie and I could go on and on and on. At bes"},{"id":19750940,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2019-04","story":"Chimpanzee Browsing Instagram on iPhone with Precision","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"that is too simple to explain what is happening over the world","text":"It's always interesting to see the opening scene, but of course that is too simple to explain what is happening over the world: [VIDEO]"}]},"cY_o4A1wzsg":{"wy":"HN references it in discussions of how misconceptions are studied and how interview editing can make people look foolish.","m":[{"id":38747161,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2023-12","story":"Let me repeat that back to you","stance":"reference","why":"Counterpoint on how interviews make people look stupid.","quote":"Of course, there is this counterpoint...","text":"So many great videos on Veritasium, and that is one of my favorites. Anyone who is not familiar with the channel should check it out. Of course, there is this counterpoint... [VIDEO]"},{"id":6523538,"author":"lake99","month":"2013-10","story":"Misconceptions about evolution","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium on how misconceptions are studied.","quote":"I think this guy [1], with his PhD thesis on the topic, would beg to differ.","text":"> Spread facts, not \"myths\" I think this guy [1], with his PhD thesis on the topic, would beg to differ. Also, I don't think the website's sole purpose is to destroy them (misconceptions/people?). Some of us lay people who are knowledgeable, but have learnt the wrong factoid, for one reason or another, could still benefit from it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6814534,"author":"Strilanc","month":"2013-11","story":"Myth: The astronauts didn’t float away because they had heavy boots","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"To the point where you might think he was purposefully making fun of them","text":"Veritasium [1] often interviews people as part of his videos, and the results are consistent with what's being described in the post: somewhat distressing [2]. To the point where you might think he was purposefully making fun of them [3]. 1: [LINK] 2: [LINK] 3: [VIDEO]"}]},"g1Sq1Nr58hM":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke in threads about capitalism, SpaceX and Red Alert.","m":[{"id":46871754,"author":"SketchySeaBeast","month":"2026-02","story":"XAI Joins SpaceX","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Tim Curry just barely keeping it together during that one cut scene in Red Alert 3.","text":"Every time I hear stuff like this I think of Tim Curry just barely keeping it together during that one cut scene in Red Alert 3. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45993504,"author":"Xelbair","month":"2025-11","story":"Red Alert 2 in web browser","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"I'm escaping to the one place that hasn't been corrupted by capitalism, barely holds laughter back and takes a break SPACE!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33087709,"author":"thwarted","month":"2022-10","story":"Climate Change Is the Product of How Capitalism “Values” Nature","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Considering how this thread started, you're saying that we need to escape to the one place that hasn't been corrupted by capitalism? [VIDEO] But more pragmatically, neither Biosphere project was considered successful enough to make this workable on earth at any large, or even small, scale. There is still much more work and research to do here. Any attempt here currently has a hard dependency on the larger ecosystem to supply it with inputs. Musk working on building moonbases on earth looks a lot like what doomday preppers are already focused on."}]},"gLcWfecmZhE":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible presentation of Lane's ideas on life's origins and the rarity of complex life.","q":{"x":"There is also his \"Why is Life the Way it Is?\" talk he held last year at the Royal Institution.","i":11726501,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":32395198,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2022-08","story":"A biochemist’s view of life’s origin reframes cancer and aging","stance":"reference","why":"Nick Lane presentation at the Royal Institution","quote":"You can watch his presentations at the Royal Institute.","text":"You can watch his presentations at the Royal Institute. How the Krebs cycle powers life and death - [LINK] Why is Life the Way it Is? [VIDEO]"},{"id":23575803,"author":"cmrdporcupine","month":"2020-06","story":"Are planets with oceans common in the galaxy? It's likely, NASA scientists find","stance":"reference","why":"Lane's theory on life origins and rarity of complex life","quote":"Biochemist Nick Lane espouses the 'alkalkine thermal vent' theory of abiogenesis","text":"Biochemist Nick Lane espouses the 'alkalkine thermal vent' theory of abiogenesis and believes that single celled prokaryotic type life is almost inevitable on any planet that has or has had a mildly acidic ocean and active plate tectonics with aklaline thermal vents. It's a pretty convincing theory. _Complex_ cellular life (eukaryotic) is another story entirely. There's no reason to believe they progress inevitably from bacteria. Bacteria have been happily doing their thing without much change for the last 3.5 billion (or more) years. The development of eukaryotes seems like mostly a fluke, and they are very young in evolutionary history. And weird. Very weird. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11726501,"author":"okket","month":"2016-05","story":"This Biology Book Blew Me Away","stance":"reference","why":"Nick Lane's Royal Institution talk on why life is as it is","quote":"There is also his \"Why is Life the Way it Is?\" talk he held last year at the Royal Institution.","text":"There is also his \"Why is Life the Way it Is?\" talk he held last year at the Royal Institution. [VIDEO]"}]},"iK6SS8CXYZo":{"wy":"HN mentions it as evidence of how disliked Gates was in the 1990s, usually in passing or as a joke.","m":[{"id":43933278,"author":"maest","month":"2025-05","story":"My new deadline: 20 years to give away virtually all my wealth","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Gates was so hated during the early growth days of Microsoft, that he literally got pied in the face.","text":"Gates was so hated during the early growth days of Microsoft, that he literally got pied in the face. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21235474,"author":"Razengan","month":"2019-10","story":"A Relationship with Jeffrey Epstein That Bill Gates Now ‘Regrets’","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He used to be reviled enough during the 1990s to receive literal pies in the face.","text":"He used to be reviled enough during the 1990s to receive literal pies in the face. [0] He has done some good of course, but some people tend to see anyone \"giving away money\" == faultless saint. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38004932,"author":"corobo","month":"2023-10","story":"Bill Gates Memorized Employees' License Plates to Track Them","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Maybe we should bring back pieing, haha","text":"About a year after being pied in the face he launched the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation. Maybe we should bring back pieing, haha [VIDEO]"}]},"iWeu2dxHRDg":{"wy":"Commenters link the glitter bomb series whenever package theft comes up.","q":{"x":"Mark Rober has a series where he builds fake packages with cameras, stink bombs, etc.","i":35458495,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35458495,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2023-04","story":"Bob Lee, Former CTO Square, Creator of CashApp, Stabbed to Death in SF","stance":"reference","why":"Fake packages with cameras and stink bombs to get vengeance on thieves.","quote":"Mark Rober has a series where he builds fake packages with cameras, stink bombs, etc.","text":"Mark Rober has a series where he builds fake packages with cameras, stink bombs, etc. Goal is to get vengeance against car thieves in SF since police do nothing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34815706,"author":"hbarka","month":"2023-02","story":"99 phones in handcart make Google Maps traffic jam, turn green street red (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates predatory patterns of car thieves.","quote":"","text":"Geolocation data on practically everyone, with unique identifiers. If Google had a crime analysis unit, they could easily \"data science\" the identity of these organized criminals breaking into cars daily in the Bay Area. The cumulative damage to society that these few individuals cause is great. Predatory patterns: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34082380,"author":"ubj","month":"2022-12","story":"Porch pirates stole 260M packages this year","stance":"reference","why":"Latest in Rober's glitter bomb series against package thieves.","quote":"Obligatory links to YouTuber Mark Rober's quest for vengeance against porch pirates","text":"Obligatory links to YouTuber Mark Rober's quest for vengeance against porch pirates: * Glitter Bomb 1.0 vs Porch Pirates: [LINK] * Glitter Bomb 2.0 vs Porch Pirates: [LINK] * Glitter Bomb 3.0 vs Porch Pirates: [LINK] * Exploding Glitter Bomb 4.0 vs Package Thieves: [LINK] * Car Thieves vs the Final Glitter Bomb 5.0: [VIDEO]"}]},"jWTGsUyv8IE":{"wy":"Cited as visual evidence of Soviet command-economy scarcity, usually in arguments about choice and central planning.","q":{"x":"Video of a grocery store in Moscow, USSR, 1989","i":23993902,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23993902,"author":"TimTheTinker","month":"2020-07","story":"Universal Basic Income is Capitalism 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Moscow grocery store in 1989 as an example of command economy","quote":"Video of a grocery store in Moscow, USSR, 1989","text":"> democratically planned economy Sounds like an absolute nightmare on anything but the most local scale. Think about the number of economic decisions made daily by individuals in a free-market economy. That’s many orders of magnitude more than the number of decisions that could be made by a democratically elected group of representatives at the federal level, even if assisted by computer systems. And that’s not even considering secondary effects. Command economies have been tried many times and have never worked well. Beyond a certain limit, the larger the scale the more colossal the failure. The USSR was only able to go as long as it did because Stalin was shipping trainloads of grain out of the Ukraine leaving millions of people starving. EDIT: Video of a grocery store in Moscow, USSR, 1989: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19972074,"author":"akurilin","month":"2019-05","story":"When Boris Yeltsin Went Grocery Shopping in Clear Lake","stance":"reference","why":"Moscow grocery store footage linked from the article","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] - linked from the article. I still remember walking into those stores with my mother during that era, to my eyes at the time that looked \"just fine\", I didn't know any better. Looking back now, not so much. And as someone on YouTube pointed out, that was in Moscow, which had it much better than regions further away from the sources of power."},{"id":10431934,"author":"adventured","month":"2015-10","story":"Why too much choice is stressing us out","stance":"reference","why":"Soviet grocery store footage used to argue for freedom of choice","quote":"A nicer Soviet-era grocery store","text":"No doubt Barry Schwartz has never lived without choice. He's wrong, and obviously so. Freedom of choice is vastly superior, in every possible way, to the alternative. A nicer Soviet-era grocery store: [VIDEO] The stories are overly plentiful of people that were held in low-choice captivity in the former USSR, breaking down in tears upon seeing western grocery stores. I know several people who lived under Communism in Romania, their experience was identical. I'd suspect that very few people arguing against choice, have ever lived in a situation where they were actually deprived of it. They're essentially spoiled brats."},{"id":26250480,"author":"cbozeman","month":"2021-02","story":"The grisly trials that gave poison to prisoners","stance":"critical","why":"Shows barren Soviet grocery store to mock communism","quote":"There were no supermarkets in Soviet Russia.","text":"> a communistic state Yeah that worked out well for the Союз Советских Социалистических Республик! It managed to give the world the worst nuclear disaster ever seen! And also managed to keep an entire nation with vast resources so fucking poor that their supermarkets - lol, who am I kidding? There were no supermarkets in Soviet Russia. Hell, there were barely even markets... but they basically looked like this: [VIDEO] Keep your goddamned \"Ridiculous idealism that will never work because it defies human nature\" to yourself. You will never, and I mean ever have a successful communist state. There's never been a single one, and never will be."}]},"kJ1_P5oqf6Y":{"wy":"HN cites it, along with the rest of the series, as evidence that Amazon's Choice badges do not mean good quality.","m":[{"id":20988924,"author":"partialrecall","month":"2019-09","story":"Amazon Changed Search Algorithm in Ways That Boost Its Own Products","stance":"reference","why":"High-security padlock opened trivially because it was unshielded.","quote":"","text":"I get the \"thieves don't pick locks\" thing, but I really think it's sleazy as hell if a lock marketed as \"high security\" can be raked open in one second. That's a low-skill attack. In this case he didn't even have to rake it, because the locks were unshielded: [VIDEO] An unshielded padlock may as well be a warded lock like you'd find on a child's diary."},{"id":20500408,"author":"mey","month":"2019-07","story":"Amazon Warns Customers: Those Supplements Might Be Fake","stance":"reference","why":"Series on poor quality of Amazon's Choice locks.","quote":"has a series of videos on the poor quality of \"Amazon Choice\" for various locks","text":"LockPickingLawyer (Youtube) has a series of videos on the poor quality of \"Amazon Choice\" for various locks. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] (Branded Amazon Basics) [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":20098407,"author":"larkinrichards","month":"2019-06","story":"What does it mean when a product is “Amazon’s Choice”?","stance":"reference","why":"Amazon's Choice high-security lock with a terrible flaw.","quote":"","text":"I think an interesting point to keep an eye on is recommendations with negative side effects, e.g. amazons choice for a high security lock has a terrible flaw. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20099796,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2019-06","story":"What does it mean when a product is “Amazon’s Choice”?","stance":"reference","why":"Series showing Amazon's Choice locks are low quality.","quote":"","text":"I see two problems. First, the Amazon rating system is untrustworthy. There is simply far too much counterfeit reviewing and far too little done about it. This is a systemic problem that just needs to be fixed for the platform as a whole. The more nuanced problem, I think, is the fact that it can create a feedback loop. Something can be popular simply because it's cheap, but \"Amazon's Choice\" implies quality, which makes people think they're getting a deal because it's also cheap. What sprung to mind for me was LockpickingLawyer's[1] series on Amazon's Choice for things like \"padlock\", lock, and safe, all of which were of fairly low quality, or at least lower quality than \"Amazon's Choice\" would suggest. The problem is that, most of the time, people aren't going to know that what they have is low quality because it will almost never get tested and most people don't know anything about lock security. So they trust Amazon to tell them, when really it's the blind leading the blind [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}]},"lEplzHraw3c":{"wy":"Cited as context for Cuba's Street Net and as an example of grassroots networks built under constraints.","q":{"x":"More context on the Cuban Street Net","i":20668399,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40686181,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2024-06","story":"The ‘Network of Web Trees’ in Gaza","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Ah this reminds of Cuba","text":"Ah this reminds of Cuba: [VIDEO] I bet everywhere with restricted internet something of this sort exists. It also reminds the tech startup scene in Gaza, when I was browsing Google Maps to see what kind of economy they have there I recall stumbling upon startup schools. It's fascinating to browse the maps and see familiar things on places you wouldn't expect , I wish the best both to Gazans and the Israeli. Anyway, just for a bit of humanisation of the people there, here is a spontaneous street performans by a Gazan dancer in Europe: [LINK] It's one of the most beautiful things I've seen. Notice how she transforms from fathers little girl into this elegant creature the moment she makes her first move? I think people completely transform into another persona when the assume a role and in this video you can see the transformation. IMHO a similar transformation happens when people have a connection to an environment outside of theirs, therefore this stuff is important for things beyond the practical needs."},{"id":24323944,"author":"akritrime","month":"2020-08","story":"Level 3 Global Outage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I guess I got too fascinated by this video[0]","text":"Hmm, I actually didn't think about that at all. I guess I got too fascinated by this video[0] and wanted to apply something similar to our current scenario. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20668399,"author":"tank6b","month":"2019-08","story":"Cuban Street NET members protest to his network alive","stance":"reference","why":"Context on Cuba's Street Net","quote":"More context on the Cuban Street Net","text":"The Cuban goverment put a new law that renders all the network illegal. More context on the Cuban Street Net [VIDEO]"}]},"rYPMbLO4pAY":{"wy":"Used as a critical example of hostile, spiteful behavior and of the culture around coal rolling.","m":[{"id":27256293,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-05","story":"Cryptoqueen: A woman scammed the world, then vanished (2019)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I roll coal because I want people I don't like to breath coal smoke and I want to contribute to global warming and I have the coal and money to burn. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48155182,"author":"mmh0000","month":"2026-05","story":"U.S. DOJ demands Apple and Google unmask over 100k users of car-tinkering app","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The \"people\" doing this are exactly the type of people you'd expect...","text":"The \"people\" doing this are exactly the type of people you'd expect... Just the first thing that came up in a youtube search, there are thousands more: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34198587,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-12","story":"Remote Work Is Poised to Devastate America’s Cities","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your misconceptions about reality and kids are sad and badly misinformed. Bakfiets are quite common and extremely popular in the Netherlands, and people carry their kids around in them all over the place all the time. They even have transparent hoods and windshields for the rain. And the kids and parents absolutely love the delightful experience of safely biking all around the beautiful parks and ubiquitous well maintained bike paths of the city and countryside together. Biking in Amsterdam on a bakfiets with kids [LINK] And now they even have bakfiets eBikes with heavy duty powerful motors and stepless continuous variable planetary hubs with automatic transmissions (Enviolo Automatiq), which make them easy and effortless to ride. So you can stop at lights without shifting down, and they automatically shift down without pedaling and back up as you accelerate, so you can pay full attention to the road, other riders, and your kids, instead of worrying and hassling with shifting gears. Review: Lovens cargo bike (with English subtitles!) [LINK] @3:23> After a week this cargo bike really feels like a second car. Maybe even my first car. Before every journey I thought: Can I take the cargo bike, or do I need the car? Often the cargo bike sufficed. My wife needed to take the kids to the other side of town. She was late going to the playground, took the cargo bike, and arrived well ahead of time. We had a day out with it, disposed of waste paper and went shopping. There's no problem "},{"id":23246634,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-05","story":"Tesla adds bidirectional charging capability","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's an actual \"thing\" done by the same kind of people who think Hummers make them look cool.","text":"The cafe scene really happened. The Dutch tend to be straightforward and just say what they think. Have you ever heard the term \"Rolling Coal\"? It's an actual \"thing\" done by the same kind of people who think Hummers make them look cool. Rolling Coal On Protesters Compilation (BlackLivesMatter, Trump Haters, Tree Huggers): [VIDEO] [LINK] >Rolling coal is the illegal practice of modifying a diesel engine to increase the amount of fuel entering the engine in order to emit large amounts of black or grey sooty exhaust fumes into the air. Rolling coal is sometimes used as a form of anti-environmentalism. Such modifications may include the intentional removal of the particulate filter. Practitioners often additionally modify their vehicles by installing smoke switches and smoke stacks. Modifications to a vehicle to enable rolling coal may cost from US$200 to US$5,000."},{"id":19809827,"author":"Frondo","month":"2019-05","story":"Environmentalist didn't speak for 17 years to learn how to listen to opponents","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is what you get instead: The comments on that video are all mean-spirited, too","text":"It is hard to characterize anti-environmental behaviors as anything other than spiteful; there's never a conversation about how to appropriately account for externalities, never talk about trade-offs. This is what you get instead: [VIDEO] The comments on that video are all mean-spirited, too, and that's just one of many..."}]},"z6ep308goxQ":{"wy":"Cited as a companion to articles on Japanese web design and as an example of clustering websites by appearance.","q":{"x":"It's not an explanation, but show a possible way to cluster (thus classify) websites based on the how they appear","i":35119290,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":47123170,"author":"klez","month":"2026-02","story":"The peculiar case of Japanese web design","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I believe this is a continuation of her video on the same topic.","text":"I believe this is a continuation of her video on the same topic. [VIDEO] . It's been on my to-watch list for a while. I guess it's time to check it out."},{"id":35119290,"author":"byschii","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: How to Classify Websites?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a way to cluster websites by appearance.","quote":"It's not an explanation, but show a possible way to cluster (thus classify) websites based on the how they appear","text":"Maybe you can take inspiration from this [VIDEO] It's not an explanation, but show a possible way to cluster (thus classify) websites based on the how they appear, if you want your classification based on content... Maybe you need something different"},{"id":33745487,"author":"ericol","month":"2022-11","story":"Why is Japanese web design different to the rest of the world?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just found [1] this in \"newest\" [VIDEO] \"Why Japan’s Internet is weirdly designed [video]\" [1] I made a comment before regarding how HN works, and usually when one certain issue piques interest there's an influx of related posts."}]},"zHU1xH6Ogs4":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as a source or related clip in math and neural network discussions, not as a strong recommendation.","m":[{"id":43038539,"author":"bob1029","month":"2025-02","story":"Why is everyone trying to replace Software Engineers?","stance":"reference","why":"Functions as a framing idea.","quote":"Remember, everything is a function [0] and LLMs support calling them.","text":"I think the more realistic long term victims will be implementation specialists and business analysts. The middlemen who sit between product (developer) and customer. Giving your product the ability to talk & work directly with the customer seems to me a much more valuable activity than covering all the edge cases that would arise during the development of such a solution with an LLM. Remember, everything is a function [0] and LLMs support calling them. The trick is having the skill and domain expertise to establish bounded contexts and prompts that align with realistic product use cases and transition between them cleanly. I know there is a lot of doubt that users would enjoy using a chat interface, but I strongly believe it would be popular as long as it actually works. If it's not well designed and the GUI tools are faster even for a novice, you will have wasted a lot of time/money for nothing. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38315765,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2023-11","story":"Thomas Garrity – Functions Describe the World (2022) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Full series the short clip comes from.","quote":"The above short clip is from the following series","text":"The above short clip is from the following series: \"On Mathematical Maturity (1) Thomas Garrity\": [VIDEO] \"On Mathematical Maturity (2) Thomas Garrity\": [LINK]"},{"id":37205508,"author":"allangrant","month":"2023-08","story":"Watching Neural Networks Learn [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source in a video about neural networks.","quote":"On Mathematical Maturity, Thomas Garrity","text":"A video about neural networks, function approximation, machine learning, and mathematical building blocks. This is a submission for #SoME3 (0:00) Functions Describe the World (3:15) Neural Architecture (5:35) Higher Dimensions (11:55) Taylor Series (15:20) Fourier Series (21:25) The Real World (24:32) An Open Challenge Links and Content: • On Mathematical Maturity, Thomas Garrity: [VIDEO] • Earth Rotation Loop: [LINK] • Earth Rotation Loop Modeling Shell Surfaces: [LINK] • Fourier Features Paper: [LINK] • Code for mandelbrot/image approximations: [LINK] • Code for line/surface approximations: [LINK]"}]},"zYTxuW2vmzk":{"wy":"HN cites it as primary evidence of US conduct in Iraq and as the leak most associated with Julian Assange's prosecution.","q":{"x":"What does happen is that a van stops to help and they blast it and the kids inside.","i":24400941,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":33025918,"author":"DiogenesKynikos","month":"2022-09","story":"Europe braces for mobile network blackouts","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of helicopter pilots firing on a van in Baghdad","quote":"helicopter pilots crack jokes while shooting explosive rounds into the windshield of a van full of children","text":"I cannot see how one can claim that the US invasion of Iraq was less barbaric than the Russian invasion of the Ukraine. First of all, hundreds of thousands of Iraqis died. The US practiced torture, as we all found out when the pictures of Abu Ghraib came out. The US worked with Shiite militias that conducted ethnic cleansing and torture on a massive scale. The US put down uprisings in places like Sadr City, Najaf and Fallujah with full urban ground invasions. As a result of the invasion, Baghdad turned into a warzone for years, and the city's religiously mixed neighborhoods were ethnically cleansed (or however you refer to its religious equivalent). Iraq became a byword for failed state. Finally, I would tell anyone who talks about \"values\" to take a look at the \"Collateral Murder\" video, in which helicopter pilots crack jokes while shooting explosive rounds into the windshield of a van full of children.[0] Only two people have gone to jail for that: the soldier who leaked the video (Manning) and the guy who published it (Assange). 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31751845,"author":"ghostwriter","month":"2022-06","story":"US quietly urges more use of Russia fertilizer to ease food crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of US war crimes published by Assange","quote":"broadcasting US war crimes evidence in Iraq","text":"> In the US where free speech is protected, those who made various claims and conspiracy theories about the September 11 attacks (Bush did it! Israel did it! The CIA did it! etc.) didn’t get locked up or murdered Only, for some reason the free speech protection did not help Assange from being prosecuted and his life being destroyed for broadcasting US war crimes evidence in Iraq [1]. The same could be said about your prior mentioning of the Battle of Grozny. There were real terrorist groups in Grozny that had to be eliminated, and in a 2007 Baghdad there was nothing but a false pretence. [1] the infamous US taxpayer-funded \"keep shooting\" \"collateral damage\" done in Iraq: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28535693,"author":"discordance","month":"2021-09","story":"Ex-NSA cyberspies reveal how they helped hack foes of UAE (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Leaked grim footage of Iraq helicopter attack","quote":"Note, pretty grim material.","text":"Sounds like you're referring to the Wikileaks leaked 'Collateral murder' video [0]. Note, pretty grim material. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25042742,"author":"tehjoker","month":"2020-11","story":"Army wants smaller brigades, stronger divisions and lots of robots","stance":"reference","why":"Leaked footage of US war crimes in Iraq","quote":"(The Collateral Murder video, warning: war crimes)","text":"That's the argument for having a defensive force, not what we have. EDIT: Since y'all are downvoting: [VIDEO] (The Collateral Murder video, warning: war crimes)"},{"id":24400941,"author":"renewiltord","month":"2020-09","story":"Journalists cheered Assange's abuse. Now they've paved his path to a US gulag","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a US helicopter attack in Iraq","quote":"What does happen is that a van stops to help and they blast it and the kids inside.","text":"When referencing Youtube videos you have to use a timestamp, mang. [VIDEO] Also what you're saying doesn't happen. What does happen is that a van stops to help and they blast it and the kids inside. Which is obviously a horrible thing to do."}]},"zhr6fHmCJ6k":{"wy":"Used as a compact exhibit of Musk's repeatedly missed self-driving timelines, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":44568907,"author":"Qwertious","month":"2025-07","story":"LLM Inevitabilism","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the promises starting in 2016 are somewhere between 'undelivered' and 'blatant bullshit'.","text":"[VIDEO] (1min video, 'Elon Musk's broken promises') Musk's 2014/2015 promises are arguably delivered, here in 2025 (took a little more than '1 month' tho), but the promises starting in 2016 are somewhere between 'undelivered' and 'blatant bullshit'."},{"id":39137794,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-01","story":"No car company has taken up Tesla's offer to license FSD","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"8 years of Elon promising things within a year","text":"8 years of Elon promising things within a year: [VIDEO] The original Youtube link is dead (I wonder why) but as far as I can tell that one's a faithful copy."},{"id":37802362,"author":"belter","month":"2023-10","story":"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Maybe the CEO of Tesla would feel less empowered to make those claims","text":"Maybe the CEO of Tesla would feel less empowered to make those claims, if many of the darlings of AI, would not be silent, while sharing the stage with him during those wild statements. [VIDEO]"}]},"zrv78nG9R04":{"wy":"Cited as a documentary look at gutter oil and as an example of restaurant cost-cutting and food safety problems.","m":[{"id":23049415,"author":"cma","month":"2020-05","story":"Why Restaurants Are So Fucked","stance":"reference","why":"Shows gutter oil as a cost cutting measure","quote":"it shows a cost cutting measure (gutter oil)","text":"This video is funded by he American government so I'm not sure how biased it is, but it shows a cost cutting measure (gutter oil): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19515466,"author":"beardog","month":"2019-03","story":"In Beijing, it’s often cheaper to have food delivered than to get it yourself","stance":"reference","why":"Shows gutter oil recovery from unsafe sources","quote":"cooking oil is retrieved/derived from random unsafe sources such as sewers","text":"Such as using Gutter Oil (not limited to delivery), a practice where cooking oil is retrieved/derived from random unsafe sources such as sewers, a practice thought to be common in parts of China: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18435606,"author":"keketi","month":"2018-11","story":"In China, Desperate Patients Smuggle Drugs or Make Their Own","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary look at how gutter oil is produced","quote":"I didn't believe something as repulsive could exist.","text":"When I saw a video[1] about Chinese gutter oil, I didn't believe something as repulsive could exist. I was wrong. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46256126,"author":"joecool1029","month":"2025-12","story":"Multiple Indicted on Charges of Theft and Re-Sale of Restaurant Cooking Oil","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">It's not toxic per se, but you can't just dump it down the drain. That's what the gutter is for: [VIDEO]"}]}});
